window.LADDER_NOOK={"series":[{"id":"G","page":"dbtg","name":"general","footings":15,"steps":[{"id":"G1","title":"the frame: states are maps of categories, skills are functions","marks":[["≔","is defined as"],["∧","join","r01c.html#f8b6cf","1c · And, and the Ors"],["∀","speak of everything by speaking of anything","r02b.html#b4f71d","2b · Every and Some"],["𝔰","a state of mind: a map of categories 𝐖 → 𝐗"],["w","a situation"],["∃","show an example","r02b.html#b4f71d","2b · Every and Some"],["x","a response"],["h","a person, the other"],["ς","a skill"],["∘","g after f","r09b.html#333979","9b · Composing, and Brackets Don't Matter"],["¬","clash","r01b.html#c19f8b","1b · Not, and the Totally False"],["⇒","carry an if through","r01d.html#2f2e8d","1d · If, and Exactly When"],["◇","necessarily and possibly","r03b.html#cb1054","3b · Necessary and Possible"],["□","necessarily and possibly","r03b.html#cb1054","3b · Necessary and Possible"],["𝐖","the category of situations: things are situations w, arrows are happenings h : w → w′"],["𝐗","the category of responses: things are x = (t, b, e, u, a), arrows are changes of response"],["a","an act"],["id","the identity on A","r09b.html#250552","9b · Composing, and Brackets Don't Matter"],["t","a time; in 𝐗, a thought"],["b","a body state; in G13, the problem act"],["e","an emotion; e₀ is calm"],["u","urge: an emotion’s readied act"],["∈","the same-members axiom","r02d.html#9d86c6","2d · Membership and Same Members"],["T","thoughts"],["×","the products-exist technique","r02h.html#50591a","2h · Pairs in Order"],["B","body"],["E","emotions"],["U","urges"],["A","actions"],["↦","","r04b.html","4b · The Smallest Inductive Set"],["W","the world: situations"],["⊆","the self-part technique","r02d.html#b78709","2d · Membership and Same Members"],["⋯","","r01b.html","1b · Not, and the Totally False"],["⋃","","r02f.html","2f · Gather and Merge"],["⇔","making and using an exactly-when","r01d.html#982a95","1d · If, and Exactly When"]],"practice":[],"u":"A state of mind reads every situation as a response. What, then, does a skill act on? Why does a skill work the same whichever way the situation was reached?","tree":{"t":"Zoe gets a C on a chemistry quiz and reads it as \"I will never get into medicine\". What does she do?","o":[["She works out which questions she lost marks on and books a study slot for each topic.",["G1","G13","G15"],4,"A skill acts on the reading, whichever way the C came about. Solving turns \"never\" into a list of topics, and each one can be fixed."],["She turns the reading into \"one quiz is one quiz\" and lets the panic settle.",["G1","M1","DT19"],6,"The C is a cause of the panic, and the reading is the panic's other cause. Changing the reading changes the response without changing the grade."],["She lets the disappointment sit, and goes to the next lecture anyway.",["DT13","ER22"],1,"She is doing the best she can today. Tolerating the feeling keeps the next lecture, which a crisis act would cost her."],["She keeps telling herself medicine is over, and stops revising chemistry.",["G4","G6"],6,"This is a choice too, and it has causes. It costs her the next quiz, and the reading she started with grows stronger."]],"e":[["The study slots are booked, but the second quiz comes back a C too.",[["She runs a chain on the second quiz to find which link the study slots missed.",["G13","G15"],"One trial is not the whole record. The chain shows where the plan fell short."],["She checks the facts: two Cs are two quizzes, still a small share of the course.",["ER7","G1"],"The reading \"never\" is back, and checking the facts answers it again."],["She keeps the slots and adds one: a question session with the tutor.",["IE5","G2"],"One more condition can only raise her readiness. Persevering is adding, not quitting."]]],["The panic settles, but a friend says \"a C in chemistry, that's medicine over then\".",[["She notices the friend's words as a thought she can let pass, not a verdict.",["DT19","M1"],"Seeing a thought as a thought works on other people's thoughts too."],["She tells the friend, kindly and plainly, that one quiz is one quiz.",["IE5","IE10"],"Saying what she believes keeps her self-respect, and the reading she worked for."],["She lets the sting be there, and goes back to her reading.",["ER22","DT13"],"The panic can rise again and fall again. She does not have to win the argument."]]],["She goes to the next lecture, and it covers the topic she got wrong. She feels it all again.",[["She stays, and takes notes on exactly that topic.",["ER10","G15"],"Acting opposite to the urge to leave, all the way, turns the lecture into the study she needed."],["She rides the wave of disappointment through the hour.",["ER22","DT11"],"A wave rises and falls. One moment at a time, she gets to the end of the lecture."],["She half-smiles and keeps her hands open on the desk.",["DT18"],"The body tells the brain. It is a small move she can make in a full lecture hall."]]]]}},{"id":"G2","title":"degree as a cardinality: one measure for states and skills","marks":[["≤","reason by cases","r01c.html#01c421","1c · And, and the Ors"],["≥","","r04h.html","4h · Order"],["^","to the power of"],["𝔴","wise mind"],["Σ","","r04k.html","4k · Counting Choices"],["ω","a weight; in M3, wise mind’s combining map"],["ν","vulnerability"],["ι","invalidate (in IE11, intensity)"],["Z","what gets in the way"],["⊖","the opposite (of an urge)"],["🙂","happy; pleasant"],["Θ","attention: where it is at each time"]],"practice":[],"u":"If a degree is how many of a fixed set of conditions hold, why can one more condition never lower it? What do \"even a little\", \"all the way\" and \"one at a time\" each count?","tree":{"t":"Mike's readiness for a calculus final is low: of past papers, formulas, sleep and notes, only formulas hold. What does he do?","o":[["He adds one condition a day: a past paper tonight, notes tomorrow, sleep before the exam.",["G2","G15"],3,"Each condition added can only raise his degree. He needs to try harder, and one more condition is a measured step."],["He counts what holds, even a little, and stops calling himself completely unready.",["G2","M5"],7,"\"Even a little\" means at least one. Counting is not judging, and the count is true."],["He accepts going in at two of four, and does the best he can on the day.",["G2","DT13"],1,"Some limits are fixed by the time left. Accepting the count is not approving it, and it stops the panic costing him a third condition."],["He decides it is hopeless and does nothing more before the final.",["G4"],6,"His degree stays where it is. Doing nothing is still one of the four options, and its cost is his to weigh."]],"e":[["He does the past paper, and scores worse than he expected.",[["He marks which questions cost him, and makes them tomorrow's notes.",["G2","G13"],"The paper did its job: it found the weak topics. The next condition is aimed better."],["He records the score as one trial, and keeps the plan.",["G15"],"One trial is not induction. He needs several before he judges the plan."],["He notices the urge to give up, and runs STOP before deciding anything.",["DT4"],"Stopping comes first. Deciding while discouraged would cost him the next condition."]]],["He counts what holds, but his roommate says \"you're not ready at all\".",[["He describes what holds, plainly: \"formulas and one past paper\".",["M4","G2"],"A description holds where a judgment wobbles. The count is still true."],["He validates himself: worry before a final makes sense.",["IE24","IE22"],"Self-validation works when someone else invalidates."],["He lets the roommate have his opinion, and gets back to the notes.",["DT13","M5"],"Not judging the roommate keeps the evening for studying."]]],["He accepts two of four, but on the day the exam has a topic he never covered.",[["He does the questions he can first, then attempts the new one.",["DT11","ER12"],"One thing in the moment, then the hard one. Marks come from what he can do."],["He breathes out longer than in for a minute, then reads the question again.",["DT6"],"Paced breathing brings the panic down enough to think."],["He accepts this question will cost him, and does the rest well.",["DT13","DT16"],"Accepting one lost question is a turn of the mind, and it saves the others."]]]]}},{"id":"G3","title":"goals: each lack down, each skill up","marks":[["↓","","r11econ.html","11·econ · Why Would Oranges Be Left to Rot?"],["↑","","r05b.html","5b · Integers as Pairs"],["𝐌","the mindfulness series"],["∅","","r02e.html","2e · Sifting, and a Set to Start From"],["∨","add an option","r01c.html#01c421","1c · And, and the Ors"],["J","judgments"],["≠","clash","r01b.html#c19f8b","1b · Not, and the Totally False"],["☉","the self"],["⊢","","r38.html","38 · Arithmetic Describes Its Own Proofs"],["𝐈𝐄","the interpersonal effectiveness series"],["R","relationship"],["♡","wish well; like; love"],["𝐄𝐑","the regulating emotion series"],["𝐃𝐓","the distress tolerance series"],["F","facts"],["⛓","the addictive act: the substance or repeated act a person is hooked on (never medicine)"],["ℙ","probability"],["🧰","the toolbox: the skills held"]],"practice":[],"u":"The goal lowers four lacks and raises four skill sets. Pair each lack with the series that works on it.","tree":{"t":"Randy studies alone, knows no one in his lecture, and skips problem sets when his mood is low. What does he do?","o":[["He asks two classmates to work through problem set 3 with him on Thursday.",["G3","IE16","IE5"],4,"He did not cause being new and alone, but he is the one who can solve it. Asking lowers loneliness and the mood-led skipping together."],["He notices the low mood, names it, and does just the first problem.",["G3","ER1","M2"],5,"A new behaviour has to be learned where it is needed: here, at the desk on a low evening. One problem breaks the rule that mood decides the act."],["He lets the loneliness be there while he works, without making it the reason to stop.",["G3","DT13"],1,"He is doing the best he can. Holding the feeling and the act apart is tolerating it, not curing it."],["He skips the set again and stays away from the lecture.",["G4"],6,"Both lacks stay up. This choice has causes too, and next week starts from further back."]],"e":[["He asks two classmates, and one says no.",[["He meets the one who said yes, and asks a third person next week.",["G3","IE16","IE13"],"A no to one ask is not a no to all. Finding people takes more than one try."],["He walks the obstacles: was the ask clear, was the time good for them?",["IE15"],"Troubleshooting finds the first check that failed."],["He lets the sting of the no be, and still goes on Thursday.",["ER22","IE17"],"He cannot see why they said no. A guess is not a reason to stop."]]],["He does the first problem, but the low mood sinks lower.",[["He does one pleasant thing for twenty minutes, then the second problem.",["ER16","DT10"],"A little positive now lowers the mood's grip. Then the work goes on."],["He checks his body: has he eaten or slept?",["ER19","ER14"],"Low mood often has a body cause. PLEASE lowers vulnerability."],["He notes the mood and keeps a record of it next to the work.",["G15","ER6"],"Describing the mood puts it on paper, and the record shows whether the skill helps over time."]]],["He works with the loneliness, but halfway through he gets a message from home that makes it worse.",[["He answers the message later, and finishes the problem first.",["DT9","DT11"],"Putting something away for a while is a crisis skill used once, with a plan to come back."],["He calls someone he trusts and tells them.",["IE5","IE16"],"Reaching out is a skill too, and the loneliness is the problem it solves."],["He lets both feelings be there, and does one more line.",["ER22","DT13"],"Feelings come in waves, and he can carry them through one more line."]]]]}},{"id":"G4","title":"four options: move the thing, move the map, keep both, or nothing","marks":[["p","a problem"],["κ","change the situation"],["ϵ","change the emotional response"],["τ","tolerate"],["μ","stay miserable; in a myth list, a myth"],["σ","suffering"],["∞","","r08g.html","8g · Inequalities"],["π","pain"],["🤲","accept; radical acceptance"],["∉","","r02d.html","2d · Membership and Same Members"],["K","consequences, K = K⁺ + K⁻ (wanted and unwanted)"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why are there only four ways to meet a painful problem? Why does it follow that you are already carrying induction into action, even when you use no skill?","tree":{"t":"Sarah's group-project partner has done nothing, and the report is due Friday. What does she do?","o":[["She messages the partner and the tutor today, with a plan for who writes what by Wednesday.",["G4","IE5"],4,"She did not cause the gap, but she has to solve it. Changing the situation is the first of the four."],["She tells herself the mark is shared, and lets go of the resentment so she can work.",["G4","ER9"],7,"Finding the cause works better than blaming the partner. Changing her response is the second of the four."],["She writes her part well and accepts that the partner may not deliver.",["G4","DT13"],1,"Accepting both the problem and her response is the third option. It leaves her free to do her best part."],["She stews all week and hands in nothing extra.",["G4"],6,"Staying miserable is the fourth option, and it is a choice. The report and her week both get worse."]],"e":[["She messages them both. The tutor replies, but the partner still does nothing.",[["She writes the partner's part as a list of tasks and asks the tutor to divide the mark.",["IE5","IE14"],"Asking through the person with the authority is a valid move when the pair cannot settle it."],["She checks: is the plan clear, is it fair, is she asking the right person?",["IE15","IE11"],"Troubleshooting walks the obstacles in order and fixes the first that fails."],["She stops waiting, and writes enough for the report to pass.",["G4","ER12"],"Changing the situation is still open. She solves the part she can."]]],["She lets go of the resentment, but on Thursday the partner sends a page of nonsense.",[["She checks the facts: does the resentment fit now? It does, and it is the right size.",["ER7","ER8"],"A fitting emotion is information. Checking it keeps it from growing."],["She uses the anger for one clear message: \"I need your section rewritten by noon\".",["IE5","ER13"],"When anger fits, acting on it usefully is one of its solutions."],["She rides the wave of anger, then works.",["ER22"],"The anger passes faster when she does not feed it."]]],["She accepts it, but the tutor says the mark will be shared equally anyway.",[["She asks the tutor to look again, with the evidence of who wrote what.",["IE5","IE10"],"Self-respect is an aim too. Asking is valid even after accepting."],["She accepts this too, and turns the mind each time it comes back.",["DT16","DT13"],"Acceptance is a turn of the mind made again at every fork; one turn does not make the next."],["She notices the urge to complain to everyone, and does not act on it.",["DT4","DT25"],"Noticing an urge without obeying it keeps her reputation and her energy."]]]]}},{"id":"G5","title":"being present: four absences in a row is a drift","marks":[["⇏","does not give"],["✓","validate; valid"],["∴","","r05a.html","5a · Equations Revisited"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why is one who drifts out of their own life still in it? Why does a drift call for a fresh commitment, and not for leaving?","tree":{"t":"Terry has gone to no lectures and opened no coursework for four days running. What does he do?","o":[["He makes a fresh commitment: tomorrow's 9 a.m. lecture, and one hour on the backlog.",["G5"],2,"Four days in a row is a drift, and a drift calls for a fresh commitment. He wants to improve, and the commitment gives that a time and place."],["He reminds himself that drifting does not take him out of his own life, and lets the shame ease.",["G5","M5"],6,"The drift has causes. Seeing them without judging lowers the shame that keeps him away."],["He accepts that the backlog is real and goes to one lecture with it still undone.",["G5","DT13"],1,"Being present comes first and the backlog comes later. Tolerating it is how he gets back in."],["He stays in his room a fifth day.",["G4","G5"],6,"He is still in his life, drifting. The drift grows, and the fresh commitment he needs gets bigger."]],"e":[["He goes to the 9 a.m. lecture, but oversleeps the next day.",[["He makes a fresh commitment for tomorrow, without punishing himself.",["G5","DT16"],"A drift calls for a fresh commitment, not leaving. Every fork is a new choice."],["He sets two alarms and puts the phone across the room.",["G14","DT24"],"Missing links are fixed by a cue. Burning the bridge to snoozing helps."],["He runs a chain: what led to oversleeping?",["G13"],"The chain finds the link a skill could break."]]],["The shame eases, but his tutor emails to say he is at risk of failing.",[["He replies today, with a plan for the backlog.",["IE5","ER12"],"Answering early is the skill. The tutor can help with a plan."],["He checks the facts: at risk is not failed.",["ER7"],"The fear fits a threat, but the size of the threat is checked."],["He lets the fear come, and does one hour of the backlog.",["ER22","ER18"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["He goes to the lecture with the backlog undone, and is called on to answer a question about last week.",[["He says plainly, \"I missed last week, I'll catch up\".",["IE10","M4"],"Truthfulness keeps his self-respect, and the description is all that is needed."],["He lets the embarrassment rise and fall while the lecture goes on.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."],["He asks the lecturer afterwards what to read first.",["IE5","G5"],"Being present means turning up again, and asking."]]]]}},{"id":"G6","title":"the axioms: laid down, held in trust","marks":[["ℓ","a link in a chain"],["K_","K_☉ x: ☉ knows x"]],"practice":[],"u":"\"People are doing the best they can\" and \"they still need to do better\" are both laid down. Why do these two not contradict each other?","tree":{"t":"Claire has failed the same statistics midterm twice. What does she do?","o":[["She asks the tutor which topics cost her most, and plans her third attempt around them.",["G6","G13"],3,"She is doing the best she can, and she still needs to try harder. Solving puts both assumptions to work."],["She drops \"I'm hopeless at statistics\" and says \"I haven't learned this yet\".",["G6","M5"],7,"Finding causes works better than judging. The new sentence names a cause she can change."],["She accepts that she has to resit, and that this is hard.",["G6","DT13"],4,"She may not have caused the poor teaching, but she has to solve it. Accepting the resit stops the fight with what has happened."],["She decides she is stupid and stops going to the class.",["G4","G6"],6,"That is a choice with causes, not a fact about her. It closes the road the other three options keep open."]],"e":[["She plans her third attempt, but the tutor's list covers almost the whole course.",[["She breaks the list into weeks, one topic a day.",["ER18","DT11"],"Mastery is a step at a time, and only today's step counts."],["She asks the tutor which three to start with.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She reminds herself she needs to try harder, and that trying is not failing.",["G6","M5"],"Assumption 3 fits: she needs to try harder, and she also is doing her best."]]],["She says \"I haven't learned this yet\", but a classmate laughs at her for failing twice.",[["She validates herself, exactly as she would a friend.",["IE24"],"Recovering from invalidation starts with her own validation."],["She keeps the words, and notices the laugh as a sting that will pass.",["ER22","DT19"],"The sting is real, and it is not a fact about her."],["She tells the classmate it is not funny to her.",["IE10","IE5"],"FAST keeps self-respect: fair, no needless apologies, sticking to values, truthful."]]],["She accepts the resit, but finds it clashes with a family wedding.",[["She asks the department about another date, with the facts.",["IE5","ER12"],"Solving is open again: a clash is a new problem."],["She makes pros and cons for each choice.",["DT5"],"Pros and cons, written before the urge decides, weigh the long term above the now."],["She accepts that she will miss one, and chooses in wise mind.",["M3","DT13"],"Wise mind holds both, and acceptance holds the cost."]]]]}},{"id":"G7","title":"biosocial: biology is the base case, the transaction is the step","marks":[["f","the facts of a situation"],["i","interpret"],["min","max and min","r08b.html#0ef1ef","8b · Any Two Reals Compare"],["ε","the emotional reading of a situation"],["t½","how long until it halves"],["🔥","crisis; in IE, the extreme signal"],["Δ","the change-heads-to-0 technique","r13b.html#23f506","13b · A Slope Means No Gap"],["⇐","","r02i.html","2i · Sets by Hand"],["δ","","r10.html","10 · Pushing the Limit as Close as You Can Imagine"],["lim","f heads to L at w","r10a.html#c5f5eb","10a · The Challenge and the Answer"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why does the tulip in a rose garden show a poor fit with no one at fault? Which of the two sides, the born or the round-by-round, could you still change?","tree":{"t":"Jean was born sensitive, and her school mocked anyone who cried over a grade. A bad grade arrives. What does she do?","o":[["She finds one place where feelings about grades are allowed, a friend or a study group, and goes there with it.",["G7","IE16"],4,"She did not cause either side, but she can change the surrounding one. Changing the fit helps the tulip without blaming the garden."],["She tells herself her sensitivity is a starting point, not a fault, and the shame eases.",["G7","IE24"],7,"No one is at fault in a poor fit. Dropping blame changes the response."],["She lets the tears come in private, then goes to class.",["G7","ER22"],1,"She is doing the best she can with what she was born with. Riding the feeling is tolerating it."],["She hides the feeling and tells everyone she doesn't care.",["G7","G10"],6,"That is one end of a line she was taught. It works for a day, and the feeling stays in."]],"e":[["She goes to a study group, but someone there says \"it's only a grade\".",[["She says what she needs: \"I just want to say it, not be fixed\".",["IE5","IE23"],"Asking for the kind of validation she needs is a skill."],["She notices it as invalidation and validates herself.",["IE24","G11"],"She knows where this comes from. She does not have to agree."],["She lets it go and finds one person there who listens.",["IE16","DT13"],"Finding people is near, alike, talking. One person is enough."]]],["The shame eases, but at home a parent says she is too sensitive.",[["She describes what happened without arguing: \"I got a bad grade and I'm upset\".",["M4","IE10"],"A description cannot be argued with, and it keeps her self-respect."],["She remembers the biosocial view: sensitivity is a starting point, the setting is the other half.",["G7","G9"],"Seeing both halves keeps the shame from coming back."],["She lets the hurt be there, and does something soothing.",["DT10","ER22"],"Soothing each of the five senses is a way of being gentle with yourself, then going on."]]],["She lets the tears come, but they start again in class.",[["She steps out for two minutes and splashes cold water on her face.",["DT6"],"TIP changes the body fast, and then she can go back in."],["She focuses on one thing: writing down what the lecturer says.",["DT9","M5"],"One thing in the moment gives her something else to attend to."],["She lets them come quietly, and stays.",["ER22","DT13"],"Crying in class is not a disaster. She is doing the best she can."]]]]}},{"id":"G8","title":"the transaction drawn: one dysfunction, five instabilities, fed back","marks":[["⇄","each moves the other"],["Λ","instability: Λ_e emotion, Λ_a acts, Λ_h relationships, Λ_☉ self, Λ_T thinking"]],"practice":[],"u":"Pick one instability and trace how it feeds back into the pair it came from.","tree":{"t":"Evita keeps changing her major and dropping study groups. What does she do?","o":[["She picks one study group and stays for the whole term, whatever her mood.",["G8","G15"],3,"She needs to do better and be more motivated. Holding one group steady breaks one instability, and that eases the pair it feeds."],["She notices the dropping as part of a loop, not a flaw in her, and feels less ashamed.",["G8","G6"],6,"Every act is caused. Seeing the loop lowers the shame, and shame is one of the things that keeps it turning."],["She accepts that her choices have been unstable, and keeps her current major for now.",["G8","DT13"],1,"She has done the best she could. Accepting the history stops it driving the next change."],["She switches major again next week.",["G8"],6,"The loop turns once more, and each instability feeds the others."]],"e":[["She stays with one group, but by week five she wants to quit it.",[["She makes pros and cons of quitting, before the urge decides.",["DT5"],"Pros and cons, written before the urge decides, weigh the long term above the now."],["She notices it is the old loop, and commits to week six.",["G8","G5"],"Seeing the loop is what lets her stay one more week."],["She tells the group she is finding it hard, and asks for one change.",["IE5","IE7"],"Asking for one change keeps the group and changes what is wrong."]]],["She sees it as a loop, but her parent says she can't stick to anything.",[["She validates herself: the loop has causes, and she is working on it.",["IE24","G6"],"Every behaviour is caused, and she is doing her best."],["She weakens \"anything\" to \"some things\": she has kept this group for a month.",["IE20","IE21"],"Weakening the quantifier fits the facts."],["She lets the comment pass, and keeps going to the group.",["DT19","DT13"],"The comment is a thought someone else had."]]],["She keeps the major, but fails a core module in it.",[["She asks her adviser whether the module can be retaken, and plans it.",["IE5","ER12"],"A failed module is a problem to solve, not a verdict on the major."],["She checks the facts: one module, not the major.",["ER7","IE21"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."],["She accepts the fail, and keeps the major for now, as she decided.",["DT13","DT16"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]]]}},{"id":"G9","title":"the environment–person system: a dialogue between two systems","marks":[["⇢","can lead to (possible, not necessary)"],["bg","background"]],"practice":[],"u":"The person is three systems, each moving the other two. Why does that make thoughts of dying possible but not fixed?","tree":{"t":"Mike's scholarship is cut. What does he do?","o":[["He sees the financial aid office this week and applies for two bursaries.",["G9","ER12"],4,"He did not cause the cut, but he has to solve it. Acting on the environment changes what reaches his three systems."],["He notices the worry in his body and his thoughts, and slows his breathing before he decides anything.",["G9","DT6"],6,"The cut moves his thinking, acting and body systems, each through the others. Calming one calms the rest."],["He accepts that money will be tight this term and keeps studying.",["G9","DT13"],1,"He is doing the best he can. Tolerating the change keeps his studies going while the rest is uncertain."],["He stops eating properly and stops going to class.",["G9"],6,"Each system moves the others, so the cut spreads. This choice has causes, and it makes them stronger."]],"e":[["He sees the aid office, but both bursaries are closed for this year.",[["He asks what opens next term, and sets a reminder.",["IE5","G14"],"The ask finds the next door, and the cue makes sure he goes through it."],["He looks for part-time work on campus that fits his timetable.",["ER12"],"The problem loop goes back to the start with a new solution."],["He notices the worry in body and thought, and slows down before deciding.",["G9","DT6"],"The systems move one another. Calming the body first is a valid step."]]],["He calms his breathing, but that night he can't sleep for worrying about rent.",[["He writes a two-line plan for the rent, then lies down again.",["ER21","ER12"],"A solvable worry gets a plan, then the bed is for rest."],["He aims for rest, not sleep.",["ER21"],"Rest is worth something even without sleep."],["He uses paired relaxation, muscle by muscle.",["DT7"],"Relaxing the body quiets the worry a little."]]],["He accepts tight money, but his laptop breaks.",[["He asks the library about a loan laptop.",["IE5","ER12"],"A new problem goes back to the start of the loop."],["He accepts this too, and does the next essay in the library.",["DT13","DT12"],"Living a term that is not the one he wanted."],["He tells a friend how hard it is, and asks to borrow one for a week.",["IE16","IE5"],"Friendships grow from being near, being alike, talking, and joining in, so one more try widens the chance."]]]]}},{"id":"G10","title":"three dialectical dimensions: three lines a person swings along","marks":[["⟷","a line between two ends"]],"practice":[],"u":"On each of the three lines, why is the work a synthesis and not a jump to the other end?","tree":{"t":"Zoe panics in front of her teacher, then tells everyone the exam is no big deal. What does she do?","o":[["She tells her teacher she is anxious about the exam and asks for one practice paper.",["G10","IE5"],5,"This sits between feeling everything and disowning it. It has to be learned in the classroom, where she needs it."],["She owns the worry to herself: \"I care about this, and it scares me\".",["G10","ER6"],7,"A synthesis keeps both ends. Naming the feeling without blaming herself is how both stay true."],["She lets herself feel the fear before the exam without hiding it or acting it out.",["G10","ER22"],1,"She is doing the best she can. Tolerating the fear is the middle of the line, not a jump to either end."],["She keeps swinging: panic one day, \"don't care\" the next.",["G10"],6,"The swing has causes on both sides. It goes on, and neither end gets her ready."]],"e":[["She tells her teacher, who says \"you'll be fine\" and moves on.",[["She asks again, clearly: \"could I have one practice paper?\"",["IE5","IE13"],"The broken record: say the ask again, calmly."],["She validates her own worry, since the teacher didn't.",["IE24","G10"],"She can hold both: the teacher meant well, and her worry is real."],["She finds a practice paper online herself.",["ER12"],"Problem solving is a loop: when a solution fails or a new problem comes, go back to the start."]]],["She owns the worry, but it grows as the exam gets closer.",[["She copes ahead: she rehearses the exam morning in her mind.",["ER18"],"Rehearsing makes the real morning familiar."],["She checks the facts about the exam: what it covers, how much it counts.",["ER7"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."],["She rides each wave of worry without acting on it.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["She feels the fear openly, and a classmate tells her to calm down.",[["She says, \"I'm nervous, that's all, I'll be okay\".",["IE10","M4"],"Truthful and brief keeps her self-respect."],["She lets the comment pass and keeps feeling what she feels.",["G10","ER22"],"Owning the emotion is the synthesis between hiding it and acting it out."],["She does paced breathing before going in.",["DT6"],"TIP changes the body to change the emotion fast: cold water, hard exercise, paced breathing, paired relaxation."]]]]}},{"id":"G11","title":"genesis: worlds in which emotion is not validated","marks":[["g","how far a consequence meets a goal"],["⊨","","r29c.html","29c · A Plane Where Parallels Are Many"],["♮","name the emotion"],["⊣","blocks, or works against"],["dom","domain and range","r09a.html#1128ad","9a · A Function Is Its Pairs"]],"practice":[],"u":"Choose two of the worlds that do not validate emotion. What does each one teach a vulnerable person about their feelings?","tree":{"t":"Randy's family told him to sort out his grades on his own. At university, a module goes badly. What does he do?","o":[["He books a meeting with his personal tutor, which his family never did.",["G11","IE5"],5,"He is in a new context, and a new behaviour must be learned there. Asking for help is the opposite of the world he grew up in."],["He reminds himself that needing help is normal, not weak.",["G11","IE3"],7,"That world taught him a myth. Finding where it came from works better than blaming himself for it."],["He accepts that asking feels strange and asks anyway, feeling awkward.",["G11","DT13"],1,"He is doing the best he can. Tolerating the awkwardness is what lets the new behaviour happen."],["He tries to fix it alone and tells no one.",["G11"],6,"That is what he was taught, and it has a cause. The module stays unsolved for longer."]],"e":[["He books the tutor meeting, but the tutor cancels.",[["He rebooks the same day, and asks for a time this week.",["IE5","G11"],"Asking again is the skill his family never showed him."],["He checks what he can do before then: the module page, the office hours.",["ER12"],"Problem solving is a loop: when a solution fails or a new problem comes, go back to the start."],["He lets the disappointment be, without reading it as a sign.",["ER22","IE17"],"He cannot see the tutor's reasons. A cancelled meeting is a cancelled meeting."]]],["He tells himself needing help is normal, but a parent asks why he can't manage alone.",[["He validates himself: asking for help is how university works.",["IE24","G11"],"He knows where the parent's view comes from, and he need not take it on."],["He tells the parent what he is doing, without apologizing.",["IE10"],"FAST keeps self-respect: fair, no needless apologies, sticking to values, truthful."],["He weakens \"I should manage alone\" to \"sometimes I need help\".",["IE20"],"Weakening \"always\" to \"sometimes\" fits the facts and leaves room to act."]]],["He asks anyway, and the tutor seems impatient.",[["He keeps to his question, calmly, until it is answered.",["IE5","IE10"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["He gives the benefit of the doubt: the tutor may be busy.",["IE17"],"No one can observe another's thoughts, so a guess about them is held loosely and given the benefit of the doubt."],["He notices the urge to apologize and leave, and stays.",["ER10","DT4"],"Opposite action works only all the way, and repeated until the emotion changes."]]]]}},{"id":"G12","title":"two analyses: what was done that should not be, what was not done that should be","marks":[["G","goals"],["⋆","a⋆: the best choice"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why do something ineffective done and something effective not done call for two different analyses?","tree":{"t":"Sarah snapped at a teaching assistant, and also never asked for the extension she needed. What does she do?","o":[["She runs a chain on the snap and a missing-links check on the extension.",["G12","G13","G14"],7,"Something done that should not be and something not done that should be call for two analyses. Finding causes beats blaming herself."],["She stops calling the week a disaster and sees two separate problems.",["G12","M5"],6,"Each act has its own causes. Telling them apart makes each one smaller."],["She accepts that both happened, and lives with the missed extension and the awkwardness with the TA.",["G12","DT13"],1,"She was doing the best she could that week. Acceptance covers what cannot be undone, and leaves the analysis for when she is ready."],["She goes over both in her head all week and does nothing.",["G4"],6,"Rumination is a choice with causes. Neither problem gets analysed, so both can happen again."]],"e":[["She runs both analyses, but the chain shows she snaps when she is tired, which is often.",[["She plans a cut at the tired link: no hard conversations after 10 p.m.",["G13","ER19"],"Cutting the chain where tiredness enters."],["She works on sleep first.",["ER19","ER14"],"Lowering vulnerability lowers the snaps."],["She apologizes to the TA, and says what she will do differently.",["IE10","IE7"],"Repair keeps the relationship and her self-respect."]]],["She sees two problems, but the TA is now cool with her.",[["She gives the benefit of the doubt and greets the TA normally.",["IE17","IE7"],"GIVE keeps the tie even when it is strained."],["She apologizes once, briefly.",["IE10","G12"],"FAST keeps self-respect: fair, no needless apologies, sticking to values, truthful."],["She lets the awkwardness be, and keeps going to the seminar.",["ER22","DT13"],"Awkwardness passes when she keeps showing up."]]],["She accepts both, but the missed extension means a late penalty on her transcript.",[["She asks whether there is an appeal route for the penalty.",["IE5","ER12"],"Accepting the past does not stop solving the present."],["She runs a missing-links check, so the next extension is asked for in time.",["G14"],"The first question that failed gets a fix."],["She accepts the penalty, and turns the mind again when it stings.",["DT16"],"Acceptance is a turn of the mind made again at every fork; one turn does not make the next."]]]]}},{"id":"G13","title":"chain analysis: a path through 𝐖 and 𝐗, cut at its key links","marks":[["⟹","sets the stage for"],["S","a prompting event"],["k","what follows an act"],["″","the slope of the slope","r13d.html#de837e","13d · The Power Technique"],["∖","","r02d.html","2d · Membership and Same Members"],["≪","is much smaller than"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why describe the problem act so that an actor could play it? Why look for the links where a skill would have broken the chain?","tree":{"t":"Terry stayed up all night gaming before an exam. What does he do?","o":[["He writes the chain and plans a cut at the link \"one more game\", with his console off by 11 p.m. the night before.",["G13"],7,"Finding the link where a skill would have broken the chain works better than blaming himself. The plan targets that link."],["He drops \"I'm so lazy\" and describes the night so an actor could play it.",["G13","M4"],6,"Every act is caused. A description with no judgment shows the causes."],["He accepts the exam went badly, and lets the disappointment be without calling himself lazy.",["G13","DT13","M5"],1,"He was doing the best he could with the links he had. Acceptance keeps the night from sinking the rest of his week."],["He decides he will never change and games again tonight.",["G13","G4"],6,"The chain runs again. That is a choice with causes, and the same links are still there."]],"e":[["He plans the console cut, but the next night his friends invite him to play online.",[["He says no clearly, and offers a time after the exam.",["IE13","IE7"],"Saying no firmly and keeping the friendship."],["He burns the bridge: he unplugs the console and gives the controller to his roommate.",["DT24"],"One bridge left standing is enough for a relapse."],["He makes pros and cons before he answers.",["DT5"],"Pros and cons, written before the urge decides, weigh the long term above the now."]]],["He describes the night plainly, but writing it down makes him feel worse.",[["He keeps going, and finds the link where a skill could have broken the chain.",["G13","ER10"],"The bad feeling is part of the work, and stopping halfway leaves it."],["He takes a break and does something soothing, then finishes.",["DT10"],"Soothing each of the five senses is a way of being gentle with yourself, then going on."],["He drops the judgments that crept back in.",["M5"],"Not judging keeps the description useful."]]],["He accepts the bad exam, but the results show he failed the module.",[["He asks about the resit and plans it.",["ER12","IE5"],"Problem solving is a loop: when a solution fails or a new problem comes, go back to the start."],["He checks the facts: a failed module, a resit, not a failed degree.",["ER7"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."],["He accepts this too, without calling himself lazy.",["DT13","M5"],"Acceptance all the way, with no judgment added."]]]]}},{"id":"G14","title":"missing links: the first question that fails","marks":[["🤲̂","willingness"],["j⋆","the first check that fails"],["∪","","r02d.html","2d · Membership and Same Members"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why stop at the first no among the four questions, and not answer all four?","tree":{"t":"Claire knew she should email her professor about a missed lab, and was willing to, but it never came to mind. What does she do?","o":[["She stops at the third question and sets a phone reminder for 9 a.m. tomorrow.",["G14"],5,"The first no is where to stop. The behaviour has to be learned in her own day, and a reminder places it there."],["She stops calling herself careless, because forgetting is a cause and not a character flaw.",["G14","G6"],7,"Finding the cause works better than judging. Knowing that the thought never came up is a cause she can fix."],["She accepts that the lab mark may be lost, and emails anyway.",["G14","DT13"],1,"She is doing the best she can now. Accepting the loss and still acting is tolerating it."],["She decides it is too late and never sends the email.",["G14","G4"],6,"That choice has causes. The missing link stays missing for next time."]],"e":[["She sets the reminder, but it goes off during a lecture and she dismisses it.",[["She moves the reminder to straight after the lecture.",["G14"],"Fix the first check that failed: the cue came at the wrong time."],["She writes the email in the lecture break.",["DT11"],"IMPROVE the moment: one thing at a time, meaning, a short break. Only this moment has to be survived."],["She asks a friend to remind her at lunch.",["IE5","IE16"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."]]],["She stops calling herself careless, but the professor's reply says \"you should have been more careful\".",[["She validates herself: forgetting has a cause, and she has fixed it.",["IE24","G6"],"Validating yourself, exactly as you would a friend, is how to recover when someone invalidates you."],["She replies briefly, thanks the professor, and states her fix.",["IE10","IE7"],"Keeping the relationship and her self-respect."],["She lets the sting pass.",["DT19","ER22"],"A comment is a thought, not a fact about her."]]],["She emails anyway, but the lab mark is lost.",[["She asks whether another lab can count instead.",["IE5","ER12"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She accepts the lost mark, and keeps the reminder system.",["DT13","G15"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."],["She notices the urge to give up on the module, and doesn't.",["DT4","DT5"],"STOP: stop, step back, observe, then proceed mindfully. Stopping gives a skill time to work."]]]]}},{"id":"G15","title":"practice: a trial, a measure, a record, and induction over trials","marks":[["⊇","","r02g.html","2g · All the Subsets"],["∩","","r02d.html","2d · Membership and Same Members"],["𝐆","the general series"],["☼","each day"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why rate the state before and after each trial? What lets you conclude, from many trials, where a skill works for you?","tree":{"t":"Jean tries a timed study block on three evenings before her history exam. What does she do with what she found?","o":[["She records each trial, with her focus rated before and after, and keeps the block if two out of three helped.",["G15"],3,"From many trials she can conclude where the skill works for her. Trying harder means measuring, not guessing."],["She notices that her focus went up even a little, and gives herself credit for it.",["G15","G2"],2,"She wants to improve. Even a little is at least one condition, and it counts."],["She accepts that one evening did not work, and keeps going.",["G15","DT13"],1,"One failed trial is one trial. Tolerating it keeps the induction going."],["She stops after the first bad evening and decides the method doesn't work.",["G15","G4"],6,"One trial cannot carry a conclusion. Stopping is a choice, and it leaves her with no evidence."]],"e":[["She keeps the block, but the fourth evening it doesn't help at all.",[["She adds it to the record and keeps going: three of four still helped.",["G15","G2"],"Induction is over many trials. One bad evening is one trial."],["She checks what was different that evening: sleep, food, mood.",["ER19","ER14"],"The cause may be vulnerability, not the block."],["She troubleshoots the block: was it the same length, the same place?",["ER24"],"When a skill didn't work, check in order and fix the first thing that failed."]]],["She gives herself credit, but her exam mark is still disappointing.",[["She keeps the record going into the next term, and adds a past paper each week.",["G15","ER18"],"One try is one trial. Only a record of several shows where a skill works, so the trial goes on."],["She separates the block from the exam: the block raised focus, the exam had other causes.",["G13","G6"],"A chain finds the link where a skill would have broken it, and that is where the next try aims."],["She lets the disappointment be.",["ER22","DT13"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["She keeps going, but a second evening doesn't work either.",[["She changes one thing (the time of day) and tries again.",["G15","ER24"],"A new trial with one change tests the skill fairly."],["She accepts two bad evenings, and keeps the plan for one more week.",["DT16","DT13"],"Acceptance is a turn of the mind made again at every fork; one turn does not make the next."],["She asks a classmate what works for them.",["IE5","IE16"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."]]]]}}]},{"id":"M","page":"dbtm","name":"mindfulness","footings":10,"steps":[{"id":"M1","title":"goals: less suffering, the mind held, reality as it is","marks":[["𝕋","times; t₀ is now"],["~","is joined to, by some chain of touches"],["J_eval","a judgment that rates good or bad"],["💭","thoughts"]],"practice":[],"u":"To hold the mind is to hold attention. What two things about attention do you hold? Why does seeing a thought as a thought give you time before you react?","tree":{"t":"During a physics exam, Evita thinks \"I'm going to fail\". What does she do?","o":[["She starts with the question she knows best, so marks go on the board while the thought plays out.",["M1","DT19"],5,"Holding attention is a skill that has to be learned in the exam hall. Starting where she is strongest turns the time she has bought into marks."],["She notes \"that's a thought, not a fact\" and lets the fear drop a notch.",["M1"],6,"The thought is caused, and it is not a fact. Seeing it as a thought changes the response."],["She lets the thought stay in the background while she works.",["M1","DT19"],1,"She need not chase the thought away. Working with it there is tolerating it."],["She believes the thought and stops writing.",["M1","G4"],6,"The thought decides the act. That is a choice too, and the paper pays for it."]],"e":[["She starts with the question she knows best, but halfway through she realizes she has misread it.",[["She notes \"that's a thought\" about the rising panic, and rereads the question slowly.",["M1","DT19"],"Holding attention on the page, not on the thought, gets the time back."],["She fixes what she can in the time left, then moves on.",["DT11","ER12"],"One thing in the moment, and the loop returns to the start with the right question."],["She runs STOP: pen down, one breath, then decides.",["DT4"],"Stopping first keeps one mistake from becoming three."]]],["The fear drops, but at the next hard question the thought comes back louder.",[["She notices it again, and lets it pass again.",["M1","DT16"],"Each return is a new fork. Seeing a thought as a thought is done again each time."],["She does paced breathing for three breaths.",["DT6"],"The body brings the alarm down when the thought won't go."],["She skips the hard question for now and comes back to it.",["DT11","M5"],"Doing what works, one question at a time."]]],["She works with the thought in the background, but the student next to her finishes early and leaves.",[["She brings her attention back to her own paper.",["M2","M5"],"On purpose, now, without judging: the other student is not her exam."],["She checks the facts: finishing early says nothing about marks.",["ER7"],"A fact checked takes the sting out of the comparison."],["She lets the jolt rise and fall, and keeps writing.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]]]}},{"id":"M2","title":"what mindfulness is: on purpose, now, no judging, no clinging","marks":[["⟨","","r19d.html","19d · A Part’s Size Divides the Whole’s"],["⟩","","r19d.html","19d · A Part’s Size Divides the Whole’s"]],"practice":["meditation ≔ 𝐌  for a set  Δt ,   sitting ,  standing  or  lying ;   focus  or  open","movement :   yoga ,  martial arts ,  dance ,  walking ,  hiking   Θ on the moving body","every form :   drift  ⇒  return ;   then carry it into the day"],"u":"Mindfulness at a moment needs all four conditions at once. Which one fails when you are fully attending to a memory you cling to?","tree":{"t":"Randy rereads the same paragraph of his set text while replaying yesterday's bad seminar. What does he do?","o":[["He notices the drift, writes \"seminar\" on a note to deal with later, and rereads with his finger on the line.",["M2","M5"],5,"Attention on purpose, in the present, has to be learned at the desk. The note puts the past where it can wait."],["He stops judging himself for drifting and simply comes back.",["M2","M5"],7,"Judging the drift is one more drift. Coming back without blame is the skill."],["He lets the seminar memory come and go while he keeps reading.",["M2"],1,"Not clinging means letting it pass, not forcing it out. He does the best he can."],["He keeps replaying the seminar for the rest of the evening.",["M2","G4"],6,"Clinging to the past fails one of the four conditions. The reading does not get done."]],"e":[["He writes the note and rereads, but the seminar memory keeps pushing back in.",[["He sets a ten-minute slot after reading to think about the seminar on purpose.",["M2","ER21"],"Giving the memory a time makes it easier to leave it now."],["He reads one sentence at a time, finger on the line.",["M5"],"Not judging, one thing at a time, doing what works: the how skills keep the next step clear."],["He notices clinging to the memory, and lets go of it again.",["M2","M6"],"No clinging: the memory can come and go."]]],["He comes back without judging, but loses a whole page again.",[["He comes back again, as many times as it takes.",["M2","DT16"],"Attention comes back on purpose, now, without judging and without clinging, as often as it drifts."],["He takes a five-minute walk, then starts the page again.",["ER14","DT9"],"Each of A, B, C and PLEASE lowers vulnerability on its own, so even one still helps."],["He describes what he is doing: \"reading, drifting, coming back\".",["M4"],"A plain description of what happened can't be argued with, and it keeps judgments out."]]],["He lets the memory come and go, but it brings a strong wave of shame.",[["He checks whether the shame fits: was anything he did against his values?",["ER7","ER8"],"Checking the fit tells him whether to repair or to let go."],["He rides the wave, and goes on reading.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."],["He half-smiles, unseen, and opens his hands.",["DT18"],"Half-smile and willing hands: the body tells the brain, and no one else need see it."]]]]}},{"id":"M3","title":"wise mind: the pair of reason and emotion, read together","marks":[["𝔢","emotion mind"],["φ","feel: the emotion made from a thought and the body"],["𝐅","facts"],["𝐄","emotions"],["𝔯","reasonable mind"],["ρ","reason’s map to action"],["η","emotion’s map to action"],["≺","partial order","r06e.html#7d5412","6e · Choosing, Lining up Any Set, and the Top of Every Chain"],["🫁","breath"]],"practice":["ways in :   🫁 settles to the centre  ;   a flake of stone sinking in a lake  ;   steps down an inner spiral stair\nΘ ↦ the pause between breaths  ;   «wise» on the in-breath ,  «mind» on the out-breath\nask(q) in ,  listen out  ;   ask «is this 𝔴?»","r(τ) ≔ how centred in 𝔴 :   0  could not do it   …   5  centred","r(τ) ≠ calm(τ)   it rates getting into 𝔴 ,  not feeling better","eyes open :   learned with eyes shut  ⇏  there with eyes open","where :   the belly ,  the centre of the head ,  or between the eyes","widen :   Θ = centre  ↦  centre ∪ the room around ,   centre kept   focused ,  yet aware of where things are","breath focus is offered ,  never required ,  when it cannot be borne"],"u":"Why can reason not beat emotion mind? Why is a strong emotion not the same as emotion mind?","tree":{"t":"Sarah has to choose between a safe course and the one she loves. What does she do?","o":[["She lists the facts (entry grades, jobs) and her values side by side, and picks what both support.",["M3","DT5"],3,"Wise mind sees the worth of both reason and emotion. Weighing both is the harder, better work."],["She steps back until the choice feels clear and without inner conflict.",["M3"],1,"Everyone has wise mind. Waiting for its signal is like tuning a radio until the static clears."],["She accepts that either choice costs something, and chooses.",["M3","DT13"],4,"No choice is free. Accepting the cost is part of acting from wise mind."],["She lets the deadline pass without choosing.",["M3","G4"],6,"Not choosing makes sense when both courses pull hard. But not choosing is a choice too, and the calendar makes it for her."]],"e":[["She lists facts and values, but both columns point to different courses.",[["She asks wise mind: which choice brings a sense of peace?",["M3"],"Wise mind reads reason and emotion together."],["She writes pros and cons for both, long term above short term.",["DT5"],"Four boxes weigh what the list could not."],["She talks it through with someone she trusts.",["IE16","IE5"],"Friendships grow from being near, being alike, talking, and joining in, so one more try widens the chance."]]],["She waits for the choice to feel clear, but the deadline is in two days.",[["She chooses with the clearest sense she has, and accepts the doubt.",["M3","DT13"],"Wise mind reads reason and emotion together, and can act before every doubt is gone."],["She asks for a short extension on the deadline.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She notices the urgency without letting it decide.",["DT4","M4"],"STOP: stop, step back, observe, then proceed mindfully. Stopping gives a skill time to work."]]],["She chooses, and the next day she regrets it.",[["She checks the facts: is the regret about the course, or about losing the other?",["ER7"],"Regret has causes, and checking them finds which."],["She turns the mind again: she chose, and she accepts the cost.",["DT16"],"Acceptance is a turn made again at every fork."],["She asks whether a switch is still possible, without committing to it.",["IE5","ER12"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."]]]]}},{"id":"M4","title":"the what skills: observe, describe, participate, one at a time","marks":[["Ω","what is observed"],["N","words"],["∌","does not hold"],["⊋","holds everything in, and more than"],["⊊","","r38k.html","38k · Truth Cannot Be Defined Inside"],["👁","what is observed; sight"],["👂","hearing"],["👃","smell"],["👅","taste"],["✋","hold back; touch"],["🌊","urges"]],"practice":["𝗈 :   one sense at a time :   👁 without following ,  👂 ,  👃 ,  👅 ,  ✋ ,   then 💭 and 🌊 as they pass","𝗉 :   sing ,  dance ,  play ,  run ,  join a talk ;   become the count of the breath ,   become a word said over and over","𝗈 :   threes :  Θ = {🫁} → {🫁, ✋} → {🫁, ✋, 👂} ,   three breaths each ,  restart when one drops","𝖽 :   thoughts sorted into boxes as they pass :  planning ,  worry ,  the past ,  …","𝗉 :   held by the chair ,  the floor ,  the walls :  the kindness of ordinary things","r(τ) ≔ focus ,   wise mind ,   before and after"],"u":"Why is \"a sound\" a description and \"a bird\" not yet one? Why can no one describe another's intent?","tree":{"t":"Terry's lab partner rolls her eyes at his result. What does he do?","o":[["He describes only what he saw (\"you rolled your eyes\") and asks what she thinks of the result.",["M4","IE17"],7,"He cannot observe her thought. Asking finds the cause, which works better than blaming her for one he guessed."],["He separates \"she rolled her eyes\" from \"she thinks I'm stupid\", and the hurt shrinks.",["M4"],6,"A sound is a sound, not yet a bird. The guess was causing the hurt."],["He notices the sting and keeps working on the lab.",["M4","ER22"],1,"Observing the feeling without acting on it is tolerating it."],["He decides she despises him and stops talking to her.",["M4","G4"],6,"He describes a thought he never observed. The lab and the partnership both suffer."]],"e":[["He describes what he saw and asks, and she shrugs and says nothing.",[["He asks once more, plainly, and lets it be if she still says nothing.",["IE5","IE13"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["He describes only her shrug, and does not fill in the rest.",["M4","IE17"],"A plain description of what happened can't be argued with, and it keeps judgments out."],["He goes on with the lab and checks the result with the demonstrator.",["ER12"],"Problem solving is a loop: when a solution fails or a new problem comes, go back to the start."]]],["He separates the facts, but she rolls her eyes again at his next result.",[["He keeps the facts separate, again.",["M4","DT16"],"A plain description of what happened can't be argued with, and it keeps judgments out."],["He says how it lands: \"when you roll your eyes, I'm not sure what you mean\".",["IE6","IE5"],"When the conversation itself is the problem, describe what is happening between you and ask for one change."],["He lets the sting rise and fall.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["He keeps working, but the result turns out to be wrong.",[["He finds where it went wrong and reruns the step.",["ER12","G13"],"The error is a problem to solve, not a verdict."],["He drops \"she was right, I'm hopeless\".",["M5","IE21"],"Not judging, one thing at a time, doing what works: the how skills keep the next step clear."],["He accepts the mistake and tells his partner what he changed.",["DT13","IE10"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]]]}},{"id":"M5","title":"the how skills: not judging, one thing, what works, all at once","marks":[["J_disc","a judgment that tells things apart"],["≟","is it the same as?"],["a⋆","the act that works"],["max","max and min","r08b.html#0ef1ef","8b · Any Two Reals Compare"],["Wish_","Wish_☉ φ: ☉ wishes that φ"],["≻","ranks above"]],"practice":["𝗇 :   say «a judging thought arose»  →  count them  →  replace each with a fact\n𝗈( judging face ,  posture ,  voice )  →  change them","n_J(☼)  ↓  over the days   the count is the measure","n_J(d)  reset each day ;   the steps taken easiest first ,  one a week","judging word or tone  ⇒  start the sentence over","𝟣 :   one word ,  «one» ,  on each breath ;   𝗈( wanting to move )  ∧  ¬move ;   go a little past wanting to stop","𝖾 :   angry ,  wilful  or  wanting to be right   ⇒   ask «does this work?» ;   willing hands"],"u":"Not judging is not approving. What do you say in place of \"bad\", and why does that keep what follows?","tree":{"t":"Claire calls her own essay \"rubbish\". What does she do?","o":[["She lists what is weak (the argument in section 2, no counterexample) and fixes those.",["M5"],3,"Saying what is, and what follows, gives her work to do. Trying harder needs a target."],["She swaps \"rubbish\" for \"it is a first draft, and I am disappointed\".",["M5"],7,"That drops the rating and keeps the facts and the feeling. Judging gives way to describing."],["She accepts it is not her best, and submits it on time.",["M5","DT13"],1,"Not judging is not approving. She is doing the best she can with the time she has."],["She deletes the draft and starts again the night before.",["M5","G4"],6,"The judgment decides the act. It costs her the work she had."]],"e":[["She fixes section 2, but now section 3 looks weak too.",[["She lists what is weak in section 3 and fixes one thing at a time.",["M5","ER18"],"One thing at a time, and mastery by steps."],["She asks a friend to read it and say what works.",["IE5","IE16"],"Another reader gives facts, not judgments."],["She sets a limit: two fixes, then submit.",["M5","DT11"],"What works: a finished essay beats a perfect one."]]],["She swaps the words, but the disappointment stays strong.",[["She lets the disappointment be.",["ER22","DT13"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."],["She does one pleasant thing before going back to it.",["ER16"],"Small pleasant events add up when there are many of them, varied, over time."],["She describes the disappointment as a wave, and watches it.",["M4","ER22"],"A plain description of what happened can't be argued with, and it keeps judgments out."]]],["She submits on time, and the mark is lower than she hoped.",[["She reads the feedback without judging it, and notes two things to change.",["M5","IE24"],"Feedback that corrects is invalidation that helps."],["She accepts the mark.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."],["She asks the tutor which change would help most.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."]]]]}},{"id":"M6","title":"mindfulness from a spiritual side: boundaries let go","marks":[["∂","the boundary of; in a rate, a partial change"]],"practice":[],"u":"How can freedom be wanting what you lack and still finding a life worth living without it?","tree":{"t":"Mike did not get into his first-choice university. What does he do?","o":[["He looks at transfer routes and second-year entry at his first choice.",["M6","ER12"],4,"He has to solve it even though he did not cause it. Wanting what he lacks can drive a plan."],["He finds what is worth living for at the university he did get into.",["M6"],2,"Freedom is wanting what you lack and finding a life worth living without it. He wants to improve where he is."],["He lets himself be sad about it, and goes to the open day anyway.",["M6","DT13"],1,"A spacious mind lets the sadness come and go. He does the best he can."],["He refuses to accept the offer and sulks.",["M6","G4"],6,"Sulking makes sense: he worked hard for that place. The cost is that clinging to the lost place keeps him from the one he has."]],"e":[["He looks at transfer routes, but they need a grade average higher than he has.",[["He makes a plan to raise his average this year.",["ER12","ER17"],"Problem solving is a loop: when a solution fails or a new problem comes, go back to the start."],["He holds the wish lightly while he works where he is.",["M6"],"Wanting what he lacks, and living without it."],["He asks the admissions office what else counts.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."]]],["He finds things worth living for, but a friend posts photos from the first-choice campus.",[["He notices the envy and lets it pass.",["ER6","ER22"],"Naming an emotion and what it is saying lets you stand apart from it and still own it."],["He mutes the posts for a while.",["DT24"],"Removing a cue is burning a small bridge."],["He sends his friend a kind message.",["M7","IE7"],"Kindness moves outward by steps, and it can be acted before it is felt."]]],["He goes to the open day, and it rains and the halls look shabby.",[["He describes what he sees without judging: \"old building, wet day\".",["M4","M5"],"A plain description of what happened can't be argued with, and it keeps judgments out."],["He finds one thing he liked: the library, the people.",["M6","DT11"],"Wanting what is missing can sit beside a life worth living; holding the wish lightly is the skill."],["He lets the sadness come back, and stays for the afternoon.",["ER22","DT13"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]]]}},{"id":"M7","title":"loving kindness: wishing well, outward by steps","marks":[],"practice":["O ⊆ { warmth ,  love ,  compassion ,  connection ,  wisdom ,  🙂 ,  ✓_☉(☉) }   toward h ,   even a little","palms open ,  breathe slowly ;   after each round ,  let go of tension ;   drift  ⇒  Wish_☉(happy(☉)) ,  then return ;   each day"],"u":"Why does loving kindness move outward by steps, and why choose only someone you want to feel kindly toward?","tree":{"t":"Zoe resents a classmate who got the part she wanted in the school play. What does she do?","o":[["She congratulates the classmate, and asks to understudy.",["M7","IE7"],2,"Wishing well outward can become an act. She still wants to improve, and an understudy role keeps her in."],["She works out from herself to the classmate, a few sincere words at each step.",["M7"],5,"Loving kindness moves outward by steps, and has to be practised for this person in particular."],["She lets the resentment be there without feeding it.",["M7","ER22"],1,"She chooses someone she wants to feel kindly toward, and does the best she can until she does."],["She keeps the grudge all term.",["M7","G4"],6,"The grudge makes sense: she wanted that part. The cost is a term of rehearsals spent resenting the person beside her."]],"e":[["She congratulates the classmate, but the classmate seems smug.",[["She gives the benefit of the doubt and keeps to her part as understudy.",["IE17","M7"],"She cannot observe the classmate's intent. Kindness is still hers to give."],["She lets the resentment rise and fall.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."],["She focuses on learning the lines.",["ER18","M5"],"Mastery is built one hard-but-possible step at a time, and coping ahead means rehearsing the hard scene first."]]],["She works outward with kind words, but they feel fake.",[["She keeps going, starting closer to home.",["M7"],"Loving kindness moves outward by steps; she can start nearer."],["She acts as if she meant them, and lets the feeling follow.",["ER10"],"Opposite action works only all the way, and repeated until the emotion changes."],["She notices the judgment \"fake\" and drops it.",["M5"],"Not judging, one thing at a time, doing what works: the how skills keep the next step clear."]]],["She lets the resentment be, but the classmate asks her to help rehearse.",[["She says yes for one session, with GIVE.",["IE7","IE4"],"GIVE keeps the relationship: gentle, interested in their side, validating, with an easy manner."],["She says no kindly, high on the ladder.",["IE13","IE10"],"The ladder lets the ask or the no be said again, at the same height or higher, without softening it."],["She makes pros and cons before she answers.",["DT5"],"Pros and cons, written before the urge decides, weigh the long term above the now."]]]]}},{"id":"M8","title":"doing mind and being mind, balanced in wise mind","marks":[["𝔡","doing mind"],["𝔟","being mind"],["λ","a mixing weight between 0 and 1"],["🫀","body sensations"],["❤","emotions"]],"practice":["reminders :   a written line ,  a set alarm ,  a note left where you will see it","read a line that makes you want to practise ;   «just this one moment ,  just this one task» ≔  Θ = one dish","resting :   stay aware of what needs doing ;   asked to do it :  do it ,  willing ,  whole","∀ ☼ :   one everyday act done one moment at a time","pleasant event :   aware of 🙂 while it happened ,  true or false   🫀 ,  ❤ ,  💭  during","unpleasant event :   aware of it while it happened ,  true or false   🫀 ,  ❤ ,  💭  during"],"u":"What does each mind get wrong when there is too much of it? Why does wise mind balance them instead of choosing one?","tree":{"t":"Jean is so driven by her grade average that she stops noticing her own day. What does she do?","o":[["She keeps her study plan and adds one hour a day with no goal in it.",["M8"],5,"Too much doing runs on autopilot. Balance has to be practised in her real week."],["She lets go of needing to reach the average, while still working toward it.",["M8"],2,"Work toward a goal, and let go of needing it. She wants to improve without losing her life."],["She accepts that some weeks will lean to doing, and notices one moment each day.",["M8","DT13"],1,"Wise mind balances the two over time. She does the best she can this week."],["She keeps grinding and stops noticing anything else.",["M8","G4"],6,"Too little being misses her own life. That is a choice with causes."]],"e":[["She adds the free hour, but spends it worrying about grades.",[["She brings her attention to one sense at a time: taste, sound, touch.",["M8","DT10"],"Being mind needs something to attend to."],["She lets the worry come and go, and stays in the hour.",["M2","DT19"],"Attention comes back on purpose, now, without judging and without clinging, as often as it drifts."],["She plans the hour as a walk, so it has something in it.",["ER16"],"Small pleasant events add up when there are many of them, varied, over time."]]],["She lets go of needing the average, and her next mark drops.",[["She checks the facts: one mark, still within range.",["ER7"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."],["She keeps working toward the average, without needing it.",["M8","M3"],"Doing mind and being mind both have a place; wise mind keeps a little of each."],["She accepts the dip, and keeps the plan.",["DT13","G15"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["She notices one moment each day, but forgets for a week.",[["She sets a cue: one moment at each meal.",["G14"],"The first question that fails (knew it, willing, remembered) shows what to fix: often just a cue."],["She starts again without judging the week.",["M5","DT16"],"Not judging, one thing at a time, doing what works: the how skills keep the next step clear."],["She asks a friend to share one moment a day with her.",["IE16","IE5"],"Friendships grow from being near, being alike, talking, and joining in, so one more try widens the chance."]]]]}},{"id":"M9","title":"the middle path: a synthesis of opposites","marks":[["x̄","the opposite pole of x"],["½","one half","r05f.html#3281a5","5f · Between Any Two, Another; Yet Holes"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why is the middle path not half of each side, nor the point halfway between?","tree":{"t":"Evita wants to be top of the year and wants to enjoy university. What does she do?","o":[["She sets a study schedule that leaves two evenings a week free, and keeps both.",["M9"],3,"The middle path is both-and. She works hard and lives fully."],["She drops \"either top or happy\" for \"both, in my own balance\".",["M9","IE20"],7,"Either-or becomes both-and. Her centre is her own, not someone else's."],["She accepts that some weeks will tip one way, and comes back to balance.",["M9"],1,"The middle path can go to an extreme and come back. She does the best she can."],["She gives up enjoying anything until graduation.",["M9","G4"],6,"It makes sense: one goal is simpler than two. But it is an extreme, chosen, and it costs her three years of university life."]],"e":[["She keeps two evenings free, but a big assignment lands on one of them.",[["She swaps the free evening to another day that week.",["M9","ER12"],"The middle path holds both sides whole and bends with the week without breaking."],["She asks for a short extension.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She works that evening, and plans the swing back.",["M10","DT13"],"A balanced life swings and comes back; planning the swing back keeps the pairs held."]]],["She says \"both\", but her friends call her a try-hard.",[["She validates herself: wanting both is fine.",["IE24"],"Validating yourself, exactly as you would a friend, is how to recover when someone invalidates you."],["She lets the comment pass.",["DT19"],"A thought is a sensation of the brain, not a fact and not you. It can be watched as it comes and goes."],["She keeps one evening with those friends, as planned.",["M9","IE7"],"The middle path holds both sides whole and bends with the week without breaking."]]],["She comes back to balance, but finals tip her far to one side for a month.",[["She accepts the month, and plans one small rest each day.",["M9","ER16"],"A small positive keeps the line from snapping."],["She checks her body: sleep and food.",["ER19"],"The body's basics (illness treated, balanced eating, sleep, exercise, no mood-altering drugs) lower vulnerability."],["She plans the swing back for after finals.",["M10","ER18"],"A balanced life swings and comes back; planning the swing back keeps the pairs held."]]]]}},{"id":"M10","title":"a balanced life: thirteen pairs held at once","marks":[["🖐","actions"],["≈","","r05.html","5 · Making Room to Undo"]],"practice":["∀ j :   ☉ marks a point x_☉ on the line  xⱼ ⟷ x̄ⱼ   where ☉ stands most of the time","x_☉ near an end   ⇒   that end is the one to work on","pull to xⱼ ,  pull to x̄ⱼ ,  and how the tension was held   a calendar of it","pick one pair :   too much ,  too little  ↦  do less ,  do more ;   check the facts and your own values ;   one or two exact steps next week"],"u":"Why does a balanced life hold all thirteen pairs at once, and not settle each one in turn?","tree":{"t":"A student cramming for finals has tipped toward the extremes. What does she do?","o":[["She picks two pairs, work and rest and need and want, and plans one move toward the middle in each.",["M10"],3,"A balanced life holds all thirteen pairs. Two moves is trying harder in a way she can keep up."],["She notices where she has tipped without judging it, and the strain eases.",["M10","M5"],7,"Seeing which end she has gone to finds the cause. Judging it would add one more extreme."],["She accepts that exam weeks lean one way, and plans the swing back for afterwards.",["M10"],1,"Balance is over time, not in every hour. She does the best she can."],["She stays at the extremes after finals too.",["M10","G4"],6,"The extremes have causes, and they last until she chooses otherwise."]],"e":[["She plans two moves toward the middle, but the first week she manages neither.",[["She picks just one, and one small step in it.",["M10","ER18"],"A balanced life swings and comes back; planning the swing back keeps the pairs held."],["She runs a missing-links check: did she forget, or was she unwilling?",["G14"],"The first question that fails (knew it, willing, remembered) shows what to fix: often just a cue."],["She lets the week go, and begins again.",["DT16","M5"],"Acceptance is a turn of the mind made again at every fork; one turn does not make the next."]]],["She notices where she has tipped, and sees how far: four pairs, not two.",[["She chooses one pair to start with, and leaves the rest.",["M10","DT11"],"A balanced life swings and comes back; planning the swing back keeps the pairs held."],["She does not judge the count.",["M5"],"Not judging, one thing at a time, doing what works: the how skills keep the next step clear."],["She talks it through with someone who knows her.",["IE16","IE5"],"A balance is easier to see from outside."]]],["She plans the swing back for afterwards, but finals end and she feels flat and empty.",[["She plans one pleasant thing a day for a week.",["ER16"],"Small pleasant events add up when there are many of them, varied, over time."],["She lets the flatness be, and checks her body.",["ER22","ER19"],"Flatness after strain often has a body cause."],["She picks one value to move toward next term.",["ER17"],"A value comes down to one step that can be taken now."]]]]}}]},{"id":"IE","page":"dbtie","name":"interpersonal effectiveness","footings":27,"steps":[{"id":"IE1","title":"goals: get what you want, keep the tie, keep your respect, walk the middle","marks":[["γ","the objective"],["Q","self-respect"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why use the skills early? What does the tear that is left alone show about mending?","tree":{"t":"Zoe has let a tutor's dismissive comments pile up all term. What does she do?","o":[["She asks for a short meeting and raises one comment, calmly, before the next essay is due.",["IE1","IE5"],4,"A tear left alone gets harder to mend. She did not cause the comments, but she can head off the next ones."],["She sorts which comments were fair feedback and which were not, and lets the fair ones help.",["IE1","IE24"],7,"Corrective feedback is invalidation that helps. Telling the two apart beats resenting them all."],["She accepts that the tutor is curt, and keeps her focus on the work.",["IE1","DT13"],1,"Weighing her priorities against the tutor's manner is a choice. She is doing the best she can, and the essays come first this term."],["She keeps quiet and dreads every class.",["IE1","G4"],6,"Keeping quiet makes sense: speaking up feels risky with someone who holds her marks. The cost is that the hurts keep piling up, and the tear gets harder to mend."]],"e":[["She raises one comment calmly, and the tutor says she is being oversensitive.",[["She stays on her one point, calmly, and asks for one change.",["IE1","IE5"],"A tear is mended early, one stitch at a time. The broken record keeps her on her ask."],["She validates herself: raising it was fair, whatever the reply.",["IE24"],"Validating yourself, exactly as you would a friend, is how to recover when someone invalidates you."],["She lets it be for today, and writes down what was said.",["DT13","G15"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["She sorts the comments, but the fair ones make her feel worse about her writing.",[["She picks the one fair comment that would help most, and works on that.",["ER18","M5"],"Mastery is built one hard-but-possible step at a time, and coping ahead means rehearsing the hard scene first."],["She keeps sorting, all the way through.",["IE1","IE24"],"Telling corrective feedback from dismissal is the skill."],["She does something soothing, then comes back to the list.",["DT10"],"Soothing each of the five senses is a way of being gentle with yourself, then going on."]]],["She keeps her focus on the work, but the next comment is in front of the whole class.",[["She asks for a word after class about how feedback is given.",["IE5","IE6"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She lets the embarrassment rise and fall, and stays in the seat.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."],["She talks it over with a classmate she trusts.",["IE16","IE22"],"Friendships grow from being near, being alike, talking, and joining in, so one more try widens the chance."]]]]}},{"id":"IE2","title":"what gets in the way","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why is having a skill not the same as wanting to use it? How do two of the obstacles feed one another?","tree":{"t":"Mike never asks for help in office hours. What does he do?","o":[["He names the first obstacle (he doesn't know what to ask) and writes down two questions before he goes.",["IE2","IE15"],5,"A skill has to be used where it is needed. Writing the questions removes the first obstacle."],["He sees that worry and lack of skill feed each other, and stops blaming himself.",["IE2"],7,"The obstacles feed one another. Finding the loop beats calling himself shy."],["He goes once, feeling awkward, and lets the awkwardness be.",["IE2","ER22"],1,"Having a skill is not the same as wanting to use it. He is doing the best he can, and the awkwardness can come along."],["He keeps working alone and falls behind.",["IE2","G4"],6,"Working alone makes sense: it spares him the awkward minute. The cost is that short-term comfort beats the long-term goal, and the gap grows."]],"e":[["He goes with his two questions, but there is a long queue and he loses his nerve.",[["He stays in the queue and reads his questions again.",["ER10","IE2"],"Opposite action to the urge to leave, and the obstacle he named is already handled."],["He emails the questions instead.",["IE5","ER12"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["He breathes out longer than in while he waits.",["DT6"],"TIP changes the body to change the emotion fast: cold water, hard exercise, paced breathing, paired relaxation."]]],["He stops blaming himself, but still can't make himself go.",[["He walks the obstacles in order: skill, worry, emotion, wants, setting.",["IE15","IE2"],"Troubleshooting walks the obstacles in order and fixes the first that fails."],["He makes pros and cons of going.",["DT5"],"Pros and cons, written before the urge decides, weigh the long term above the now."],["He asks a classmate to go with him.",["IE16","IE5"],"Friendships grow from being near, being alike, talking, and joining in, so one more try widens the chance."]]],["He goes once, and the lecturer answers briskly and moves on.",[["He gives the benefit of the doubt: the lecturer had a queue.",["IE17"],"No one can observe another's thoughts, so a guess about them is held loosely and given the benefit of the doubt."],["He goes again next week with one question.",["ER10","G5"],"Opposite action is repeated until it changes."],["He notices the urge to never go back, and lets it pass.",["DT19","DT4"],"A thought is a sensation of the brain, not a fact and not you. It can be watched as it comes and goes."]]]]}},{"id":"IE3","title":"myths: universal claims, broken by one case","marks":[["⊥","","r01.html","1 · Making Expressions"]],"practice":[],"u":"A myth claims something always holds. Why is one real case enough to break it, and why is the weakest contradicting claim often enough to answer it?","tree":{"t":"Jean believes \"if I ask my teacher for help, they will think I'm stupid\". What does she do?","o":[["She asks one question this week and checks what actually happens.",["IE3","ER7"],5,"One real case breaks a myth. She learns the new behaviour by testing it in class."],["She answers the myth with \"sometimes asking shows I care\".",["IE3"],7,"The weakest claim that contradicts a myth is often enough. Answering it beats blaming herself for believing it."],["She accepts that asking feels risky, and asks anyway.",["IE3","ER10"],1,"Acting against the myth while the fear is still there is a way of tolerating it. She is doing the best she can."],["She keeps the myth and asks no one.",["IE3","G4"],6,"The myth makes sense: someone once made her feel stupid. The cost is that it goes unchecked, and she stays stuck on what she could have asked."]],"e":[["She asks one question, and the teacher looks puzzled by it.",[["She rephrases the question, plainly.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She checks the facts: puzzled is not \"you're stupid\".",["ER7","IE17"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."],["She counts it as one case, and asks again next week.",["IE3","G15"],"One case can break a myth, and more trials build the record."]]],["She answers the myth, but a classmate sniggers when she asks.",[["She keeps asking, and lets the snigger pass.",["ER10","DT19"],"Opposite action works only all the way, and repeated until the emotion changes."],["She validates herself: asking is how learning works.",["IE24","IE3"],"Validating yourself, exactly as you would a friend, is how to recover when someone invalidates you."],["She asks the teacher after class instead this time.",["IE4","IE5"],"Weighing the three aims again (what you want, the relationship, your self-respect) tells which to put first now."]]],["She asks anyway, and the answer confuses her more.",[["She asks one follow-up question.",["IE5","IE13"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She notes what she still doesn't get and looks it up.",["ER12"],"Problem solving is a loop: when a solution fails or a new problem comes, go back to the start."],["She lets the confusion be, and accepts it may take two tries.",["DT13","DT16"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]]]}},{"id":"IE4","title":"clarifying priorities: weigh three aims before acting","marks":[["ω⃗","the three weights taken together"],["V","values"]],"practice":["use it when :   your rights are not respected  ;   you want something done ,  changed or given  ;   you must say no\nyou want your view taken seriously  ;   there is conflict  ;   you want a relationship better","rank :   γ , γ_R , γ_Q   ↦   1 , 2 , 3 ;   note where the ranks pull against each other"],"u":"Why does how you want to feel after not depend on getting the objective? What goes wrong when the relationship always comes first?","tree":{"t":"Sarah wants her tutor to regrade an essay. What does she do?","o":[["She decides the regrade matters most, keeps it polite for the relationship, and asks with evidence.",["IE4","IE5"],4,"All three aims count. The objective leads, and the other two decide how she goes after it."],["She sees that her self-respect is the real aim, whatever the mark, and the pressure eases.",["IE4","IE10"],7,"How she wants to feel afterwards does not depend on the mark. Finding the real aim removes the pressure."],["She accepts the mark this time, and asks how to do better next time.",["IE4","DT13"],1,"The relationship can be the objective too. She is doing the best she can, and keeps the tutor on side."],["She complains to friends and never asks the tutor.",["IE4","G4"],6,"Venting makes sense: it feels safer than asking. The cost is that no aim is met: no regrade, no relationship, no self-respect."]],"e":[["She asks with evidence, and the tutor says no regrades this term.",[["She asks what the formal appeal route is.",["IE5","ER12"],"The objective is still open by another route."],["She weighs again: is the mark worth an appeal, or is the relationship worth more now?",["IE4"],"Priorities can be weighed again with new facts."],["She thanks the tutor, and keeps her self-respect.",["IE10","IE7"],"The relationship and her self-respect are aims too."]]],["She sees self-respect as the aim, but the low mark still stings at night.",[["She lets the sting be, and aims for rest.",["ER21","ER22"],"In bed, a solvable worry gets a two-line plan; otherwise the aim is rest, not sleep."],["She writes down what she did well in the essay.",["IE24","ER16"],"Validating yourself, exactly as you would a friend, is how to recover when someone invalidates you."],["She plans the next essay with the comments beside her.",["ER18"],"Mastery is built one hard-but-possible step at a time, and coping ahead means rehearsing the hard scene first."]]],["She asks how to do better, and the tutor's advice contradicts the last tutor's.",[["She asks which matters most for this module.",["IE5","IE19"],"Two truths can both hold; she asks which applies here."],["She tries both on a practice paragraph and sees which works.",["G15"],"One try is one trial. Only a record of several shows where a skill works, so the trial goes on."],["She accepts the mixed advice, and follows the current tutor.",["DT13","IE4"],"Weighing aims: this tutor marks this module."]]]]}},{"id":"IE5","title":"DEAR MAN: getting what you want","marks":[],"practice":["before :   write the script for 𝖽𝖾𝖺𝗋 𝗆𝖺𝗇 ,  𝗀𝗂𝗏𝖾 ,  𝖿𝖺𝗌𝗍   →   say it aloud   →   say it in mind   →   cope ahead","after :   what was said ,  which letters were used ,  what came of it for γ , γ_R , γ_Q","look for chances to practise ,  small ones first"],"u":"Why say \"I want\" and not \"you should\"? Why does answering an attack hand over control?","tree":{"t":"Jean needs a one-week extension on her dissertation chapter. What does she do?","o":[["She emails her supervisor using DEAR MAN: the facts, her feelings, a clear ask, what is in it for him, and a new date.",["IE5"],4,"She has to solve it, even if illness caused the delay. DEAR MAN is the skill for getting what you want."],["She stops dreading the email by seeing it as a request, not a confession.",["IE5","ER7"],6,"The dread comes from the reading. Checking the facts changes the response."],["She sends the ask and accepts that it might be refused.",["IE5","DT13"],1,"She is doing the best she can. Accepting a possible no is part of asking."],["She doesn't ask, and hands in a rushed chapter.",["IE5","G4"],6,"Not asking makes sense: asking feels like admitting failure. The cost is a rushed chapter that carries her name."]],"e":[["She sends the DEAR MAN email, and the supervisor replies \"we'll see\".",[["She replies with a date and asks for a yes or no by Friday.",["IE5","IE12"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She plans for both answers.",["ER18"],"Mastery is built one hard-but-possible step at a time, and coping ahead means rehearsing the hard scene first."],["She lets the uncertainty be until Friday.",["DT13","DT11"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["The dread eases, but she reads the reply as a no before it arrives.",[["She checks the facts: no reply yet is no answer yet.",["ER7"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."],["She notices the thought as a thought.",["DT19","M1"],"A thought is a sensation of the brain, not a fact and not you. It can be watched as it comes and goes."],["She does one task on the chapter while she waits.",["DT9","ER18"],"Distraction (wise mind ACCEPTS) gets you through a crisis, used once, with a plan to come back."]]],["She asks, and the extension is refused.",[["She asks for half: three days.",["IE5","IE13"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She accepts the no, and plans the week around the original date.",["DT13","ER12"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."],["She asks who else can grant it, if illness is the reason.",["IE14","IE11"],"Telling the self, the others and the agent apart shows who can decide and by whose rule."]]]]}},{"id":"IE6","title":"DEAR MAN on the conversation itself","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"When you turn the skills on the conversation itself, why describe what is happening between you, but not why they do it?","tree":{"t":"Terry's group keeps talking over his plan in meetings. What does he do?","o":[["He describes the meeting itself: \"I've started three times and haven't finished. Can I have two minutes?\"",["IE6","IE5"],4,"When the others keep pressing, turn the skills on the exchange itself. He leaves out motives and shoulds."],["He stops reading it as \"they think I'm useless\".",["IE6","IE17"],7,"He cannot observe their motives. Finding the cause, the group's habits, beats blaming them."],["He writes his plan in the group chat, and accepts the meetings as they are.",["IE6","DT13"],1,"He is doing the best he can in that setting. The plan still reaches them."],["He stops contributing at all.",["IE6","G4"],6,"Going quiet makes sense: being talked over hurts. The cost is that his plan is lost, and the group learns he will not push back."]],"e":[["He describes the meeting, and someone says \"we don't have time for this\".",[["He repeats his ask, calmly: two minutes.",["IE6","IE5"],"The broken record, on the exchange itself."],["He offers to send the plan in writing tonight.",["IE4","ER12"],"Weighing the three aims again (what you want, the relationship, your self-respect) tells which to put first now."],["He gives the benefit of the doubt: they are stressed.",["IE17"],"No one can observe another's thoughts, so a guess about them is held loosely and given the benefit of the doubt."]]],["He stops reading it as \"useless\", but at the next meeting it happens again.",[["He describes the meeting again, earlier this time.",["IE6","IE1"],"When the conversation itself is the problem, describe what is happening between you and ask for one change."],["He checks the facts about the group's habits.",["ER7","IE17"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."],["He asks one teammate to back him when he speaks.",["IE16","IE5"],"Friendships grow from being near, being alike, talking, and joining in, so one more try widens the chance."]]],["He writes the plan in the chat, and no one reads it.",[["He asks the group lead to put it on the next agenda.",["IE14","IE5"],"Asking through the one with the authority."],["He accepts it for now, and keeps the plan ready.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."],["He pins it, and asks for a thumbs-up from each person.",["IE25"],"Rewarding small steps right away shapes a behaviour where it is needed."]]]]}},{"id":"IE7","title":"GIVE: keeping the relationship","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"\"A little\" humour and a \"soft\" sell are amounts. Some of what, and why neither none nor all the way?","tree":{"t":"Claire must tell her lab partner that his half of the report is not good enough. What does she do?","o":[["She says it with GIVE: gently, interested in his view, validating his workload, with a light touch.",["IE7","IE5"],5,"Keeping the relationship while asking for change is a skill, and the lab is where it gets learned."],["She validates why he rushed while disagreeing with the result, and feels less angry.",["IE7","IE22"],6,"His rush has causes. Seeing them changes her own response."],["She fixes the worst parts herself this time, and accepts the uneven split.",["IE7","DT13"],1,"She is doing the best she can before the deadline. The talk can come after."],["She says nothing and resents him.",["IE7","G4"],6,"Silence makes sense: it avoids a hard talk. The cost is that the report and the partnership both get worse."]],"e":[["She uses GIVE, and her partner gets defensive.",[["She validates how stressful the week was for him, and repeats her one ask.",["IE7","IE22"],"GIVE keeps the relationship: gentle, interested in their side, validating, with an easy manner."],["She stops and suggests talking tomorrow.",["DT4","IE18"],"Deciding in wise mind, not emotion mind."],["She keeps her voice gentle, and asks what he needs to fix it.",["IE7","IE8"],"GIVE keeps the relationship: gentle, interested in their side, validating, with an easy manner."]]],["She validates his rush, but he takes it as agreement and changes nothing.",[["She says it plainly: \"I understand the rush, and the section needs rewriting\".",["IE22","IE5"],"Validating what makes sense is not agreeing with all of it, and it calms things enough to go on."],["She sets a clear deadline.",["IE12","IE5"],"How firmly to act follows the count of checks in favour, and one more check never makes it softer."],["She lets her frustration be, and asks again tomorrow.",["ER22","IE13"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["She fixes the worst parts, but the tutor marks the report down for uneven style.",[["She asks for the marks to be split by section.",["IE5","IE10"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She accepts this mark and plans the split earlier next time.",["DT13","ER18"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."],["She talks to her partner about the next report, using GIVE.",["IE7","IE4"],"GIVE keeps the relationship: gentle, interested in their side, validating, with an easy manner."]]]]}},{"id":"IE8","title":"a helper's stance: three pairs held at once","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why must the helper hold all six stances at once, and not move along one line from end to end?","tree":{"t":"Randy tutors a younger student who keeps giving up on algebra. What does he do?","o":[["He says \"this is hard, and you can do the next step\", and sets one small problem.",["IE8","ER18"],3,"Change and acceptance held at once: he asks for more out of goodwill, and he nurtures. She needs to try harder, one step at a time."],["He reminds himself the student is doing the best she can, and his frustration drops.",["IE8","G6"],6,"His frustration was caused by reading her as lazy. That assumption makes room for patience."],["He accepts slow progress, and stays steady and centred.",["IE8"],1,"Being unwavering and flexible at once is how a helper tolerates. He is doing the best he can, and so is she."],["He gets frustrated and does the problems for her.",["IE8","G4"],6,"It makes sense: the session ends faster. The cost is that she learns nothing new, and gives up sooner next time."]],"e":[["He sets one small problem, and she won't even try it.",[["He makes the step smaller still: just the first line.",["IE8","IE25"],"A helper holds change and acceptance at once: steady and flexible, asking for more and nurturing."],["He validates how frustrating algebra feels, and waits.",["IE22","IE23"],"Validating what makes sense is not agreeing with all of it, and it calms things enough to go on."],["He tries a different kind of problem she likes.",["IE27"],"A consequence is relative: find what works for her."]]],["His frustration drops, but she says \"you think I'm stupid\".",[["He tells her plainly that he doesn't, and why he's there.",["IE10","IE5"],"FAST keeps self-respect: fair, no needless apologies, sticking to values, truthful."],["He validates the feeling: \"it makes sense to feel that way when it's hard\".",["IE22"],"Validating what makes sense is not agreeing with all of it, and it calms things enough to go on."],["He stays steady and centred, and goes on.",["IE8"],"A helper holds change and acceptance at once: steady and flexible, asking for more and nurturing."]]],["He accepts slow progress, but her parents ask why she hasn't improved.",[["He describes what she can do now that she couldn't before.",["M4","IE5"],"A plain description of what happened can't be argued with, and it keeps judgments out."],["He weighs his aims: the job, the relationship, his self-respect.",["IE4"],"Weighing the three aims again (what you want, the relationship, your self-respect) tells which to put first now."],["He lets their worry be theirs, and keeps teaching.",["DT13","IE8"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]]]}},{"id":"IE9","title":"what wears a relationship down","marks":[["D_R","behaviours that wear a relationship down"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why do these behaviours wear down any relationship, whoever does them? What do many of them signal about the one doing them?","tree":{"t":"Evita keeps cancelling on her study group at the last minute. What does she do?","o":[["She commits to two sessions she can keep, and tells the group a day ahead if she can't make one.",["IE9","G5"],3,"Unreliability wears any relationship down. She needs to do better, and a smaller promise is one she can keep."],["She sees the cancelling as a sign she is worn out, not that she is a bad person.",["IE9","ER19"],6,"Many of these behaviours are signs of being worn out. Finding the cause lowers the shame."],["She accepts that the group is cooler with her, and shows up next time.",["IE9","DT13"],1,"She is doing the best she can, and the trust comes back over time."],["She keeps cancelling.",["IE9","G4"],6,"Cancelling makes sense: each time, she is too tired. The cost is that the tie wears through."]],"e":[["She commits to two sessions, and on the second day she is ill.",[["She tells the group a day ahead, as she promised.",["IE9","IE1"],"Reliability is what wears down or builds a tie."],["She checks the facts: being ill is not unreliable.",["ER7","IE20"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."],["She asks for the notes and joins online.",["IE5","ER12"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."]]],["She sees she is worn out, but the group lead says she is letting them down.",[["She says what she can promise, plainly.",["IE10","IE5"],"FAST keeps self-respect: fair, no needless apologies, sticking to values, truthful."],["She validates herself: worn out is a cause, not a flaw.",["IE24","G6"],"Validating yourself, exactly as you would a friend, is how to recover when someone invalidates you."],["She works on her sleep and meals this week.",["ER19"],"The body's basics (illness treated, balanced eating, sleep, exercise, no mood-altering drugs) lower vulnerability."]]],["She shows up next time, and they have moved on without her.",[["She asks what she missed and takes a task.",["IE5","IE16"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She lets the hurt be there, and stays.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."],["She gives it time, and keeps showing up.",["DT16","IE9"],"Acceptance is a turn of the mind made again at every fork; one turn does not make the next."]]]]}},{"id":"IE10","title":"FAST: keeping self-respect","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why does apologizing too much wear down both your mastery and the relationship? When may GIVE be set aside?","tree":{"t":"Mike apologizes three times for asking a question in seminar. What does he do?","o":[["He asks his next question once, with no apology.",["IE10"],5,"No needless apologies. The skill is learned one question at a time, in the seminar."],["He reminds himself that asking is his right, and the shame eases.",["IE10","IE24"],7,"Validating himself beats the shame that drives the apologies."],["He notices the urge to apologize, and lets it pass without acting on it.",["IE10","ER22"],1,"He is doing the best he can, and the urge loses its hold."],["He keeps apologizing, or stops asking.",["IE10","G4"],6,"Apologizing makes sense: it feels polite and safe. The cost is his self-respect, which wears down a little each time."]],"e":[["He asks without apologizing, and the lecturer says \"that's a basic question\".",[["He keeps to his question, calmly, until it is answered.",["IE10","IE5"],"FAST keeps self-respect: fair, no needless apologies, sticking to values, truthful."],["He validates himself: basic questions are allowed.",["IE24"],"Validating yourself, exactly as you would a friend, is how to recover when someone invalidates you."],["He lets the sting pass.",["DT19","ER22"],"A thought is a sensation of the brain, not a fact and not you. It can be watched as it comes and goes."]]],["The shame eases, but he catches himself apologizing again in the next seminar.",[["He notices it, and asks the next question without one.",["IE10","DT16"],"FAST keeps self-respect: fair, no needless apologies, sticking to values, truthful."],["He runs a chain: when does the apology come?",["G13"],"A chain finds the link where a skill would have broken it, and that is where the next try aims."],["He does not judge the slip.",["M5"],"Not judging, one thing at a time, doing what works: the how skills keep the next step clear."]]],["He lets the urge pass, but feels he sounded rude.",[["He checks the facts: did anyone react?",["ER7"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."],["He asks a friend how he came across.",["IE16","IE5"],"Friendships grow from being near, being alike, talking, and joining in, so one more try widens the chance."],["He lets the doubt be, and keeps asking.",["ER22","ER10"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]]]}},{"id":"IE11","title":"ten checks: four about your act, six about the pair of you","marks":[["pⱼ","pⱼ(x, y): a pair check, a relation between the one who wants and the one asked"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why does refusing use the same six checks with the two of you swapped? What limits the count?","tree":{"t":"Zoe wants to ask a professor she has never met for a reference letter. What does she do?","o":[["She runs the six checks, finds the request clear and within the professor's role, and asks.",["IE11","IE12"],4,"The checks are only as good as the wise mind doing them. Here they favour asking."],["She sees that the professor is not bound to say yes, and lowers the stakes.",["IE11"],7,"Who is bound to give is one of the checks. Knowing it calms her."],["She asks, and accepts that a stranger may decline.",["IE11","DT13"],1,"She is doing the best she can. A no from a stranger is not a verdict on her."],["She doesn't ask, and goes without the reference.",["IE11","G4"],6,"Not asking makes sense: a stranger's no feels hard to face. The cost is the reference."]],"e":[["She runs the checks and asks, and the professor doesn't reply for two weeks.",[["She sends one polite follow-up.",["IE5","IE13"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She asks a second professor too.",["ER12"],"Problem solving is a loop: when a solution fails or a new problem comes, go back to the start."],["She lets the wait be, and gives the benefit of the doubt.",["IE17","DT13"],"No one can observe another's thoughts, so a guess about them is held loosely and given the benefit of the doubt."]]],["She lowers the stakes, but the professor says yes with a deadline she can't meet.",[["She asks for a different date, plainly.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She runs the checks again with the new facts.",["IE11"],"The checks (your act, and the pair of you) say whether to ask, and how firmly, with the facts as they are now."],["She plans the week to meet it.",["ER12","ER18"],"Problem solving is a loop: when a solution fails or a new problem comes, go back to the start."]]],["She asks, and the professor declines.",[["She thanks them, and asks who might be better placed.",["IE7","IE5"],"GIVE keeps the relationship: gentle, interested in their side, validating, with an easy manner."],["She accepts the no, and asks someone else.",["DT13","ER12"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."],["She lets the disappointment rise and fall.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]]]}},{"id":"IE12","title":"intensity is a cardinality","marks":[["oⱼ","oⱼ(a): an own check, a property of the act a"],["𝒫","","r02g.html","2g · All the Subsets"]],"practice":[],"u":"If how firmly to act is the number of checks in favour, why can one more check never make you less firm?","tree":{"t":"Most of the checks favour Zoe asking firmly. A new check comes out in favour too. What does she do?","o":[["She asks more firmly: she names a date and follows up.",["IE12"],3,"A check added never makes her less firm. She acts on the count, and tries harder."],["She lets the count settle her doubts.",["IE12"],7,"The count is a cause she can see. It beats guessing."],["She asks firmly, and accepts whatever answer comes.",["IE12","DT13"],1,"She is doing the best she can. Firmness is in the asking, not the answer."],["She asks timidly despite the count.",["IE12","G4"],6,"Timidity makes sense: firmness feels rude to her. The cost is that the intensity she chose does not match the count, and the ask falls flat."]],"e":[["She asks firmly and names a date, and the reply is curt.",[["She stays firm, and keeps her tone kind.",["IE12","IE7"],"How firmly to act follows the count of checks in favour, and one more check never makes it softer."],["She gives the benefit of the doubt.",["IE17"],"No one can observe another's thoughts, so a guess about them is held loosely and given the benefit of the doubt."],["She checks the count again: is it still in favour?",["IE12","IE11"],"How firmly to act follows the count of checks in favour, and one more check never makes it softer."]]],["The count settles her, but her friend says she is being pushy.",[["She checks the facts: she asked once, clearly.",["ER7","IE12"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."],["She validates herself.",["IE24"],"Validating yourself, exactly as you would a friend, is how to recover when someone invalidates you."],["She asks her friend what pushy looked like to them.",["IE6","IE17"],"When the conversation itself is the problem, describe what is happening between you and ask for one change."]]],["She asks firmly, and the answer is no.",[["She asks once more, higher on the ladder.",["IE13","IE5"],"The ladder lets the ask or the no be said again, at the same height or higher, without softening it."],["She accepts the no.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."],["She asks what would change the answer.",["IE5","ER12"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."]]]]}},{"id":"IE13","title":"the ladder: how it is put, and how a no is met","marks":[["ℓ_ask","the ladder of ways to ask"],["ℓ_no","the ladder of ways to refuse"],["⪯","is no stronger than"]],"practice":[],"u":"Each rung says two things. Why does going up a rung never soften either one?","tree":{"t":"Sarah is asked to join a fourth extracurricular during exam term. What does she do?","o":[["She says no firmly, high on the ladder, and offers to help next term.",["IE13"],4,"Going up a rung never softens the no. Nobody else will protect her exams for her."],["She sees that saying no is not letting people down, and the guilt drops.",["IE13","IE3"],7,"That breaks a myth, and finding the myth beats feeling guilty."],["She says no with grace, and accepts their disappointment.",["IE13","DT13"],1,"She is doing the best she can. Their disappointment is theirs to carry."],["She says yes, and resents it all term.",["IE13","G4"],6,"Saying yes makes sense: she wants to be liked. The cost is a term of resentment, and her exams pay for it."]],"e":[["She says no firmly, and the organizer keeps pressing.",[["She says no again, the same way.",["IE13","IE5"],"The ladder lets the ask or the no be said again, at the same height or higher, without softening it."],["She ends the conversation politely.",["IE10","IE4"],"Self-respect and the relationship both kept."],["She notices the guilt rising and lets it pass.",["ER22","IE3"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["The guilt drops, but a friend in the club stops talking to her.",[["She asks her friend kindly if they can talk.",["IE7","IE5"],"GIVE keeps the relationship: gentle, interested in their side, validating, with an easy manner."],["She gives it time.",["DT13","IE17"],"She cannot observe the friend's reasons."],["She validates her own choice.",["IE24","IE13"],"Validating yourself, exactly as you would a friend, is how to recover when someone invalidates you."]]],["She says no with grace, and they ask her to do \"just one thing\".",[["She says no to that too, kindly.",["IE13"],"The ladder lets the ask or the no be said again, at the same height or higher, without softening it."],["She weighs it: is one small thing worth it?",["IE4","IE11"],"Weighing the three aims again (what you want, the relationship, your self-respect) tells which to put first now."],["She offers a time after exams.",["IE7","ER12"],"GIVE keeps the relationship: gentle, interested in their side, validating, with an easy manner."]]]]}},{"id":"IE14","title":"the third: self, others, agent","marks":[["Hₒ","the others: one person or any number"],["H","people"],["ag","the agent: the one who acts, perhaps on another’s behalf"],["𝒜","","r02d.html","2d · Membership and Same Members"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why does a third person change what an act can be? Why do the parts stay three however many others there are?","tree":{"t":"Terry asks his teacher for an extension on his friend's behalf. What does he do?","o":[["He names it as an ask for his friend, with his friend's consent, and checks the teacher's rule.",["IE14","IE5"],4,"The self, the others and the agent are three parts. He acts as agent: by whose rule, and on whose behalf."],["He sees he is the agent, not the one who owes the work, and stops feeling guilty.",["IE14"],7,"Telling the roles apart clears the confusion that was causing the guilt."],["He accepts that the teacher may want to hear from the friend directly.",["IE14","DT13"],1,"He is doing the best he can as agent."],["He gives up and lets the friend miss the deadline.",["IE14","G4"],6,"Giving up makes sense: it was not his problem. The cost is a friend let down when a word might have helped."]],"e":[["He asks for his friend, and the teacher says the friend must ask in person.",[["He tells his friend exactly what the teacher said.",["IE14","IE5"],"Telling the self, the others and the agent apart shows who can decide and by whose rule."],["He offers to go with his friend.",["IE16","IE7"],"Friendships grow from being near, being alike, talking, and joining in, so one more try widens the chance."],["He accepts the rule.",["DT13","IE14"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["He stops feeling guilty, but his friend blames him when it fails.",[["He describes what he did, plainly.",["M4","IE10"],"A plain description of what happened can't be argued with, and it keeps judgments out."],["He validates his friend's frustration without taking the blame.",["IE22"],"Validating what makes sense is not agreeing with all of it, and it calms things enough to go on."],["He lets the hurt be.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["He accepts it, but his friend is too anxious to go.",[["He helps his friend write a DEAR MAN email instead.",["IE5","IE8"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["He validates the anxiety.",["IE22","IE23"],"Validating what makes sense is not agreeing with all of it, and it calms things enough to go on."],["He lets it be his friend's choice.",["IE14","DT13"],"Telling the self, the others and the agent apart shows who can decide and by whose rule."]]]]}},{"id":"IE15","title":"troubleshooting: the first check that fails","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why check the six obstacles in order, and stop at the first that fails?","tree":{"t":"Claire's request to switch seminar groups failed. What does she do?","o":[["She walks the six obstacles in order, finds the request was unclear, and asks again clearly.",["IE15","IE5"],4,"Stop at the first that fails, and fix it."],["She stops reading the refusal as personal.",["IE15","IE17"],7,"Finding the obstacle beats blaming herself or the office."],["She accepts her group for this term.",["IE15","DT13"],1,"She is doing the best she can. A term is survivable."],["She gives up and skips seminars.",["IE15","G4"],6,"Skipping makes sense: the group feels wrong. The cost is the seminar mark."]],"e":[["She asks again clearly, and the office says the groups are full.",[["She asks to be put on a waiting list.",["IE5","ER12"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She walks the obstacles again: is someone else able to swap?",["IE15"],"Troubleshooting walks the obstacles in order and fixes the first that fails."],["She accepts it for this term.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["She stops reading it as personal, but the office was rude.",[["She gives the benefit of the doubt.",["IE17"],"No one can observe another's thoughts, so a guess about them is held loosely and given the benefit of the doubt."],["She notes it, and lets it pass.",["DT19","ER22"],"A thought is a sensation of the brain, not a fact and not you. It can be watched as it comes and goes."],["She asks to speak to someone else there.",["IE14","IE5"],"Telling the self, the others and the agent apart shows who can decide and by whose rule."]]],["She accepts her group, but a member there makes fun of her.",[["She says plainly that it isn't funny to her.",["IE10","IE5"],"FAST keeps self-respect: fair, no needless apologies, sticking to values, truthful."],["She sits with someone else.",["IE16"],"Friendships grow from being near, being alike, talking, and joining in, so one more try widens the chance."],["She tries the switch again next term.",["IE15","ER18"],"Troubleshooting walks the obstacles in order and fixes the first that fails."]]]]}},{"id":"IE16","title":"finding people: near, alike, talking, liking, joining","marks":[["≳","is at least about as large as"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why disclose about as much as the other does? Why is praise for everyone worth nothing?","tree":{"t":"Jean has just started university and knows no one. What does she do?","o":[["She joins a society that meets weekly, and talks to the same two people each time.",["IE16"],4,"We like those we are often near. She did not cause the loneliness, but she has to solve it."],["She reminds herself that everyone is lovable to someone.",["IE16"],2,"She wants friends, and that belief keeps her trying."],["She accepts that the first weeks are lonely, and keeps showing up.",["IE16","DT13"],1,"She is doing the best she can. Loneliness at the start is normal."],["She stays in her room.",["IE16","G4"],6,"Staying in makes sense: meeting people is tiring. The cost is that the loneliness grows."]],"e":[["She joins the society, but the two people she talks to already have their own friends.",[["She keeps going, and talks to two others.",["IE16"],"Friendships grow from being near, being alike, talking, and joining in, so one more try widens the chance."],["She joins one more thing that meets weekly.",["IE16","ER12"],"Friendships grow from being near, being alike, talking, and joining in, so one more try widens the chance."],["She lets the loneliness be there while she keeps showing up.",["ER22","DT13"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["She reminds herself she is lovable, but a weekend passes with no plans.",[["She invites someone for coffee.",["IE5","IE16"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She plans one pleasant thing alone.",["ER16"],"Small pleasant events add up when there are many of them, varied, over time."],["She lets the sadness rise and fall.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["She keeps showing up, and is still not invited to things.",[["She invites them instead.",["IE16","IE5"],"Friendships grow from being near, being alike, talking, and joining in, so one more try widens the chance."],["She weakens \"never\" to \"not yet\".",["IE20","IE21"],"Weakening \"always\" to \"sometimes\" fits the facts and leaves room to act."],["She accepts it takes months.",["DT13","DT16"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]]]}},{"id":"IE17","title":"mindfulness of others: what cannot be observed cannot be described","marks":[["⊭","","r38d.html","38d · What Is True Everywhere Is Provable"]],"practice":[],"u":"No one has observed another's thoughts. What do you do with a guess about them, and why give the benefit of the doubt all the way?","tree":{"t":"Randy is sure his tutor dislikes him. What does he do?","o":[["He treats it as a hypothesis, asks for feedback in person, and watches what happens.",["IE17","ER7"],7,"No one has observed another's motives. Testing the guess beats judging the tutor."],["He gives the benefit of the doubt, all the way, and the worry eases.",["IE17"],1,"Assuming his tutor is doing their best changes his own response."],["He accepts that he cannot know what the tutor thinks, and does the work.",["IE17","DT13"],6,"Not knowing has a cause: thoughts can't be observed. He can live with that and still do well."],["He decides it is true, and avoids the tutor.",["IE17","G4"],6,"Avoiding makes sense if the guess were true. But treating a guess as a fact costs him the help he needs."]],"e":[["He asks for feedback, and the tutor is short with him.",[["He treats it as one observation, and asks again next week.",["IE17","G15"],"No one can observe another's thoughts, so a guess about them is held loosely and given the benefit of the doubt."],["He gives the benefit of the doubt.",["IE17"],"No one can observe another's thoughts, so a guess about them is held loosely and given the benefit of the doubt."],["He asks a classmate how the tutor is with them.",["IE16","ER7"],"Friendships grow from being near, being alike, talking, and joining in, so one more try widens the chance."]]],["He gives the benefit of the doubt, but the tutor marks him lower than a friend with a similar essay.",[["He asks the tutor to explain the difference.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["He compares the essays plainly, without judging.",["M4","M5"],"A plain description of what happened can't be argued with, and it keeps judgments out."],["He lets the doubt be.",["ER22","DT13"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["He does the work, and the tutor praises it.",[["He notes it as data against his guess.",["IE17","G15"],"No one can observe another's thoughts, so a guess about them is held loosely and given the benefit of the doubt."],["He thanks the tutor.",["IE7"],"GIVE keeps the relationship: gentle, interested in their side, validating, with an easy manner."],["He lets himself enjoy it.",["ER16","M2"],"Small pleasant events add up when there are many of them, varied, over time."]]]]}},{"id":"IE18","title":"ending a relationship: from wise mind, safely","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why decide in wise mind and never in emotion mind? Why negotiate how it ends, but not whether?","tree":{"t":"Evita's study partner copies her work and leaves her to take the blame. What does she do?","o":[["She decides in wise mind to end the partnership, plans what she will say, and tells the course leader.",["IE18","IE5"],4,"The tie is harming her. Plan the ending, be direct, and put safety first."],["She tells justified guilt from unjustified guilt, and lets go of the unjustified kind.",["IE18","ER11"],7,"That sorts the causes and drops the blame she does not owe."],["She stays in the partnership for now, and makes room for herself within it.",["IE18"],1,"Staying is allowed when leaving costs more. She is doing the best she can for now."],["She keeps covering for the partner.",["IE18","G4"],6,"Covering makes sense: it avoids a fight. The cost is a risk to her own record that grows."]],"e":[["She ends the partnership, and the partner spreads a story about her.",[["She tells the course leader the facts, with dates.",["IE5","IE18"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She lets the hurt rise and fall.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."],["She tells her friends the truth briefly, and leaves it.",["IE10"],"FAST keeps self-respect: fair, no needless apologies, sticking to values, truthful."]]],["She lets go of unjustified guilt, but the partner apologizes and asks to work together again.",[["She decides in wise mind, not in the moment.",["IE18","M3"],"A tie that harms is decided on from wise mind, with safety first and a plan for how it ends."],["She sets clear terms if she says yes.",["IE5","IE12"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She says no kindly.",["IE13","IE7"],"The ladder lets the ask or the no be said again, at the same height or higher, without softening it."]]],["She stays for now, and the partner copies again.",[["She ends it now, with a plan.",["IE18"],"A tie that harms is decided on from wise mind, with safety first and a plan for how it ends."],["She tells the course leader.",["IE5","IE14"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She protects her work: separate files, dated drafts.",["ER12","DT24"],"Problem solving is a loop: when a solution fails or a new problem comes, go back to the start."]]]]}},{"id":"IE19","title":"dialectics: four claims about the world","marks":[["⇝","a path of links"],["Φ","the rule that takes one round of a transaction to the next"],["∋","","r03f.html","3f · Why Infinity Is Chosen, Not Discovered"]],"practice":[],"u":"How can opposites both be true? What does it mean that change is transactional?","tree":{"t":"Mike thinks he is either a maths person or not. What does he do?","o":[["He tracks his marks over a term to see change.",["IE19","G15"],3,"Change is the only constant. He needs to try harder, and to see that it shows."],["He holds both: \"maths is hard for me, and I am getting better\".",["IE19"],7,"Opposites can both be true, and holding both beats judging himself."],["He accepts where he is now as one point on a line.",["IE19","DT13"],1,"He is doing the best he can. Now is not forever."],["He decides he is not a maths person, and drops the module.",["IE19","G4"],6,"It makes sense: either-or is simpler. The cost is a module he could have passed."]],"e":[["He tracks his marks, and they drop for two weeks.",[["He keeps tracking: change goes both ways.",["IE19","G15"],"Opposites can both be true, and things keep changing, so one point is not the whole line."],["He finds the cause: a harder topic.",["G13","ER7"],"A chain finds the link where a skill would have broken it, and that is where the next try aims."],["He asks for help with that topic.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."]]],["He holds both, but a classmate says some people just aren't maths people.",[["He keeps both truths.",["IE19","IE20"],"Opposites can both be true, and things keep changing, so one point is not the whole line."],["He lets the comment pass.",["DT19"],"A thought is a sensation of the brain, not a fact and not you. It can be watched as it comes and goes."],["He shows the classmate his progress chart, lightly.",["IE7","M4"],"GIVE keeps the relationship: gentle, interested in their side, validating, with an easy manner."]]],["He accepts where he is, and fails a quiz.",[["He accepts this point too, and plans the next.",["DT13","ER12"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."],["He checks the facts: one quiz.",["ER7","IE21"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."],["He rides the wave of disappointment.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]]]}},{"id":"IE20","title":"acting dialectically: weaken the quantifier, keep both sides","marks":[["⊻","either one or the other, not both"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why weaken \"always\" to \"sometimes\"? Why does a synthesis keep both sides whole instead of blending them?","tree":{"t":"Zoe says \"I always freeze in exams\". What does she do?","o":[["She weakens it to \"sometimes\", and plans for the times she doesn't freeze.",["IE20","ER18"],3,"Weakening the quantifier makes it something she can work on."],["She finds what is true on the other side: she has finished exams before.",["IE20"],7,"Both sides held, and the panic drops."],["She accepts that she may freeze, and has a plan for it.",["IE20","DT13"],1,"She is doing the best she can. A plan makes the freeze survivable."],["She keeps saying \"always\".",["IE20","G4"],6,"\"Always\" makes sense: the freezes are what she remembers. The cost is that it keeps the freeze coming."]],"e":[["She plans for not freezing, and freezes on question one.",[["She uses her plan: skip to the question she knows.",["IE20","DT11"],"\"Sometimes\" means a plan for the times she does."],["She does paced breathing.",["DT6"],"TIP changes the body to change the emotion fast: cold water, hard exercise, paced breathing, paired relaxation."],["She lets the freeze be, and waits for it to pass.",["ER22","DT13"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["She finds the other side, but her parent says \"you always panic\".",[["She weakens it again: \"sometimes\".",["IE20"],"Weakening \"always\" to \"sometimes\" fits the facts and leaves room to act."],["She validates herself.",["IE24"],"Validating yourself, exactly as you would a friend, is how to recover when someone invalidates you."],["She tells the parent what helps her.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."]]],["She has a plan for freezing, but it happens in an oral exam where she can't skip.",[["She says \"may I take a moment?\" and breathes.",["IE5","DT6"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She half-smiles and opens her hands.",["DT18"],"Half-smile and willing hands: the body tells the brain, and no one else need see it."],["She accepts the pause and answers what she can.",["DT13","DT11"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]]]}},{"id":"IE21","title":"nondialectical thinking: eight claims stronger than the facts","marks":[["≫","","r13chem.html","13·chem · How Fast Is a Chemical Change Going Right Now?"]],"practice":[],"u":"Each nondialectical pattern claims more than the facts allow. Take one and weaken it until it fits.","tree":{"t":"After a bad mark, a student thinks \"I'll never pass anything\". What does he do?","o":[["He weakens it to \"I failed this one; here is what I can change\".",["IE21","ER12"],3,"A pattern that claims more than the facts allow is weakened until it fits, and then it can be worked on."],["He checks the facts: he has passed other modules.",["IE21","ER7"],7,"The facts lower the emotion."],["He accepts the bad mark as a fact, and leaves \"never\" as a thought.",["IE21","DT19"],1,"He is doing the best he can. A thought is not a fact."],["He believes it and stops studying.",["IE21","G4"],6,"Believing it makes sense the day the mark comes back. The cost is that \"never\" becomes true by default."]],"e":[["He weakens it, but the next mark is bad too.",[["He weakens it again: \"I failed two; here is what I can change\".",["IE21","ER12"],"The claim still claims more than the facts."],["He asks for feedback on both.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["He lets the disappointment be.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["He checks the facts, but the feeling of \"never\" stays.",[["He notes it as a thought, not a fact.",["DT19","IE21"],"A thought is a sensation of the brain, not a fact and not you. It can be watched as it comes and goes."],["He does one study session anyway.",["ER10"],"Opposite action works only all the way, and repeated until the emotion changes."],["He talks to someone who knows his record.",["IE16","IE22"],"Friendships grow from being near, being alike, talking, and joining in, so one more try widens the chance."]]],["He accepts the bad mark, but his scholarship needs a higher average.",[["He asks the scholarship office what the rules are.",["IE5","ER7"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["He plans the average he needs, module by module.",["ER12","ER18"],"Problem solving is a loop: when a solution fails or a new problem comes, go back to the start."],["He accepts the risk, and works.",["DT13","DT16"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]]]}},{"id":"IE22","title":"validation: understandable for every response, valid for some","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Every response makes sense given its causes, but only some fit the facts. Why validate only the valid, and why is validating not agreeing?","tree":{"t":"A friend is furious about a fair grade. What do you do?","o":[["You validate the frustration, and help her plan the next essay.",["IE22"],3,"Validate the valid. Her frustration fits; the claim that the grade was unfair does not."],["You say how her anger makes sense given how hard she worked.",["IE22"],6,"Every response is understandable, given its causes."],["You let her vent without agreeing that the grade was wrong.",["IE22"],1,"Validating is not agreeing. You are both doing the best you can."],["You agree the grade was unfair to keep the peace.",["IE22","G4"],6,"Agreeing makes sense: it calms her down. But validating the invalid reinforces it."]],"e":[["You validate her frustration, and she says \"so you agree it's unfair\".",[["You say kindly, \"I get why you're upset, and I think the mark is fair\".",["IE22","IE10"],"Validating what makes sense is not agreeing with all of it, and it calms things enough to go on."],["You ask what she wants to do next.",["IE23","IE5"],"Validation runs from paying attention, to saying back, to acting on what is valid."],["You let her be upset with you for a bit.",["DT13","IE7"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["You say her anger makes sense, and she gets angrier.",[["You stop talking and listen.",["IE23"],"Validation runs from paying attention, to saying back, to acting on what is valid."],["You reflect back exactly what she said.",["IE23","IE22"],"Validation runs from paying attention, to saying back, to acting on what is valid."],["You suggest coffee later.",["IE7","DT4"],"GIVE keeps the relationship: gentle, interested in their side, validating, with an easy manner."]]],["You let her vent, and she wants you to complain to the tutor with her.",[["You say no kindly.",["IE13","IE10"],"The ladder lets the ask or the no be said again, at the same height or higher, without softening it."],["You offer to help her write her own email.",["IE5","IE8"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["You check the facts with her first.",["ER7","IE22"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."]]]]}},{"id":"IE23","title":"six levels of validation","marks":[["≅","","r20d.html","20d · All Three Keep Distances"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why can words alone invalidate at level five? Why is level four an insult where level five holds?","tree":{"t":"A classmate says she is overwhelmed by her coursework. What do you do?","o":[["You act on it: you offer to work on the problem set together.",["IE23"],3,"At level five, you act on what is valid. Words alone can fall short."],["You reflect back what she said, and let her correct you.",["IE23"],7,"Level two: understanding beats judging."],["You pay attention and listen without fixing.",["IE23"],1,"Level one is also validation."],["You tell her everyone is busy.",["IE23","G4"],6,"It makes sense: you are busy too. But it invalidates her, and she may not tell you next time."]],"e":[["You offer to work together, and she says no, she just wants to talk.",[["You listen.",["IE23"],"Validation runs from paying attention, to saying back, to acting on what is valid."],["You reflect back what she says.",["IE23","IE22"],"Validation runs from paying attention, to saying back, to acting on what is valid."],["You drop the plan, and accept her choice.",["DT13","IE7"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["You reflect back, and get it wrong.",[["You let her correct you, and try again.",["IE23"],"Validation runs from paying attention, to saying back, to acting on what is valid."],["You ask a question instead.",["IE23","IE7"],"Validation runs from paying attention, to saying back, to acting on what is valid."],["You don't judge yourself for missing.",["M5"],"Not judging, one thing at a time, doing what works: the how skills keep the next step clear."]]],["You listen, and she starts to cry.",[["You stay, and say \"this is a lot\".",["IE23","IE22"],"Validation runs from paying attention, to saying back, to acting on what is valid."],["You offer a tissue and some water.",["IE23","IE7"],"Validation runs from paying attention, to saying back, to acting on what is valid."],["You let the silence be.",["ER22","DT13"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]]]}},{"id":"IE24","title":"recovering from invalidation","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"When does invalidation help? Why validate yourself exactly as you would someone else?","tree":{"t":"Terry's teacher called his project lazy in front of the class. What does he do?","o":[["He asks the teacher privately which parts fell short, and fixes them.",["IE24","IE5"],4,"Invalidation helps when it corrects a real mistake. He did not choose the public way it was said, but the part that was true is his to fix."],["He validates himself, exactly as he would a friend.",["IE24"],7,"All behaviour is caused. He has been doing the best he could."],["He accepts that it happened and talks about it where he is supported.",["IE24","DT13"],1,"Radical acceptance of himself, and of the teacher too."],["He decides he is lazy, and stops trying.",["IE24","G4"],6,"It makes sense: the word was said in front of everyone. The cost is a choice built on someone else's word."]],"e":[["He asks privately, and the teacher says it again, more harshly.",[["He thanks the teacher for the specific parts, and leaves.",["IE24","IE10"],"Validating yourself, exactly as you would a friend, is how to recover when someone invalidates you."],["He validates himself.",["IE24"],"Validating yourself, exactly as you would a friend, is how to recover when someone invalidates you."],["He tells someone he trusts.",["IE16","IE22"],"Friendships grow from being near, being alike, talking, and joining in, so one more try widens the chance."]]],["He validates himself, but his marks really are low.",[["He fixes the parts that were true.",["IE24","ER12"],"Validating yourself, exactly as you would a friend, is how to recover when someone invalidates you."],["He asks for help with one part.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["He accepts both: he is doing his best, and he needs to do better.",["G6","DT13"],"Doing the best you can and needing to do better are both true, so trying again is not a failure."]]],["He accepts it, but other students start calling him lazy too.",[["He tells them plainly to stop.",["IE10","IE5"],"FAST keeps self-respect: fair, no needless apologies, sticking to values, truthful."],["He lets it pass.",["DT19"],"A thought is a sensation of the brain, not a fact and not you. It can be watched as it comes and goes."],["He asks the teacher for a word about it.",["IE14","IE5"],"Telling the self, the others and the agent apart shows who can decide and by whose rule."]]]]}},{"id":"IE25","title":"raising a behaviour: reinforce, shape, time it","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why is behaviour rewarded only some of the time very hard to stop? Why does a reward not show the behaviour was done to get it?","tree":{"t":"Claire wants her younger brother to do his homework without a fight. What does she do?","o":[["She shapes it: praise for sitting down, then for ten minutes, then for the whole sheet.",["IE25"],5,"Reward small steps, right away. The behaviour is learned where it is needed."],["She sees that his stalling is caused, not spite, and her anger drops.",["IE25","G6"],6,"That changes her own response."],["She accepts that it will take weeks.",["IE25","DT13"],1,"She is doing the best she can, and so is he."],["She shouts at him every night.",["IE25","G4"],6,"Shouting makes sense: it sometimes works. But it teaches avoidance, and the fights get longer."]],"e":[["She praises him for sitting down, and he sits but doesn't start.",[["She praises the next small step: opening the book.",["IE25"],"Rewarding small steps right away shapes a behaviour where it is needed."],["She waits a minute, then tries again.",["DT13","IE8"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."],["She asks what makes it hard.",["IE23","IE5"],"Validation runs from paying attention, to saying back, to acting on what is valid."]]],["Her anger drops, but he shouts at her when she reminds him.",[["She stays calm and repeats the ask once.",["IE5","IE13"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She lets him calm down and tries later.",["DT4","IE26"],"STOP: stop, step back, observe, then proceed mindfully. Stopping gives a skill time to work."],["She lets her own anger rise and fall.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["She accepts it will take weeks, and their parent undoes it by doing his homework for him.",[["She talks to the parent about the plan.",["IE5","IE14"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She keeps shaping when it is her turn.",["IE25","DT16"],"Rewarding small steps right away shapes a behaviour where it is needed."],["She accepts what she can't control.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]]]}},{"id":"IE26","title":"lowering a behaviour: extinguish, satiate, punish with care","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why does extinction bring a burst before the fall? Why must the alternative always be rewarded?","tree":{"t":"Jean's housemate plays music loudly the night before exams. What does she do?","o":[["She asks once, then stops rewarding the noise with attention, and thanks him when it is quiet.",["IE26","IE5"],4,"Extinction with the alternative rewarded. Expect a burst first."],["She sees the burst as a sign that it is working.",["IE26"],7,"Knowing the cause keeps her steady."],["She uses earplugs this week, and accepts the noise.",["IE26","DT13"],1,"She is doing the best she can the week before exams."],["She gives in after a few refusals.",["IE26","G4"],6,"Giving in makes sense: she is tired of the fight. But it rewards the noise some of the time, and that is the hardest kind to stop."]],"e":[["She stops rewarding the noise, and it gets louder.",[["She holds steady: the burst comes before the fall.",["IE26"],"When a behaviour stops paying it bursts before it falls, so holding steady through the burst is the skill."],["She uses earplugs through the burst.",["ER12","DT13"],"Problem solving is a loop: when a solution fails or a new problem comes, go back to the start."],["She thanks him the first quiet minute.",["IE25","IE26"],"Rewarding small steps right away shapes a behaviour where it is needed."]]],["She sees the burst as working, but it lasts a week.",[["She keeps going.",["IE26","DT16"],"When a behaviour stops paying it bursts before it falls, so holding steady through the burst is the skill."],["She asks once more, clearly.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She studies in the library this week.",["ER12"],"Problem solving is a loop: when a solution fails or a new problem comes, go back to the start."]]],["She uses earplugs, but can still hear it.",[["She asks him to turn it down after 10.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She moves to another room.",["ER12"],"Problem solving is a loop: when a solution fails or a new problem comes, go back to the start."],["She accepts it for tonight.",["DT13","DT11"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]]]}},{"id":"IE27","title":"the consequence is relative","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why is a consequence relative? Why do natural consequences work best?","tree":{"t":"Gold stars move one student and embarrass another. What does the teacher do?","o":[["She asks each student what they would work for, and uses that.",["IE27"],4,"A consequence is relative. Ask, or watch what works."],["She stops blaming the second student for not caring.",["IE27"],7,"One person's poison is another's passion."],["She accepts that no reward works for everyone.",["IE27","DT13"],1,"She is doing the best she can."],["She keeps the stars for everyone.",["IE27","G4"],6,"Keeping them makes sense: it is simple and fair on paper. The cost is one student embarrassed every week."]],"e":[["She asks each student, and one says \"nothing\".",[["She watches what that student does choose, and uses that.",["IE27"],"What counts as a reward depends on the person, so ask or watch what works."],["She validates that school feels pointless to them.",["IE22"],"Validating what makes sense is not agreeing with all of it, and it calms things enough to go on."],["She tries a natural consequence.",["IE27","IE25"],"What counts as a reward depends on the person, so ask or watch what works."]]],["She stops blaming, but the embarrassed student still won't work.",[["She asks privately what would help.",["IE5","IE23"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She uses a private reward instead.",["IE27"],"What counts as a reward depends on the person, so ask or watch what works."],["She accepts it may take time.",["DT13","IE8"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["She accepts no reward works for all, and the head teacher insists on stars.",[["She asks to use stars privately for some students.",["IE5","IE4"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She follows the rule, and adds private praise.",["IE14","IE25"],"Telling the self, the others and the agent apart shows who can decide and by whose rule."],["She accepts the rule.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]]]}}]},{"id":"ER","page":"dbter","name":"regulating emotion","footings":25,"steps":[{"id":"ER1","title":"goals: name it, fewer unwanted, less vulnerable, less suffering","marks":[["🕳","vulnerability"],["ŝ","an emotion as a signal to oneself"]],"practice":["pros and cons of changing e ,  and of not changing it :\nchecked true or false :   𝔢 serves ☉ ;   ¬reg makes a new problem\nchecked true or false :   e ↓ now  ⇒  freedom ↑ ;   clinging(e) useful ;   cost(reg) too high"],"u":"How is regulating like a thermostat? Why is the aim less suffering, and not no emotion?","tree":{"t":"Sarah's nerves before a music audition are high, but useful. What does she do?","o":[["She uses the energy: one more run-through of the hard passage, then warm-ups.",["ER1","ER2"],3,"An emotion that works for you need not be lowered. She puts it to work, and the extra effort goes where it counts."],["She turns the nerves down a notch with slow breathing, like a thermostat, until they help and no longer shake her.",["ER1","DT6"],6,"Regulating can lower an emotion as well as raise it. Breath is one of the nerves' causes she can reach."],["She lets the nerves be there, and goes on stage with them.",["ER1","ER22"],1,"The aim is less suffering, not no emotion. She is doing the best she can, and nerves can play alongside her."],["She convinces herself she will fail, and withdraws.",["ER1","G4"],6,"Withdrawing makes sense: the nerves feel like a warning. The cost is the audition, and next time the nerves feel more like proof."]],"e":[["She uses the energy for a run-through, and her hands start shaking.",[["She turns it down a notch: out-breath longer than in-breath.",["ER1","DT6"],"Regulating works both ways, like a thermostat: up when it helps, down when it gets in the way."],["She tenses and releases her hands on \"relax\".",["DT7"],"Paired relaxation, used where it is needed."],["She lets the shaking be, and plays anyway.",["ER22","DT13"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["She turns the nerves down, and now feels flat and slow.",[["She turns them back up a little: a brisk walk before she goes in.",["ER1","DT6"],"Regulating works like a thermostat: an emotion can be turned up when it helps and down when it gets in the way."],["She notices the flatness without judging it.",["M4","M5"],"A plain description of what happened can't be argued with, and it keeps judgments out."],["She rehearses the opening in her mind, from inside.",["ER18"],"Mastery is built one hard-but-possible step at a time, and coping ahead means rehearsing the hard scene first."]]],["She goes on with the nerves, and fluffs the first note.",[["She keeps playing, one bar at a time.",["DT11","M5"],"IMPROVE the moment: one thing at a time, meaning, a short break. Only this moment has to be survived."],["She lets the jolt rise and fall while she plays.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."],["She half-smiles at the next rest.",["DT18"],"Half-smile and willing hands: the body tells the brain, and no one else need see it."]]]]}},{"id":"ER2","title":"what an emotion does: moves you, tells others, tells you","marks":[],"practice":["for one e :   what it moved ☉ to do ,   the message it sent ,   its effect on h ,   what it told ☉ ,   the facts that would check it ,   the facts checked"],"u":"An emotion tells you something. Why is that a reason to go and check, and not a fact about the world?","tree":{"t":"Mike feels dread as he opens his exam results. What does he do?","o":[["He reads the results and checks the facts: which modules he passed, and what the resit rules are.",["ER2","ER7"],4,"An emotion tells you to go and check. Whatever caused the results, what they show is his to solve."],["He reminds himself the dread is a message about how much he cares, not a fact about the results, and it eases.",["ER2","M4"],6,"The dread is caused by caring and by not knowing yet. Seeing that loosens its hold."],["He opens them with the dread still there.",["ER2","ER22"],1,"He is doing the best he can. Dread and opening the email can happen together."],["He leaves them unopened for a week.",["ER2","G4"],6,"Not looking makes sense: for now there is still hope. But avoidance keeps the dread going, and the resit deadline keeps running."]],"e":[["He checks the facts, and he has failed one module.",[["He reads the resit rules and books the resit.",["ER2","ER12"],"The emotion told him to check, and the check found a problem to solve."],["He checks the size: one module, with a resit.",["ER7"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."],["He lets the disappointment come.",["ER22","DT13"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["The dread eases, but his parents call to ask how he did.",[["He tells them plainly, and what he will do next.",["IE10","IE5"],"FAST keeps self-respect: fair, no needless apologies, sticking to values, truthful."],["He asks for a day before he talks about it.",["IE5","DT4"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["He lets the shame rise and fall during the call.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["He opens them with the dread, and the page won't load.",[["He tries again in five minutes, and does something else meanwhile.",["DT9"],"Distraction (wise mind ACCEPTS) gets you through a crisis, used once, with a plan to come back."],["He breathes out longer than in.",["DT6"],"TIP changes the body to change the emotion fast: cold water, hard exercise, paced breathing, paired relaxation."],["He notices the dread climbing and names it.",["ER6","ER2"],"Naming an emotion tells him what it is saying."]]]]}},{"id":"ER3","title":"what makes it hard","marks":[["θ*","the skills breakdown point"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why does rumination keep an emotion firing? Why does worry work as an escape?","tree":{"t":"Zoe keeps replaying a presentation she fumbled. What does she do?","o":[["She writes down what to change next time, then closes the notebook.",["ER3","ER12"],3,"Rumination acts as if there is an answer still to find. Writing it down gives the answer somewhere to go, and asks her to do better next time, not to replay this one."],["She sees the replay as rumination that keeps the feeling firing, and steps out of it.",["ER3","M1"],6,"The replay is what keeps the shame firing. Seeing that gives her a way out."],["She lets the memory come up and pass without replaying it.",["ER3","DT19"],1,"She is doing the best she can. A memory can visit without being rerun."],["She replays it all night.",["ER3","G4"],6,"Replaying makes sense: it feels like learning. The cost is a night's sleep and shame kept high."]],"e":[["He writes what to change, but the replay starts again at night.",[["He reminds himself the answer is written down, and lies back.",["ER3","ER21"],"Rumination keeps an emotion firing. The notebook holds the answer now."],["He uses paired relaxation in bed.",["DT7"],"Paired relaxation links release to one word, practised first in quiet places, then used where it is needed."],["He aims for rest, not sleep.",["ER21"],"In bed, a solvable worry gets a two-line plan; otherwise the aim is rest, not sleep."]]],["She steps out of the replay, but a classmate brings up the presentation the next day.",[["She describes it plainly and moves on.",["M4","IE10"],"A plain description of what happened can't be argued with, and it keeps judgments out."],["She notices the replay starting and steps out again.",["ER3","DT16"],"Rumination keeps an emotion firing; giving the problem a place lets it rest."],["She lets the embarrassment rise and fall.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["She lets the memory pass, but it brings shame with it.",[["She checks whether shame fits: was it against her values?",["ER7","ER8"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."],["If it doesn't fit, she acts opposite: she volunteers for the next presentation.",["ER10","ER11"],"Opposite action for shame that doesn't fit."],["She rides the wave.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]]]}},{"id":"ER4","title":"myths about emotion: their forms, and the one case that breaks them","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Each myth is a claim about every emotion, situation or person. Why is \"sometimes\" often enough to answer it?","tree":{"t":"Jean believes \"if I feel anxious before an exam, I am not prepared\". What does she do?","o":[["She recalls one exam she was anxious about and did well in, and prepares as planned.",["ER4","IE3"],5,"One real case breaks the myth. She learns again, in this exam season, that anxiety and preparation can go together."],["She answers it: \"sometimes anxiety just means I care\", and the panic drops.",["ER4"],7,"The weaker claim is often enough to answer a myth. Looking at the myth beats blaming herself for the anxiety."],["She lets the anxiety be there, and keeps studying.",["ER4","ER22"],1,"She is doing the best she can. Anxiety need not be gone for the studying to work."],["She believes the myth and panics.",["ER4","G4"],6,"Believing it makes sense: the anxiety feels like evidence. The cost is study time spent panicking instead."]],"e":[["She recalls a good exam, but this one feels different.",[["She looks for one more case: any exam where nerves and preparation went together.",["ER4","IE3"],"One real case breaks a myth; two make it plainer."],["She checks the facts about her preparation.",["ER7"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."],["She prepares as planned, nerves and all.",["ER10","ER22"],"Opposite action works only all the way, and repeated until the emotion changes."]]],["She answers the myth, but her friend says \"if you're nervous, you're not ready\".",[["She answers that one too: \"sometimes\".",["ER4","IE20"],"Weakening \"always\" to \"sometimes\" fits the facts and leaves room to act."],["She lets the comment pass.",["DT19"],"A thought is a sensation of the brain, not a fact and not you. It can be watched as it comes and goes."],["She validates herself.",["IE24"],"Validating yourself, exactly as you would a friend, is how to recover when someone invalidates you."]]],["She keeps studying with the anxiety, and it grows until she can't read.",[["She takes five minutes of paced breathing.",["DT6"],"TIP changes the body to change the emotion fast: cold water, hard exercise, paced breathing, paired relaxation."],["She checks: is she past her breakdown point?",["ER23"],"Past the breakdown point only crisis skills work; they bring you back below it, and then the rest work again."],["She switches to a lighter task for an hour.",["DT9","ER12"],"Distraction (wise mind ACCEPTS) gets you through a crisis, used once, with a plan to come back."]]]]}},{"id":"ER5","title":"a model of an emotion: a path in 𝐗, with a loop back","marks":[["β","emotion to body"],["🔦","attention"],["⚡","a prompting event"],["🧠","brain changes"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why can a change at any part of the model change the whole emotion? Why does suppressing an expression make the emotion stronger, when changing it does not?","tree":{"t":"Randy reads a red-penned essay and feels shame. What does he do?","o":[["He books a feedback meeting and makes a revision plan from the comments.",["ER5","ER12","IE5"],4,"A change at any part of the model changes the whole emotion. He did not write the comments, but what he does with them is his."],["He rereads the comments as \"here is how to improve\", not \"I'm stupid\".",["ER5","ER7"],6,"The reading is one part of the model, and it was causing the shame. Change the reading and the emotion changes."],["He lets the shame rise and fall without hiding the essay.",["ER5","ER22"],1,"He is doing the best he can. Hiding the expression would make the shame stronger."],["He throws the essay away and never looks at the comments.",["ER5","G4"],6,"Throwing it away makes sense: the shame stops for now. But the same reading waits for him at the next essay."]],"e":[["He books feedback, and the meeting is a week away. The shame grows while he waits.",[["He starts the revision plan now with the comments he understands.",["ER5","ER12"],"A change at any part of the model changes the emotion: acting on the aftereffects now."],["He rereads the comments as help.",["ER7"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."],["He rides each wave of shame.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["He rereads them as help, but one comment really is harsh.",[["He takes the useful part, and lets the tone go.",["IE24"],"Invalidation that corrects helps; the rest can be left."],["He describes the comment without adding to it.",["M4","ER5"],"A plain description of what happened can't be argued with, and it keeps judgments out."],["He asks the tutor what was meant.",["IE5","IE17"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."]]],["He lets the shame rise and fall, but a classmate sees the red pen.",[["He says, \"yes, lots to fix\", and leaves it.",["IE10","M4"],"FAST keeps self-respect: fair, no needless apologies, sticking to values, truthful."],["He keeps the essay out, not hidden.",["ER10","ER5"],"Hiding the expression makes shame stronger."],["He lets the wave peak and fall.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]]]}},{"id":"ER6","title":"describing emotions: one record for each","marks":[],"practice":["∀ ☼ :   pick one e :   the strongest ,  the longest ,  or the most painful\nrecord :   what it moved ☉ to do ,   what it told others ,   what it told ☉","S = e′   ⇒   do the record for e′ first ,  then for e   a secondary emotion is analysed after the one that set it off","|e| ∈ [0 , 100] ;   vulnerability :  the story up to the event ;   after :  memory ,  other emotions ,  body ,  acts"],"u":"How does shame differ from guilt, and jealousy from envy? Why sort which of two emotions came first only afterwards?","tree":{"t":"Claire feels sick when a classmate gets the scholarship she wanted. What does she do?","o":[["She names it envy, and uses it: she asks what made the winning application strong.",["ER6","ER13"],4,"Envy wants what another has, and here it points at a skill she can build for next year."],["She fills in the record for envy, and the feeling shrinks once it is named.",["ER6","M4"],6,"Describing an emotion lets her stand apart from it and still own it. Naming its cause takes some of its force."],["She lets the envy be, and congratulates the classmate.",["ER6","ER22","IE7"],1,"She is doing the best she can. Envy and kindness can share a moment."],["She avoids the classmate for the rest of the year.",["ER6","G4"],6,"Avoiding makes sense: every sight of the classmate stings. The cost is a friendship, and a year of steering round her."]],"e":[["She asks what made the application strong, and the answer is something she can't change: a family contact.",[["She names the envy again, and asks what else she can build.",["ER6","ER12"],"Envy points at what she wants; the loop looks for what she can reach."],["She checks whether the envy fits.",["ER8"],"An emotion can fit the facts in kind and still be the wrong size or last too long."],["She accepts that part.",["DT13","DT12"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["She names it envy, and then sees the classmate celebrating.",[["She lets the envy rise and fall.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."],["She sends a kind message.",["M7","ER10"],"Kindness moves outward by steps, and it can be acted before it is felt."],["She describes the record again: what the envy is telling her.",["ER6","ER2"],"Naming an emotion and what it is saying lets you stand apart from it and still own it."]]],["She congratulates the classmate, and the classmate says \"you'll get one next year, maybe\".",[["She gives the benefit of the doubt.",["IE17"],"No one can observe another's thoughts, so a guess about them is held loosely and given the benefit of the doubt."],["She lets the sting be.",["ER22","DT13"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."],["She plans next year's application.",["ER17","ER12"],"A value comes down to one step that can be taken now."]]]]}},{"id":"ER7","title":"check the facts: six questions","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why look for a hidden threat when the facts are checked and the emotion stays high? What does catastrophizing add to the facts?","tree":{"t":"Terry is sure his professor's \"see me after class\" means he is in trouble. What does he do?","o":[["He goes and asks, and finds out what the professor wanted.",["ER7","IE5"],4,"If the fit is unsure, check it. The facts are a short walk away."],["He runs the six questions, and finds the hidden threat is \"I'll be thrown out\". Naming it shrinks it.",["ER7"],6,"Painful emotions nearly always assume a threat. Once he names that cause, he can weigh it."],["He puts up with not knowing until after class.",["ER7","DT11"],1,"He is doing the best he can. The worry is uncomfortable, and it ends at five o'clock."],["He skips class to avoid the meeting.",["ER7","G4"],6,"Skipping makes sense: it puts off the bad news. The cost is that the catastrophe goes untested and grows."]],"e":[["He goes and asks, and the professor says \"we'll talk next week\".",[["He asks what it is about, briefly.",["IE5","ER7"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["He checks the six questions about the wait.",["ER7"],"The hidden threat may be the not-knowing."],["He lets the uncertainty be for a week.",["DT13","DT11"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["He names the threat, but it still feels likely.",[["He checks the probability: how often are students thrown out after a chat?",["ER7"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."],["He copes ahead for the worst case.",["ER18"],"Mastery is built one hard-but-possible step at a time, and coping ahead means rehearsing the hard scene first."],["He breathes out longer than in.",["DT6"],"TIP changes the body to change the emotion fast: cold water, hard exercise, paced breathing, paired relaxation."]]],["He puts up with not knowing, and the professor is absent from the next class.",[["He emails to ask when they can meet.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["He notices the catastrophe growing and checks it again.",["ER7","DT19"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."],["He keeps doing his work.",["DT9","ER18"],"Distraction (wise mind ACCEPTS) gets you through a crisis, used once, with a plan to come back."]]]]}},{"id":"ER8","title":"when an emotion fits the facts","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"How can envy fit the facts and still not work? Why can shame fit when nothing immoral was done?","tree":{"t":"Jean is angry that her group got a lower mark because of a teammate's late section. What does she do?","o":[["She raises it with the module leader, with the facts and the dates.",["ER8","IE5"],4,"Anger fits when a goal is blocked unfairly. She did not cause the lateness, but she can ask for it to be weighed."],["She checks the size: anger fits, but a week of rage does not, and she lets it come down to fit.",["ER8","ER7"],6,"The size and length must fit too. The extra rage was coming from her reading, not from the facts."],["She accepts the mark, and plans clearer roles for the next project.",["ER8","DT13"],1,"She is doing the best she can. Accepting this mark frees her to protect the next one."],["She stays furious all term.",["ER8","G4"],6,"Staying angry makes sense: it was unfair. The cost is a term of it, which takes more from her than the mark did."]],"e":[["She raises it, and the module leader says group marks are final.",[["She asks whether peer assessment can count next time.",["IE5","ER12"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She checks the size of her anger now: still fitting?",["ER8","ER7"],"Anger fits; its size and length must too."],["She accepts this mark.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["She lets the anger come down to size, but the teammate laughs it off.",[["She tells the teammate how it affected her, using DEAR MAN.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She lets the anger rise and fall.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."],["She limits contact with the teammate.",["IE18"],"A tie that harms is decided on from wise mind, with safety first and a plan for how it ends."]]],["She accepts the mark, and then learns the teammate got an extension she didn't know about.",[["She asks the module leader about the extension, with facts.",["IE5","ER8"],"Anger fits unfairness, and the facts can be raised."],["She checks the facts before acting.",["ER7"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."],["She accepts this too.",["DT13","DT16"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]]]}},{"id":"ER9","title":"opposite action or problem solving: two questions","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Two checked answers say what to do. What do you do when both are no, and in what order?","tree":{"t":"Evita dreads a viva she has prepared well for. What does she do?","o":[["The fear does not fit the facts, so she acts opposite: she books a mock viva and answers every question.",["ER9","ER10"],5,"When it does not fit, act opposite. Approaching is learned by doing, in a room like the real one."],["She checks the facts (her chapters are done, her supervisor is happy), and the dread drops.",["ER9","ER7"],6,"Checking the facts often comes first, because it is the easiest way. The dread was caused by an old reading."],["She lets the fear ride along into the viva.",["ER9","ER22"],1,"She is doing the best she can. Fear can come along without taking the wheel."],["She postpones the viva.",["ER9","G4"],6,"Postponing makes sense: the dread lifts at once. But avoidance keeps the fear, and it will be waiting next time."]],"e":[["She books the mock viva, and does badly in it.",[["She asks what went wrong and practises those questions.",["ER12","ER18"],"Problem solving is a loop: when a solution fails or a new problem comes, go back to the start."],["She does another mock.",["ER10","ER9"],"Opposite action is repeated until it changes."],["She checks the facts: a mock is a practice.",["ER7"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."]]],["She checks the facts, and then her supervisor says the examiner is strict.",[["She asks what the examiner usually asks.",["IE5","ER9"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She copes ahead.",["ER18"],"Mastery is built one hard-but-possible step at a time, and coping ahead means rehearsing the hard scene first."],["She lets the fear rise and fall.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["She lets the fear ride along, and in the viva her mind goes blank.",[["She asks for a moment and breathes.",["IE5","DT6"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She says what she knows, one point at a time.",["DT11","M5"],"IMPROVE the moment: one thing at a time, meaning, a short break. Only this moment has to be survived."],["She lets the blank pass.",["ER22","DT13"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]]]}},{"id":"ER10","title":"opposite action: all the way, until it changes","marks":[["🏷","words"]],"practice":["∃ a :  avoided because of e ,  listed   |e| before ,  |e| after","if it fails ,  checked :   e ⊭ F ;   ⊖ of my own u ;   all the way :  voice ,  eyes ,  posture ;   long enough to learn"],"u":"Why must opposite action go all the way, and why does halfway not work?","tree":{"t":"Mike avoids the maths help centre out of embarrassment. What does he do?","o":[["He goes, all the way: shoulders back, asks a full question, stays the whole hour.",["ER10","IE5"],5,"Opposite action all the way; halfway does not work. He learns it in the help centre itself."],["He keeps going each week until the embarrassment falls even a little.",["ER10"],2,"Repeat until the emotion changes. He wants to improve, and each visit lowers it."],["He goes once, feeling embarrassed, and lets it be.",["ER10","ER22"],1,"He is doing the best he can. Embarrassment can come along."],["He keeps avoiding it.",["ER10","G4"],6,"Avoiding makes sense: it spares him the blush. But avoidance feeds the embarrassment, and the maths gets harder."]],"e":[["He goes all the way, and the tutor there is impatient with him.",[["He stays the whole hour anyway.",["ER10"],"Opposite action works only all the way, and repeated until the emotion changes."],["He gives the benefit of the doubt.",["IE17"],"No one can observe another's thoughts, so a guess about them is held loosely and given the benefit of the doubt."],["He asks another tutor next time.",["ER12"],"Problem solving is a loop: when a solution fails or a new problem comes, go back to the start."]]],["He keeps going each week, but the embarrassment hasn't dropped after three weeks.",[["He troubleshoots: is he going all the way?",["ER24","ER10"],"When a skill didn't work, check in order and fix the first thing that failed."],["He keeps going.",["DT16","ER10"],"Acceptance is a turn of the mind made again at every fork; one turn does not make the next."],["He checks whether embarrassment fits.",["ER7","ER8"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."]]],["He goes once, and a classmate there sees him.",[["He says hello normally.",["ER10","IE7"],"Opposite action works only all the way, and repeated until the emotion changes."],["He lets the embarrassment rise and fall.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."],["He checks the facts: the classmate is there for help too.",["ER7"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."]]]]}},{"id":"ER11","title":"the opposite of each urge","marks":[["⚔","attack"],["🕊","peace; gentleness"],["🔨","destroy"],["🔒","control; guard"],["🔓","let go of control"],["🙈","hide"],["🙇","bow down; make up for"],["🧍","stand up straight"]],"practice":[],"u":"For shame and guilt, why does the opposite action depend on which of the two fits?","tree":{"t":"Zoe feels guilty for skipping a group meeting on purpose. What does she do?","o":[["Guilt fits, so she repairs it: she apologizes and takes on an extra task.",["ER11","ER13","IE10"],4,"When guilt fits, repair is the action. She caused this one, and repairing it is hers to do."],["She checks whether it fits (she broke a promise, so it does), and the guilt settles to the right size.",["ER11","ER8"],6,"Which action helps depends on which emotion fits. Knowing it fits stops the guilt spreading into shame."],["She accepts the guilt, and lets it guide her next week.",["ER11","DT13"],1,"She is doing the best she can. Guilt that fits is useful to carry for a while."],["She avoids the group to escape the guilt.",["ER11","G4"],6,"Avoiding makes sense: facing them feels worse. But hiding keeps the guilt, and the group loses trust in her."]],"e":[["She apologizes, and the group stays cool with her.",[["She keeps the repair going: she does her task well and early.",["ER11","IE9"],"Repair takes time; reliability rebuilds a tie."],["She accepts that trust takes time.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."],["She gives the benefit of the doubt.",["IE17"],"No one can observe another's thoughts, so a guess about them is held loosely and given the benefit of the doubt."]]],["The guilt settles, but she starts to feel shame about who she is.",[["She checks: shame fits values; guilt fits acts.",["ER6","ER11"],"Naming an emotion and what it is saying lets you stand apart from it and still own it."],["She validates herself: one skipped meeting is one act.",["IE24","IE21"],"Validating yourself, exactly as you would a friend, is how to recover when someone invalidates you."],["She rides the wave.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["She accepts the guilt, and it doesn't fade after a week.",[["She checks whether repair is complete.",["ER11","ER13"],"Guilt that fits is answered by repair; shame that doesn't fit is answered by not hiding."],["She asks a group member if things are okay.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She lets it be.",["ER22","DT13"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]]]}},{"id":"ER12","title":"problem solving: a loop with two returns","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why is the loop's goal what must happen for you to feel all right? Why does a new problem go back to the start?","tree":{"t":"Randy fails a lab practical. What does he do?","o":[["He runs the loop: the problem, a goal he can reach, three solutions, his pick, and back to the start if it fails.",["ER12"],4,"Problem solving is a loop with two returns. Whatever caused the fail, the resit is his to plan."],["He sees the failure as one practical at one time, not a verdict on him, and the shame drops.",["ER12","IE20"],6,"A problem belongs to one person at one time. Seeing it that way removes a cause of the shame."],["He accepts the fail, and books the resit.",["ER12","DT13"],1,"He is doing the best he can. Accepting it is how the resit becomes possible."],["He gives up on the module.",["ER12","G4"],6,"Giving up makes sense: the fail stung. The cost is the module, and the credits he needs."]],"e":[["He runs the loop, and his first solution fails.",[["He goes back to the start of the loop.",["ER12"],"Problem solving is a loop: when a solution fails or a new problem comes, go back to the start."],["He picks the next solution on his list.",["ER12","G15"],"Problem solving is a loop: when a solution fails or a new problem comes, go back to the start."],["He asks someone for a solution he didn't think of.",["IE5","IE16"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."]]],["He sees it as one practical, but his lab partner says he's no good at practicals.",[["He weakens it: \"this one\".",["IE20"],"Weakening \"always\" to \"sometimes\" fits the facts and leaves room to act."],["He validates himself.",["IE24"],"Validating yourself, exactly as you would a friend, is how to recover when someone invalidates you."],["He lets the comment pass.",["DT19"],"A thought is a sensation of the brain, not a fact and not you. It can be watched as it comes and goes."]]],["He books the resit, and it clashes with another exam.",[["He tells the department and asks for another date.",["IE5","ER12"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["He makes pros and cons of each order.",["DT5"],"Pros and cons, written before the urge decides, weigh the long term above the now."],["He accepts a heavy week, and plans it.",["DT13","ER18"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]]]}},{"id":"ER13","title":"fitting emotions: act on the urge, solve, or avoid","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"When an emotion fits, the solutions come in an order. Why does acting on the urge come first, and why may it bring only short relief?","tree":{"t":"Sarah's fear of failing a placement exam fits the facts. What does she do?","o":[["She acts on the urge in a useful way: she studies the weak topics hard.",["ER13","ER12"],4,"When an emotion fits, act on its urge, solve, or avoid. Here acting on it is solving."],["She reads the situation in the kindest way the facts allow: she has six weeks and a list of topics.",["ER13","ER7"],6,"Rose-coloured glasses, within the facts. The fear fits, but how big it feels depends on the reading."],["She accepts the fear as fitting, and keeps preparing.",["ER13","ER22"],1,"She is doing the best she can. A fitting fear is information, not an enemy."],["She doesn't sit the exam.",["ER13","G4"],6,"Avoiding is one of the solutions for a fitting fear. Here it costs the placement."]],"e":[["She studies the weak topics hard, and her practice scores barely move.",[["She changes how she studies: past papers instead of notes.",["ER13","ER12"],"Acting on a fitting fear means solving; the solution can change."],["She asks for help with one topic.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She records each score, and looks at the trend.",["G15"],"One try is one trial. Only a record of several shows where a skill works, so the trial goes on."]]],["She reads it kindly, but a friend who sat it says it's brutal.",[["She checks the facts: past pass rates.",["ER7"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."],["She copes ahead for a hard paper.",["ER18"],"Mastery is built one hard-but-possible step at a time, and coping ahead means rehearsing the hard scene first."],["She lets the fear rise and fall.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["She accepts the fear, and the night before she can't sleep.",[["She aims for rest, not sleep.",["ER21"],"In bed, a solvable worry gets a two-line plan; otherwise the aim is rest, not sleep."],["She uses paired relaxation.",["DT7"],"Paired relaxation links release to one word, practised first in quiet places, then used where it is needed."],["She accepts a tired morning, and plans breakfast.",["ER19","DT13"],"The body's basics (illness treated, balanced eating, sleep, exercise, no mood-altering drugs) lower vulnerability."]]]]}},{"id":"ER14","title":"lowering vulnerability: A, B, C, and PLEASE","marks":[],"practice":["each morning :   plan  𝖺 ,  𝖻 ,  𝖼  by the hour ;   each night :  what was done ;   mood at start and end ,  0 to 100","over the weeks :   done / planned ↑   ⇒   ν ↓"],"u":"Why does each of A, B, C and PLEASE lower vulnerability on its own, and more of them lower it further?","tree":{"t":"Terry is going into exam term. What does he do?","o":[["He puts all four in place: one pleasant thing a day, one hard task a day, a cope-ahead plan, and sleep and meals.",["ER14","ER16","ER18","ER19"],3,"Each one in place lowers vulnerability, and more of them lower it further. It asks him to try harder before the stress arrives."],["He reminds himself that each one helps on its own, and the term feels less daunting.",["ER14"],2,"Each one in place lowers vulnerability by itself. He wants to improve, and knowing one is enough to start takes the dread out of four."],["He accepts that not all four will hold every week.",["ER14","DT13"],1,"He is doing the best he can. Three of four is still lower vulnerability."],["He puts none of them in place.",["ER14","G4"],6,"It makes sense when time feels short. The cost is that every stress of the term hits him harder."]],"e":[["He puts all four in place, and a family crisis knocks two of them out.",[["He keeps the two that still hold.",["ER14","G2"],"Each one lowers vulnerability on its own."],["He tells his tutor about the crisis.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["He accepts a harder week.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["He knows each one helps, but can't start any of them.",[["He picks the smallest: one meal at a regular time.",["ER19","ER18"],"The body's basics (illness treated, balanced eating, sleep, exercise, no mood-altering drugs) lower vulnerability."],["He runs a missing-links check.",["G14"],"The first question that fails (knew it, willing, remembered) shows what to fix: often just a cue."],["He asks a friend to join him for one.",["IE16","IE5"],"Friendships grow from being near, being alike, talking, and joining in, so one more try widens the chance."]]],["He accepts not all four will hold, and by week three none do.",[["He starts again with one.",["ER14","DT16"],"Each of A, B, C and PLEASE lowers vulnerability on its own, so even one still helps."],["He checks what knocked them out.",["G13"],"A chain finds the link where a skill would have broken it, and that is where the next try aims."],["He lets the week be what it was.",["M5","DT13"],"Not judging, one thing at a time, doing what works: the how skills keep the next step clear."]]]]}},{"id":"ER15","title":"vulnerability lowers the hill: two thresholds","marks":[["θ₀","the strength at which an emotion fires"],["odds","chance it happens over chance it does not"],["∝","","r06stat.html","6·stat · How Do You Pick a Fair Sample?"]],"practice":[],"u":"Vulnerability lowers both thresholds. Why does it not make the event any stronger?","tree":{"t":"Terry slept four hours before a chemistry exam. What does he do?","o":[["He eats breakfast and arrives early to give himself margin.",["ER15","ER19"],4,"Vulnerability lowers both thresholds, and he can push them back up a little. He did not choose the noisy flat, but he can manage the morning."],["He reminds himself the exam is no harder; he is only more reactive today.",["ER15"],6,"Vulnerability does not make the event stronger. Knowing where the reactivity comes from stops him reading it as the exam."],["He accepts he will feel more fragile today.",["ER15","DT13"],1,"He is doing the best he can on four hours."],["He skips the exam.",["ER15","G4"],6,"Skipping makes sense when he feels raw. The cost is the exam, and a resit on top of the tiredness."]],"e":[["He eats and arrives early, and the exam starts late.",[["He uses the wait for paced breathing.",["DT6"],"TIP changes the body to change the emotion fast: cold water, hard exercise, paced breathing, paired relaxation."],["He reviews his one-page summary.",["ER18"],"Mastery is built one hard-but-possible step at a time, and coping ahead means rehearsing the hard scene first."],["He accepts the delay.",["DT13","ER15"],"Vulnerability lowers the threshold; acceptance keeps the delay from tipping him."]]],["He reminds himself he is more reactive, and snaps at a friend anyway.",[["He apologizes briefly.",["IE10","ER11"],"FAST keeps self-respect: fair, no needless apologies, sticking to values, truthful."],["He notices the vulnerability, and takes it slower.",["ER15","M4"],"A plain description of what happened can't be argued with, and it keeps judgments out."],["He does not judge the slip.",["M5"],"Not judging, one thing at a time, doing what works: the how skills keep the next step clear."]]],["He accepts feeling fragile, and the first question is on his weakest topic.",[["He skips it and comes back.",["DT11"],"IMPROVE the moment: one thing at a time, meaning, a short break. Only this moment has to be survived."],["He breathes out longer than in.",["DT6"],"TIP changes the body to change the emotion fast: cold water, hard exercise, paced breathing, paired relaxation."],["He lets the jolt pass.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]]]}},{"id":"ER16","title":"accumulate positives now","marks":[],"practice":["∀ ☼ :   a⁺ planned ,  a⁺ done ,  mindful 0 to 5 ,  worries let go 0 to 5 ,  pleasant 0 to 100"],"u":"Why drop \"deserving\" and \"not deserving\"? Why do small pleasant events add up only when there are many, varied, over time?","tree":{"t":"Claire's revision weeks have no pleasant events at all. What does she do?","o":[["She plans one pleasant thing a day for a week: a walk, a call, a meal out.",["ER16","ER14"],3,"Small pleasant events add up when there are many, varied, over time. Planning them makes them happen."],["She drops \"I don't deserve a break\".",["ER16","M5"],7,"Deserving is judging. Without the judgment, a break is just a break."],["She takes one pleasant moment and is mindful of it, worries and all.",["ER16","M2"],1,"She is doing the best she can. One moment, fully lived, counts."],["She keeps grinding with no breaks.",["ER16","G4"],6,"Grinding makes sense: it feels responsible. The cost is vulnerability that rises each week."]],"e":[["She plans a pleasant thing each day, and spends the walk worrying about revision.",[["She brings her attention back to the walk, again.",["ER16","M2"],"A pleasant event only counts if she is in it."],["She notices the worry and lets it pass.",["DT19"],"A thought is a sensation of the brain, not a fact and not you. It can be watched as it comes and goes."],["She keeps the plan.",["DT16","G15"],"Acceptance is a turn of the mind made again at every fork; one turn does not make the next."]]],["She drops \"I don't deserve it\", and a friend says \"must be nice to have time off\".",[["She lets the comment pass.",["DT19"],"A thought is a sensation of the brain, not a fact and not you. It can be watched as it comes and goes."],["She validates herself.",["IE24","ER16"],"Validating yourself, exactly as you would a friend, is how to recover when someone invalidates you."],["She says, kindly, \"it helps me study\".",["IE10"],"FAST keeps self-respect: fair, no needless apologies, sticking to values, truthful."]]],["She takes a pleasant moment, and it's interrupted by a message about a deadline.",[["She finishes the moment, then reads the message.",["M5","DT11"],"Not judging, one thing at a time, doing what works: the how skills keep the next step clear."],["She deals with the deadline, then takes another moment.",["ER12","ER16"],"Problem solving is a loop: when a solution fails or a new problem comes, go back to the start."],["She lets the jolt be.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]]]}},{"id":"ER17","title":"accumulate positives long term: a value down to one step now","marks":[],"practice":["avoid avoiding :   r ≔ how much ☉ has avoided this ,  0 to 100 ,  in the past and now\nwhy :   hopeless ,  wilful ,  too hard ;   then cope ahead to stop avoiding","∀ ☼ :   v ,  g ,  the act done today ,  the next act"],"u":"How does a value differ from a goal? How can you tell a value is really yours?","tree":{"t":"Jean values learning for its own sake. What does she do?","o":[["She picks one goal, reading one paper outside her syllabus a week, and reads the first today.",["ER17"],2,"From a value down to one step now. She wants to improve, and the step makes the value real."],["She checks that the value is hers, not her parents', and feels steadier in it.",["ER17"],7,"A value counts only if it is really yours. Finding where it came from beats judging it."],["She accepts that grades will compete with it, and keeps a little room for it.",["ER17","M10"],1,"She is doing the best she can. A value can live beside a goal."],["She drops it for grades alone.",["ER17","G4"],6,"It makes sense under deadline pressure. The cost is the part of study she loved."]],"e":[["She reads one paper outside the syllabus, and it is too hard for her.",[["She picks an easier one for next week.",["ER17","ER18"],"A value comes down to one step that can be taken now."],["She asks a lecturer for a reading suggestion.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She accepts that it was hard, and keeps the value.",["DT13","ER17"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["She checks the value is hers, and her parents disagree.",[["She keeps it, kindly.",["IE10","ER17"],"FAST keeps self-respect: fair, no needless apologies, sticking to values, truthful."],["She validates their worry.",["IE22"],"Validating what makes sense is not agreeing with all of it, and it calms things enough to go on."],["She lets the disagreement be.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["She keeps room for it, and her grades dip.",[["She sets a time limit for outside reading.",["M10","ER12"],"A balanced life swings and comes back; planning the swing back keeps the pairs held."],["She checks the facts: is the reading the cause?",["ER7","G13"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."],["She accepts a small dip for something she values.",["DT13","ER17"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]]]}},{"id":"ER18","title":"build mastery; cope ahead by rehearsal","marks":[["ϖ","a plan"],["W̃","an imagined world"]],"practice":["∀ ☼ ,  hour by hour :   planned ( 𝖺 ,  𝖻 ,  𝖼 )   ,   done ( 𝖺 ,  𝖻 ,  𝖼 )","cope ahead :   helped ,  true or false"],"u":"Why make the hard thing harder as you succeed, and easier when it is too hard? Why rehearse in the present tense, from inside?","tree":{"t":"Evita has a mock interview for medical school next week. What does she do?","o":[["She does one hard-but-possible prep task each day: a timed answer, a mock with a friend, a reading.",["ER18","ER14"],3,"Mastery is built a step at a time, harder as she succeeds. It asks her to try harder each day."],["She rehearses in the present tense, from inside, with the nerves that are likely to come, and feels readier.",["ER18"],5,"Coping ahead is learned by rehearsing the scene, so the real room feels familiar."],["She accepts that some nerves will stay.",["ER18","ER22"],1,"She is doing the best she can. Nerves at an interview are normal, not a flaw."],["She doesn't prepare.",["ER18","G4"],6,"Not preparing makes sense: thinking about it brings the nerves on. The cost is meeting them cold on the day."]],"e":[["She does a hard task each day, and on day four she fails it.",[["She makes the next task easier.",["ER18"],"Mastery is built one hard-but-possible step at a time, and coping ahead means rehearsing the hard scene first."],["She does not judge the miss.",["M5"],"Not judging, one thing at a time, doing what works: the how skills keep the next step clear."],["She keeps going.",["DT16","G15"],"Acceptance is a turn of the mind made again at every fork; one turn does not make the next."]]],["She rehearses, and on the day they ask a question she never rehearsed.",[["She takes a breath and answers honestly.",["DT6","IE10"],"TIP changes the body to change the emotion fast: cold water, hard exercise, paced breathing, paired relaxation."],["She says what she does know.",["DT11","ER18"],"IMPROVE the moment: one thing at a time, meaning, a short break. Only this moment has to be survived."],["She lets the jolt rise and fall.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["She accepts the nerves, and they grow on the day.",[["She uses paced breathing outside the room.",["DT6"],"TIP changes the body to change the emotion fast: cold water, hard exercise, paced breathing, paired relaxation."],["She half-smiles and opens her hands.",["DT18"],"Half-smile and willing hands: the body tells the brain, and no one else need see it."],["She lets them ride along.",["ER22","DT13"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]]]}},{"id":"ER19","title":"the body: PLEASE","marks":[["🩺","a physical illness, treated"],["🍽","eating"],["🛌","sleep"],["🏃","exercise"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why does a body out of balance raise vulnerability? Why does looking after it count as building resilience?","tree":{"t":"Mike runs on energy drinks, skips meals and sleeps little during finals. What does he do?","o":[["He fixes sleep first: lights out at midnight every night.",["ER19","ER21"],3,"The more of the five hold, the less vulnerable he is. He needs to try harder at the dull basics."],["He sees the crash is caused by his body, not his ability, and the self-blame lifts.",["ER19"],6,"The crash has a cause he can name. That lifts the shame and points at the fix."],["He accepts finals will be tiring, and keeps one meal fixed each day.",["ER19"],1,"He is doing the best he can. One fixed meal is a floor."],["He keeps going as he is.",["ER19","G4"],6,"It makes sense: it has got him this far. The cost is vulnerability that rises as finals go on."]],"e":[["He fixes sleep, and his roommate keeps him up.",[["He asks the roommate for quiet after midnight.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["He uses earplugs and an eye mask.",["ER12","ER19"],"Problem solving is a loop: when a solution fails or a new problem comes, go back to the start."],["He accepts one bad night.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["He sees the crash as his body, but still feels like a failure.",[["He validates himself.",["IE24"],"Validating yourself, exactly as you would a friend, is how to recover when someone invalidates you."],["He checks the facts.",["ER7"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."],["He lets the feeling pass.",["DT19","ER22"],"A thought is a sensation of the brain, not a fact and not you. It can be watched as it comes and goes."]]],["He keeps one meal fixed, and then misses it for a lab that overruns.",[["He keeps a snack in his bag.",["ER19","ER12"],"The body's basics (illness treated, balanced eating, sleep, exercise, no mood-altering drugs) lower vulnerability."],["He doesn't judge the miss.",["M5"],"Not judging, one thing at a time, doing what works: the how skills keep the next step clear."],["He tells the lab demonstrator he needs a break next time.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."]]]]}},{"id":"ER20","title":"the nightmare protocol","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why change the nightmare before the harm happens? Why start with an easier one?","tree":{"t":"Zoe dreams every night that she is late for an exam she hasn't studied for. What does she do?","o":[["She writes the dream down, changes it before the harm (she arrives early, and knows the answers), and rehearses it.",["ER20"],5,"The protocol is learned by rehearsing every night and day."],["She gives the new dream a peaceful ending, and wakes less shaken.",["ER20"],6,"A changed ending can shift what she believes about herself, and that belief was feeding the dream."],["She accepts the dream for now, and starts with an easier one.",["ER20","DT13"],1,"She is doing the best she can. An easier dream is the right first step."],["She stays up to avoid sleeping.",["ER20","G4"],6,"Staying up makes sense: no sleep, no dream. The cost is the sleep she needs to study."]],"e":[["She rehearses the new dream, and the old one comes back the same night.",[["She keeps rehearsing every night and day.",["ER20"],"The nightmare is changed before the harm and rehearsed every night and day, starting with an easier one."],["She makes the change easier.",["ER20","ER18"],"The nightmare is changed before the harm and rehearsed every night and day, starting with an easier one."],["She lets the fear pass after waking.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["She gives it a peaceful ending, but wakes at 3 a.m. anyway.",[["She aims for rest.",["ER21"],"In bed, a solvable worry gets a two-line plan; otherwise the aim is rest, not sleep."],["She uses paired relaxation.",["DT7"],"Paired relaxation links release to one word, practised first in quiet places, then used where it is needed."],["She writes the dream down, and goes back to bed.",["ER20","ER21"],"The nightmare is changed before the harm and rehearsed every night and day, starting with an easier one."]]],["She accepts it for now, and it gets worse before exams.",[["She checks her body: sleep, food.",["ER19"],"The body's basics (illness treated, balanced eating, sleep, exercise, no mood-altering drugs) lower vulnerability."],["She tells someone.",["IE5","IE16"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She starts the protocol on an easier dream.",["ER20"],"The nightmare is changed before the harm and rehearsed every night and day, starting with an easier one."]]]]}},{"id":"ER21","title":"sleep: what to do instead of ruminating","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why keep the bed only for sleep? Why aim for rest and reverie, and not getting up for the day?","tree":{"t":"Randy lies awake before a deadline. What does he do?","o":[["The worry is solvable, so he writes a two-line plan, then lies down again.",["ER21","ER12"],4,"What to do depends on which case he is in. A solvable worry gets its plan, then the bed is for rest."],["He doesn't catastrophize about the lost sleep, and the worry about sleep fades.",["ER21","ER7"],6,"The sleeplessness is caused, and it is not a disaster. Worry about sleep was keeping him awake."],["He aims for rest, not sleep, and stays in bed.",["ER21"],1,"He is doing the best he can. Rest is worth something even without sleep."],["He gets up and works until dawn.",["ER21","G4"],6,"It makes sense: the deadline is real. The cost is that the bed stops meaning sleep, and tomorrow he is tired."]],"e":[["He writes the plan, and the worry comes back anyway.",[["He reminds himself the plan is written, and returns to rest.",["ER21","ER3"],"In bed, a solvable worry gets a two-line plan; otherwise the aim is rest, not sleep."],["He uses paired relaxation.",["DT7"],"Paired relaxation links release to one word, practised first in quiet places, then used where it is needed."],["He lets it pass.",["DT19"],"A thought is a sensation of the brain, not a fact and not you. It can be watched as it comes and goes."]]],["He doesn't catastrophize, but sleeps only three hours.",[["He plans tomorrow's essentials only.",["ER12","DT11"],"Problem solving is a loop: when a solution fails or a new problem comes, go back to the start."],["He has breakfast and a short walk.",["ER19"],"The body's basics (illness treated, balanced eating, sleep, exercise, no mood-altering drugs) lower vulnerability."],["He accepts a tired day.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["He stays in bed resting, and his mind keeps racing.",[["He does cold water on his face, then back to bed.",["DT6"],"TIP changes the body to change the emotion fast: cold water, hard exercise, paced breathing, paired relaxation."],["He gets up for twenty minutes, then tries again.",["ER21"],"In bed, a solvable worry gets a two-line plan; otherwise the aim is rest, not sleep."],["He lets the racing be.",["ER22","DT13"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]]]}},{"id":"ER22","title":"mindfulness of the current emotion: a wave","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why does a wall built to keep an emotion out keep it in? How does accepting an emotion leave the pain and drop the suffering?","tree":{"t":"Sarah's panic rises as she turns over the exam paper. What does she do?","o":[["She starts with the question she knows best, so marks go on the board while the panic settles.",["ER22","DT4"],5,"Acting in the exam hall is learned there. The panic is not the paper, and the paper is what she can change."],["She reminds herself she is not her emotion, and the panic loosens its grip.",["ER22","M1"],6,"Panic is caused, and it passes. She need not act on it."],["She lets the panic be there and rides the wave for a minute, without blocking it.",["ER22"],1,"A wall built to keep it out keeps it in. She is doing the best she can."],["She fights the panic until time runs out.",["ER22","G4"],6,"Fighting makes sense: panic feels like an attack. But suppressing it increases the suffering and eats the time."]],"e":[["She starts with the question she knows, and the panic comes back on question three.",[["She moves to another question she knows.",["DT11","ER22"],"IMPROVE the moment: one thing at a time, meaning, a short break. Only this moment has to be survived."],["She breathes out longer than in.",["DT6"],"TIP changes the body to change the emotion fast: cold water, hard exercise, paced breathing, paired relaxation."],["She notices it, and rides it again.",["ER22","DT16"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["She reminds herself she is not her emotion, and the panic grows anyway.",[["She checks: past the breakdown point?",["ER23"],"Past the breakdown point only crisis skills work; they bring you back below it, and then the rest work again."],["She does paced breathing.",["DT6"],"TIP changes the body to change the emotion fast: cold water, hard exercise, paced breathing, paired relaxation."],["She lets it peak.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["She lets the panic be, and time is running out.",[["She answers what she can, fast.",["DT11","ER12"],"IMPROVE the moment: one thing at a time, meaning, a short break. Only this moment has to be survived."],["She accepts a lower mark.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."],["She runs STOP: pen down, breath, then write.",["DT4"],"STOP: stop, step back, observe, then proceed mindfully. Stopping gives a skill time to work."]]]]}},{"id":"ER23","title":"past the breakdown point: a region where skills are not defined","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why work out your breakdown point while calm? Why check first that you really are past it?","tree":{"t":"Terry can't think at all ten minutes into a final. What does he do?","o":[["He checks he is past his breakdown point, uses TIP (paced breathing), then goes back to the paper.",["ER23","DT6"],4,"Crisis skills bring him back below the point, then the rest work."],["He checks it isn't just that the skills feel too hard, and finds he can still think a little.",["ER23"],6,"That check finds the cause, which was not as total as it felt."],["He sits with it, knowing it always comes down.",["ER23","DT2"],1,"He is doing the best he can. The wave will pass."],["He walks out.",["ER23","G4"],6,"Walking out makes sense when his mind is blank. The cost is the final."]],"e":[["He uses paced breathing, and still can't think.",[["He tries cold water in the break, if allowed.",["DT6","ER23"],"Another TIP skill, to get below the breakdown point."],["He tenses and releases muscles.",["DT7"],"Paired relaxation links release to one word, practised first in quiet places, then used where it is needed."],["He waits it out, knowing it falls.",["DT2","ER22"],"Crisis skills are working if things are not made worse, even when you feel no better."]]],["He checks, and he really is past the point.",[["He uses crisis skills only.",["ER23","DT2"],"Past the point, only crisis skills work."],["He tells the invigilator he feels unwell.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["He accepts a lost section.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["He sits with it, and it comes down with twenty minutes left.",[["He answers the questions worth most.",["ER12","DT11"],"Problem solving is a loop: when a solution fails or a new problem comes, go back to the start."],["He doesn't judge the lost time.",["M5"],"Not judging, one thing at a time, doing what works: the how skills keep the next step clear."],["He plans for next time with his tutor.",["ER18","IE5"],"Mastery is built one hard-but-possible step at a time, and coping ahead means rehearsing the hard scene first."]]]]}},{"id":"ER24","title":"troubleshooting: the first check that fails","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why fix the first check that fails, and not the one that seems biggest?","tree":{"t":"Claire tried opposite action for her fear of speaking in seminar, and it didn't work. What does she do?","o":[["She walks the checks: she did it halfway. She does it all the way next time.",["ER24","ER10"],3,"Fix the first check that fails. It asks her to try harder at one point, not everywhere."],["She sees one try as one trial, not proof that it fails, and the frustration drops.",["ER24","G15"],7,"Finding the cause beats blaming herself or the skill."],["She accepts that it takes many tries.",["ER24","DT16"],1,"She is doing the best she can. Many tries is normal."],["She stops speaking in seminar.",["ER24","G4"],6,"Stopping makes sense: the last try hurt. The cost is that the fear stays, and so does her silence."]],"e":[["She does it all the way, and still feels the fear.",[["She checks the next item: has she repeated it enough?",["ER24"],"Fix the first check that fails, then the next."],["She keeps going.",["ER10","DT16"],"Opposite action works only all the way, and repeated until the emotion changes."],["She checks whether the fear fits.",["ER7","ER8"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."]]],["She sees one try as one trial, but her tutor says she should speak more.",[["She tells the tutor she is working on it.",["IE5","IE10"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She sets a goal: one comment per seminar.",["ER18"],"Mastery is built one hard-but-possible step at a time, and coping ahead means rehearsing the hard scene first."],["She lets the pressure be.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["She accepts it takes many tries, and the seminar ends for the term.",[["She practises in another setting: a society meeting.",["G1","ER10"],"A skill acts on the situation as it is read, whichever way it came about, so it can be used again in a new setting."],["She copes ahead for next term.",["ER18"],"Mastery is built one hard-but-possible step at a time, and coping ahead means rehearsing the hard scene first."],["She records her progress so far.",["G15"],"One try is one trial. Only a record of several shows where a skill works, so the trial goes on."]]]]}},{"id":"ER25","title":"every skill, placed on the model","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Each skill acts at one node of the model in ER5. Pick two skills and name the node each one acts on.","tree":{"t":"A student is using skills the night before an exam. What does she do?","o":[["She places them on the model: cope ahead at vulnerability, check the facts at the reading, opposite action at the act.",["ER25","ER5"],3,"Each skill acts at one node. Knowing which helps her pick, and asks her to work at the right one."],["She sees that any node is a way in, and feels less stuck.",["ER25","ER5"],6,"A change at any part changes the whole emotion."],["She picks one skill and accepts the rest can wait.",["ER25"],1,"She is doing the best she can the night before."],["She uses none.",["ER25","G4"],6,"It makes sense when she is tired. Using no skill is still a choice, and its cost is the night."]],"e":[["She places the skills, and the one she picked doesn't work.",[["She picks a skill at another node.",["ER25","ER5"],"Each skill acts at one node of the model, so when one node is stuck another is a way in."],["She troubleshoots the one she used.",["ER24"],"When a skill didn't work, check in order and fix the first thing that failed."],["She accepts the night.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["She sees any node is a way in, but is too tired to try any.",[["She picks the body node: sleep.",["ER19","ER25"],"The body's basics (illness treated, balanced eating, sleep, exercise, no mood-altering drugs) lower vulnerability."],["She lets herself rest.",["ER21"],"In bed, a solvable worry gets a two-line plan; otherwise the aim is rest, not sleep."],["She does not judge it.",["M5"],"Not judging, one thing at a time, doing what works: the how skills keep the next step clear."]]],["She picks one skill, and the exam the next day is harder than expected.",[["She uses a crisis skill in the hall.",["DT6","DT4"],"TIP changes the body to change the emotion fast: cold water, hard exercise, paced breathing, paired relaxation."],["She answers what she can.",["DT11"],"IMPROVE the moment: one thing at a time, meaning, a short break. Only this moment has to be survived."],["She accepts the paper as it is.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]]]}}]},{"id":"DT","page":"dbtdt","name":"distress tolerance","footings":27,"steps":[{"id":"DT1","title":"goals: survive it, accept it, be free of the urge's demand","marks":[["χ","a crisis"],["🩹","pain"],["😖","suffering"],["⊒","at least as good as"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why does living as if life were all crisis make more crises? Why does freedom need both crisis survival and radical acceptance?","tree":{"t":"Jean treats every quiz as a crisis. What does she do?","o":[["She sorts the term: two real crises (the midterm and the lab report), and the rest ordinary work with a weekly plan.",["DT1","DT2","G15"],3,"Living as if life were all crisis makes more crises, so the fix is a plan, not more alarm. It asks her to try harder at the dull part, planning, so the panic has less to feed on."],["She drops the reading \"every quiz decides my future\" and puts \"each quiz is a few percent\" in its place.",["DT1","DT8","G1"],6,"The crisis feeling is caused, and the reading is one of its causes. Change the reading and the same quiz feels like a quiz."],["She lets next week's quiz be stressful, and gets through it without making it worse: no all-nighter, no skipped meals.",["DT1","DT2"],1,"Surviving a crisis without making it worse is a skill in its own right. She is doing the best she can this week, and that keeps the next week open."],["She keeps panicking before every quiz.",["DT1","G4"],6,"The panic makes sense: it once got her to study. Each week it costs sleep and focus, and the crises it creates are real ones."]],"e":[["She sorts the term, and a real crisis lands in the \"ordinary\" week: her laptop dies with the report on it.",[["She uses crisis skills for this one: STOP, then a plan.",["DT1","DT4"],"Survive the real crisis; the sorting still holds for the rest."],["She asks the IT desk and her tutor at once.",["IE5","ER12"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She accepts the lost work, and starts again.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["She drops the reading, and a teacher says \"every mark counts\".",[["She checks the facts: a few percent each.",["ER7","DT1"],"Living as if all were crisis makes more."],["She lets the comment pass.",["DT19"],"A thought is a sensation of the brain, not a fact and not you. It can be watched as it comes and goes."],["She keeps her plan.",["DT16"],"Acceptance is a turn of the mind made again at every fork; one turn does not make the next."]]],["She gets through the quiz without making it worse, and the next week has two.",[["She does the same again: one at a time.",["DT11","DT2"],"Surviving without making it worse is repeatable."],["She plans the week, one block each.",["ER12"],"Problem solving is a loop: when a solution fails or a new problem comes, go back to the start."],["She keeps sleep and meals fixed.",["ER19"],"The body's basics (illness treated, balanced eating, sleep, exercise, no mood-altering drugs) lower vulnerability."]]]]}},{"id":"DT2","title":"when crisis skills apply, and when they do not","marks":[],"practice":["for each crisis :   who ,  what ,  when ,  where  set it off ;   r_before ,  r_after   of distress ∈ [0, 100] ;   skills used ,  and how","effect ∈ {1, …, 5} :   1  «could not stand it one more minute»   …   3  «it helped somewhat»   …   5  «used skills ,  tolerated it ,  resisted the urge»"],"u":"When are crisis skills working, even if you feel no better? Why do they turn into avoidance when used for every pain?","tree":{"t":"Two cases: a missing essay draft the night before it is due, and a dull weekly reading. What does a student do?","o":[["She solves the reading with an ordinary plan (twenty pages a day), and saves crisis skills for the missing draft.",["DT2","G15"],4,"Crisis skills are for high pain that can't be fixed fast. The reading can be fixed, so it gets solved, whoever set too much of it."],["She names the reading as boring, not a crisis, and the dread drops to a shrug.",["DT2","M4"],6,"Describing it as it is (\"dull, forty pages\") removes the alarm the word crisis was causing."],["For the missing draft she uses crisis skills to get through the night, and does not make it worse: no panicked email at 3 a.m.",["DT2","DT4"],1,"Crisis skills are working if she has not made it worse, even if she feels no better. Tonight the best she can do is get to morning."],["She treats both as crises.",["DT2","G4"],6,"It makes sense if every task feels like a test of her worth. But crisis skills used for every pain turn into avoidance, and the reading never gets done."]],"e":[["She plans the reading, and then the missing draft takes the whole week.",[["She catches up the reading in small blocks after.",["DT2","ER12"],"Crisis skills are working if things are not made worse, even when you feel no better."],["She asks for the reading notes from a classmate.",["IE5","IE16"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She accepts one week behind.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["She names the reading dull, but it starts to feel like a crisis again by Thursday.",[["She describes it again: dull, forty pages.",["M4","DT2"],"A plain description of what happened can't be argued with, and it keeps judgments out."],["She does twenty pages and stops.",["DT11"],"IMPROVE the moment: one thing at a time, meaning, a short break. Only this moment has to be survived."],["She checks the facts: due Monday.",["ER7"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."]]],["She gets through the night of the missing draft, and the draft is weak.",[["She asks for feedback and a short extension to improve it.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She accepts it's a weak draft.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."],["She sleeps first, then decides.",["ER19","DT4"],"The body's basics (illness treated, balanced eating, sleep, exercise, no mood-altering drugs) lower vulnerability."]]]]}},{"id":"DT3","title":"targets: on the side of life, then the quality of life","marks":[["Q_life","quality of life"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why do behaviours on the side of life come before the quality of life? Why does whoever helps save a life take on helping to make it worth living?","tree":{"t":"After failing finals, a student writes: \"thinking life isn't worth it, skipping classes, arguing with my parents\". What comes first?","o":[["He tells someone today about the first item (a parent, a doctor, a counsellor, or a crisis line), and leaves the rest for later.",["DT3","IE5"],4,"Behaviours on the side of life come first. He did not cause the pain, but he can reach for help, and asking is a skill he already has."],["He reminds himself that while he is alive life can still get better, and a failed exam can be retaken.",["DT3","DT11"],2,"Death rules out getting better, in every case. He wants to improve, and finding that reason changes how tonight feels."],["He gets through tonight without making it worse, and keeps someone with him.",["DT3","DT2","DT6"],1,"Surviving the crisis is the first skill. He is doing the best he can, and tonight that means staying safe and not alone."],["He keeps the first item to himself.",["DT3","G4"],6,"Shame and exhaustion are real causes for staying quiet. But that leaves the most important item alone with him. Tell someone: it is the first item for a reason."]],"e":[["He tells someone, and they don't seem to understand how serious it is.",[["He tells someone else today: a doctor, a counsellor, or a crisis line.",["DT3","IE5"],"The first item comes first; if one person can't help, the next one can."],["He says it again, plainly: \"I've been thinking life isn't worth it\".",["IE5","IE10"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["He stays with someone tonight.",["DT2","DT3"],"Crisis skills are working if things are not made worse, even when you feel no better."]]],["He reminds himself life can get better, and the thoughts come back at night.",[["He calls a crisis line.",["DT3","IE5"],"Behaviours on the side of life come first: telling someone and staying safe."],["He uses cold water or paced breathing to get through the next hour.",["DT6"],"TIP changes the body to change the emotion fast: cold water, hard exercise, paced breathing, paired relaxation."],["He wakes someone in his home.",["DT3","IE16"],"Behaviours on the side of life come first: telling someone and staying safe."]]],["He gets through tonight, and tomorrow feels as heavy.",[["He tells someone today about the first item.",["DT3","IE5"],"Behaviours on the side of life come first."],["He gets through one hour at a time.",["DT11","DT2"],"IMPROVE the moment: one thing at a time, meaning, a short break. Only this moment has to be survived."],["He keeps someone with him.",["DT3","IE16"],"Behaviours on the side of life come first: telling someone and staying safe."]]]]}},{"id":"DT4","title":"STOP","marks":[["↤","is turned back toward"]],"practice":["the act to stop ;   which of  𝗌 , 𝗍 , 𝗈 , 𝗉  were used ;   r_before ,  r_after  of distress ;   effect 1 to 5"],"u":"Why does stopping come first? What does acting on impulse take away?","tree":{"t":"Mike gets a harsh comment in a group chat and wants to reply at once. What does he do?","o":[["He runs STOP: freezes, steps back, observes, and replies the next day from wise mind with what he actually needs from the group.",["DT4","IE5","M3"],5,"Stopping comes first, because acting on impulse leaves no time for a skill. The group chat is one more context where the skill has to be learned."],["He notices \"I am angry\" is something he has, not something he is, and the urge to fire back loosens.",["DT4","M1"],6,"The anger is caused by the comment and his reading of it. Seeing it as a passing state gives him time before he reacts."],["He puts the phone face down and lets the anger burn for ten minutes without typing.",["DT4","ER22"],1,"He is doing the best he can, and not replying is already a skill. The anger fades faster when he does not feed it."],["He replies in anger.",["DT4","G4"],6,"It makes sense: the comment hurt, and hitting back feels like standing up for himself. The cost is a fight in front of the whole group, and a repair he will have to make later."]],"e":[["He runs STOP, and the next morning the thread has fifty replies.",[["He replies once, from wise mind, to what matters.",["DT4","M3"],"STOP: stop, step back, observe, then proceed mindfully. Stopping gives a skill time to work."],["He mutes the thread for a day.",["DT24"],"Burn the bridges to the old behaviour and build new images that compete with craving."],["He lets the jolt rise and fall.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["He sees the anger as a state, and someone posts again, worse.",[["He runs STOP again.",["DT4","DT16"],"STOP: stop, step back, observe, then proceed mindfully. Stopping gives a skill time to work."],["He breathes out longer than in.",["DT6"],"TIP changes the body to change the emotion fast: cold water, hard exercise, paced breathing, paired relaxation."],["He asks the group admin to step in.",["IE14","IE5"],"Telling the self, the others and the agent apart shows who can decide and by whose rule."]]],["He lets the anger burn, and a friend messages \"aren't you going to say anything?\"",[["He tells the friend he will reply tomorrow.",["IE10","IE5"],"FAST keeps self-respect: fair, no needless apologies, sticking to values, truthful."],["He keeps to his plan.",["DT4"],"STOP: stop, step back, observe, then proceed mindfully. Stopping gives a skill time to work."],["He makes pros and cons of replying now.",["DT5"],"Pros and cons, written before the urge decides, weigh the long term above the now."]]]]}},{"id":"DT5","title":"pros and cons, written before the urge","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why write the pros and cons before the crisis, and fill all four boxes? Why weigh the long term above the now?","tree":{"t":"Zoe has the urge to quit her degree after one bad term. What does she do?","o":[["She fills all four boxes (pros and cons of quitting, pros and cons of staying), weighs the long term above the now, and sees her adviser about what can change.",["DT5","IE5"],3,"Pros and cons written before the urge decides. It asks her to try harder at the decision itself, and to fix what made the term bad."],["She sees that a bad term and a bad degree are different things, and the urge to run eases.",["DT5","IE20"],7,"\"Always\" weakened to \"this term\" fits the facts better. Finding the cause, one bad term, beats judging the whole degree."],["She holds off any decision until the end of next term, and lets the doubt ride along.",["DT5","DT11"],1,"She is doing the best she can, and waiting keeps both doors open. The urge is not an order."],["She quits tomorrow.",["DT5","G4"],6,"Relief is a real pro, and she has earned some. The cost is a decision made in emotion mind, which the long-term box would have weighed."]],"e":[["She fills in the four boxes, and the cons of staying look bigger.",[["She weighs the long term again, carefully.",["DT5"],"Pros and cons, written before the urge decides, weigh the long term above the now."],["She sees her adviser about what could change.",["IE5","ER12"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She waits a week before deciding.",["DT4","DT11"],"STOP: stop, step back, observe, then proceed mindfully. Stopping gives a skill time to work."]]],["She sees one bad term, and then a second bad week starts.",[["She checks the facts: one week.",["ER7","IE21"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."],["She does one pleasant thing.",["ER16"],"Small pleasant events add up when there are many of them, varied, over time."],["She lets the doubt ride along.",["ER22","DT13"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["She holds off the decision, and her parents push her to decide now.",[["She tells them plainly when she will decide.",["IE5","IE10"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She validates their worry.",["IE22"],"Validating what makes sense is not agreeing with all of it, and it calms things enough to go on."],["She keeps her plan.",["DT5","DT16"],"Pros and cons, written before the urge decides, weigh the long term above the now."]]]]}},{"id":"DT6","title":"TIP: change the body to change the emotion, fast","marks":[["β̂","body to emotion"],["♥","heart rate"],["🧊","cold water"],["°","","r05j.html","5j · Ratio, Proportion and Units"],["⚠","caution"]],"practice":["r_before ,  r_after   for   arousal ∈ [0, 100]   and   can stand it ∈ [0, 100] :   0  «I can't stand it»   …   100  «I can surely survive»","distress ∈ [0, 100] :   0  none   …   100  the most ever felt ;   it cannot go past 100"],"u":"How does TIP change the emotion by changing the body? Why follow it with another skill?","tree":{"t":"Randy is too overwhelmed to think in the hour before an exam. What does he do?","o":[["He does paced breathing in the hall, breathing out longer than in, then runs STOP and walks in with a plan for the first question.",["DT6","DT4"],4,"TIP changes the body fast, and another skill follows it. The overwhelm was not his choice, but the next hour is his to manage."],["He splashes cold water on his face beforehand, if nothing makes cold water unsafe for him, and feels his heart slow.",["DT6","ER19"],6,"The emotion is partly caused by the body, so changing the body changes the emotion."],["He sits with the overwhelm, breathing slowly, and goes in anyway.",["DT6","DT2"],1,"He is doing the best he can. Going in while overwhelmed is surviving the crisis without making it worse."],["He leaves before the exam.",["DT6","G4"],6,"Leaving makes sense when the body says danger. The cost is a missed exam and a body that learns the hall is dangerous."]],"e":[["He does paced breathing, and the hall is too crowded to hear himself think.",[["He steps outside for two minutes.",["ER12","DT6"],"Problem solving is a loop: when a solution fails or a new problem comes, go back to the start."],["He tenses and releases his hands.",["DT7"],"Paired relaxation links release to one word, practised first in quiet places, then used where it is needed."],["He focuses on one thing: his summary sheet.",["DT9","M5"],"Distraction (wise mind ACCEPTS) gets you through a crisis, used once, with a plan to come back."]]],["He splashes cold water, and still feels sick.",[["He follows it with STOP and a plan for question one.",["DT6","DT4"],"TIP changes the body to change the emotion fast: cold water, hard exercise, paced breathing, paired relaxation."],["He sips water and breathes.",["DT6","ER19"],"TIP changes the body to change the emotion fast: cold water, hard exercise, paced breathing, paired relaxation."],["He lets the sick feeling be.",["ER22","DT13"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["He goes in overwhelmed, and the first question is one he can't do.",[["He skips it.",["DT11"],"IMPROVE the moment: one thing at a time, meaning, a short break. Only this moment has to be survived."],["He breathes out longer than in.",["DT6"],"TIP changes the body to change the emotion fast: cold water, hard exercise, paced breathing, paired relaxation."],["He accepts losing it.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]]]}},{"id":"DT7","title":"paired relaxation: from sixteen muscles to one word","marks":[],"practice":["practise many times a day   until   out-breath  ⇒  muscles let go   on its own ;   then less often ,  but keep it up","each day :   times practised ;   relaxation  r_before ,  r_after ∈ [0, 100] ;   times used when tense ;   single ,  groups ,  or all at once"],"u":"Why pair each release with the word relax? Why move from single muscles to the whole body?","tree":{"t":"Sarah tenses before every presentation. What does she do?","o":[["She rehearses the talk three times in the room itself, so the room and the words are both familiar.",["DT7","ER18"],5,"A new behaviour is learned in the context it is needed. Familiar ground leaves the tension less to work with."],["She practises the muscle groups at home, then the single word \"relax\", and at the lectern releases on it, and the nerves drop a notch.",["DT7"],6,"Tension is a cause of the nerves, so the word that brings the release brings some calm too."],["She lets the tension be there, and presents with it.",["DT7","ER22"],1,"She is doing the best she can. Nerves and a good presentation can share a room."],["She skips the presentation.",["DT7","G4"],6,"Avoiding makes sense: the tension goes at once. But the mark is lost, and the next presentation feels worse."]],"e":[["She rehearses in the room, and on the day there are twice as many people.",[["She focuses on three friendly faces.",["DT9","M5"],"Distraction (wise mind ACCEPTS) gets you through a crisis, used once, with a plan to come back."],["She releases on \"relax\".",["DT7"],"Paired relaxation links release to one word, practised first in quiet places, then used where it is needed."],["She lets the nerves be.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["She releases on \"relax\", and it doesn't work this time.",[["She keeps practising it at home.",["DT7","G1"],"Paired relaxation links release to one word, practised first in quiet places, then used where it is needed."],["She uses paced breathing instead.",["DT6"],"TIP changes the body to change the emotion fast: cold water, hard exercise, paced breathing, paired relaxation."],["She presents tense.",["DT13","ER22"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["She presents with the tension, and loses her place.",[["She looks at her notes, and goes on.",["DT11"],"IMPROVE the moment: one thing at a time, meaning, a short break. Only this moment has to be survived."],["She says \"let me find my place\".",["IE10","M4"],"FAST keeps self-respect: fair, no needless apologies, sticking to values, truthful."],["She lets the jolt pass.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]]]}},{"id":"DT8","title":"rethinking, paired with relaxing","marks":[],"practice":["fear of it happening again ∈ [0, 5] ,  after rethinking ;   relaxation  r_before ,  r_after ∈ [0, 100]"],"u":"When you are this upset, why is there something you are telling yourself? Why say the new statement on the in-breath and relax on the out-breath?","tree":{"t":"Terry tells himself \"if I fail this test my life is over\". What does he do?","o":[["He checks what the test covers (10% of his grade) and books two sessions on his weakest topics.",["DT8","ER7","G15"],4,"Describing the event and checking the facts come first. Whatever made the test feel huge, preparing for it is his to do."],["He writes \"one test is one test\", says it on the in-breath, and relaxes on the out-breath, and the arousal comes down.",["DT8"],5,"The catastrophe causes much of the arousal, and a truer statement causes less. Paired with relaxing, it is practised away from the event, then before each test."],["He lets the thought pass without arguing with it, and studies.",["DT8","DT19"],1,"He is doing the best he can. A thought does not have to be won to be lived with."],["He believes it.",["DT8","G4"],6,"The belief makes sense: the test feels huge. The cost is arousal that climbs until it gets in the way of the test itself."]],"e":[["He books two sessions, and the first one is cancelled.",[["He rebooks, and studies alone in the slot.",["ER12"],"Problem solving is a loop: when a solution fails or a new problem comes, go back to the start."],["He asks a classmate to study with him.",["IE5","IE16"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["He accepts the change.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["He says the new statement, and the old one shouts back.",[["He says it again on the next in-breath.",["DT8","DT16"],"Rethinking, paired with relaxing, again."],["He lets the old thought pass.",["DT19"],"A thought is a sensation of the brain, not a fact and not you. It can be watched as it comes and goes."],["He checks the facts again.",["ER7"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."]]],["He lets the thought pass, and it's still there the next morning.",[["He lets it pass again.",["DT19","DT16"],"A thought is a sensation of the brain, not a fact and not you. It can be watched as it comes and goes."],["He writes the new statement on a card.",["DT8"],"Rethinking paired with relaxing: a truer statement on the in-breath, relax on the out-breath."],["He does an hour of study.",["DT9","ER18"],"Distraction (wise mind ACCEPTS) gets you through a crisis, used once, with a plan to come back."]]]]}},{"id":"DT9","title":"distract with wise mind ACCEPTS","marks":[["≷","compared with, either way"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why is distraction for a crisis and not a habit? Why must a sensation used in a crisis be followed by coming back to the cue on purpose?","tree":{"t":"Claire can't stop crying in the library before a seminar she must attend. What does she do?","o":[["She distracts with an activity (a crossword), goes to the seminar, and comes back to what upset her tonight.",["DT9","DT2"],4,"Distraction is for a crisis, and you come back to the cause later. The seminar gets done either way."],["She compares this with a worse time she got through, and the crying slows.",["DT9","ER2"],6,"A comparison changes what the emotion is telling her: she has survived worse."],["She pushes the upset away for an hour, on purpose, knowing she will return to it.",["DT9"],1,"She is doing the best she can. Pushing away is a crisis tool, not a habit, and she uses it once."],["She goes home and misses the seminar.",["DT9","G4"],6,"Going home makes sense: she needs to be somewhere safe. The cost is the seminar and its mark."]],"e":[["She distracts and goes, and in the seminar the topic brings it back.",[["She focuses on writing notes.",["DT9","M5"],"Distraction (wise mind ACCEPTS) gets you through a crisis, used once, with a plan to come back."],["She steps out briefly and splashes water.",["DT6"],"TIP changes the body to change the emotion fast: cold water, hard exercise, paced breathing, paired relaxation."],["She lets it be, and stays.",["ER22","DT13"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["She compares with a worse time, and it makes her feel worse.",[["She switches to a different ACCEPTS move: an activity.",["DT9"],"Distraction (wise mind ACCEPTS) gets you through a crisis, used once, with a plan to come back."],["She soothes with one sense.",["DT10"],"Soothing each of the five senses is a way of being gentle with yourself, then going on."],["She lets the crying come for five minutes.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["She pushes it away for an hour, and comes back to it at night, and it's huge.",[["She writes it down and makes one plan.",["ER12","ER21"],"Problem solving is a loop: when a solution fails or a new problem comes, go back to the start."],["She calls someone she trusts.",["IE16","IE5"],"Friendships grow from being near, being alike, talking, and joining in, so one more try widens the chance."],["She lets it be, and rests.",["ER22","ER21"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]]]}},{"id":"DT10","title":"self-soothe with the five senses","marks":[],"practice":["body scan :   Θ moves through the body ,  part by part ;   alone ,  or guided","r_before ,  r_after   for   can stand it ,   negative |e| ,   positive |e|"],"u":"Why balance soothing with the task? Why bring a wandering mind back without harshness?","tree":{"t":"Jean is frazzled after a long day of labs. What does she do?","o":[["She soothes each of the five senses for twenty minutes, then does an hour of her report.",["DT10","ER14"],3,"Soothe, then work. It asks her to try harder at both, rest and the report, so neither one eats the other."],["She is kind to herself, not harsh, about being tired, and the frazzle eases.",["DT10","M5"],7,"Self-soothing is being gentle with yourself. Not judging the tiredness keeps it from turning into shame."],["She does a short body scan, one part at a time, and lets the day settle.",["DT10","M1"],1,"She is doing the best she can after a long day. Settling is enough for tonight."],["She scrolls until 2 a.m.",["DT10","G4"],6,"Scrolling makes sense: it asks nothing of her. The cost is tomorrow's sleep and a report that waits."]],"e":[["She soothes her senses, and then can't get started on the report.",[["She does five minutes only.",["ER18","DT10"],"Mastery is built one hard-but-possible step at a time, and coping ahead means rehearsing the hard scene first."],["She runs STOP and decides.",["DT4"],"STOP: stop, step back, observe, then proceed mindfully. Stopping gives a skill time to work."],["She accepts a lighter evening.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["She is kind to herself, and a labmate calls her lazy for leaving early.",[["She validates herself.",["IE24","DT10"],"Self-soothing is being gentle with yourself."],["She lets the comment pass.",["DT19"],"A thought is a sensation of the brain, not a fact and not you. It can be watched as it comes and goes."],["She tells the labmate she needed rest.",["IE10"],"FAST keeps self-respect: fair, no needless apologies, sticking to values, truthful."]]],["She does a body scan, and finds every part tense.",[["She tenses and releases each part.",["DT7"],"Paired relaxation links release to one word, practised first in quiet places, then used where it is needed."],["She has a warm drink.",["DT10"],"Soothing each of the five senses is a way of being gentle with yourself, then going on."],["She lets the tension be.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]]]}},{"id":"DT11","title":"IMPROVE the moment","marks":[],"practice":["sensory awareness :   «can you feel …?»  ,  one question at a time ;   𝗈(felt  or  not felt) ;   no right or wrong answer ;   brief :  2 or 3 questions"],"u":"Why is the only pain to survive this moment's? How does each of the seven moves make the emotion less?","tree":{"t":"Evita faces three weeks of back-to-back deadlines. What does she do?","o":[["She lays out every deadline on one page, then takes one thing in the moment: today's deadline only.",["DT11","G15"],3,"The only pain to survive is this moment's. A plan for all three weeks frees her to try harder at just today."],["She finds meaning: each deadline is a step toward her degree.",["DT11","ER17"],2,"Meaning changes the moment, and she wants to improve. The work weighs the same, but it points somewhere now."],["She accepts that the three weeks will be hard and tiring, and stops fighting that they are.",["DT11","DT13"],1,"She is doing the best she can. Three hard weeks, accepted, cost less than three hard weeks resented."],["She tells herself it will never end.",["DT11","G4"],6,"It makes sense when every day looks the same. But hopeless thinking makes each deadline heavier, and it does end in three weeks."]],"e":[["She takes today's deadline only, and two land on the same day.",[["She asks for an extension on one.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She takes one at a time in the day.",["DT11"],"IMPROVE the moment: one thing at a time, meaning, a short break. Only this moment has to be survived."],["She accepts a long day.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["She finds meaning, and then a deadline feels pointless.",[["She finds a smaller meaning: done is done.",["DT11"],"IMPROVE the moment: one thing at a time, meaning, a short break. Only this moment has to be survived."],["She takes a short break.",["DT11","ER16"],"IMPROVE the moment: one thing at a time, meaning, a short break. Only this moment has to be survived."],["She lets the pointless feeling be.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["She accepts the three weeks, and gets ill in week two.",[["She tells her tutors and asks for time.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She rests.",["ER19"],"The body's basics (illness treated, balanced eating, sleep, exercise, no mood-altering drugs) lower vulnerability."],["She accepts it.",["DT13","DT12"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]]]}},{"id":"DT12","title":"reality acceptance: living a life that is not the one you want","marks":[["↻","turn the mind again"],["½🙂","half-smile"],["🤲✋","willing hands"]],"practice":[],"u":"When is acceptance the skill to use instead of problem solving?","tree":{"t":"Mike missed the cut for the programme he wanted, and appeals are closed. What does he do?","o":[["He solves what is left open: he applies to the next intake, and asks which modules would strengthen his application.",["DT12","IE5"],4,"Problem solving goes where it still works. He did not set the cut, but the next intake is his to aim for."],["He uses half-smile and willing hands when it stings, and the bitterness softens.",["DT12","DT18"],6,"The body tells the brain. Changing his face and hands changes some of what is causing the bitterness."],["He turns to acceptance for the part that can't be changed, and goes on with the term.",["DT12","DT13"],1,"Use acceptance when problem solving cannot work. He is doing the best he can, and the sting is real."],["He keeps refusing that it happened.",["DT12","G4"],6,"Refusing makes sense: accepting feels like losing twice. But it keeps him stuck, and the next intake slips by."]],"e":[["He applies to the next intake, and is turned down again.",[["He asks for feedback on the application.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["He looks for another route.",["ER12"],"Problem solving is a loop: when a solution fails or a new problem comes, go back to the start."],["He accepts this too.",["DT12","DT13"],"Living a life that is not the one he wanted."]]],["He uses half-smile, and a friend asks why he's smiling.",[["He says he's had bad news, and is coping.",["IE10"],"FAST keeps self-respect: fair, no needless apologies, sticking to values, truthful."],["He keeps the half-smile private next time.",["DT18"],"Half-smile and willing hands: the body tells the brain, and no one else need see it."],["He lets the moment pass.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["He accepts it, and each acceptance letter his friends get stings again.",[["He turns the mind again at each one.",["DT16","DT12"],"Acceptance is a turn of the mind made again at every fork; one turn does not make the next."],["He congratulates them.",["M7","IE7"],"Kindness moves outward by steps, and it can be acted before it is felt."],["He lets the sting rise and fall.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]]]}},{"id":"DT13","title":"radical acceptance: all the way","marks":[["≡","the carry-to-the-clock technique","r43a.html#0a68e6","43a · Clock Arithmetic"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why is pain plus refusing to accept it suffering? What may be accepted, and what may not?","tree":{"t":"Zoe got a bad grade back. Even with a perfect score on the next test, the last essay, and the final, she will cap out at a B+. What does she do?","o":[["She plans the next test, the last essay and the final to the mark, so the B+ she can still reach is the one she gets.",["DT13","ER12"],4,"To change something, first accept that it is. Solving starts from the cap, not from the A she wanted."],["She accepts that she needs to try harder, do better, and be more motivated, even for a less good result than she hoped for.",["DT13","G6"],3,"Radical acceptance all the way, of the fact and of the work it still asks of her."],["She stops fighting \"it should not be B+\", and the suffering drops.",["DT13","M5"],6,"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering. Dropping the refusal leaves the pain and drops the rest."],["She gives up because an A is gone.",["DT13","G4"],6,"Giving up makes sense: the goal she worked for is gone. But refusing reality costs her the B+ as well."]],"e":[["She plans to the mark, and the next test comes back a B.",[["She asks what cost her, and plans the essay.",["IE5","ER12"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She accepts a new cap, and plans to it.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."],["She lets the disappointment be.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["She accepts she needs to try harder, and a friend says \"why bother for a B+?\"",[["She keeps her reason: the work matters.",["ER17","DT13"],"A value comes down to one step that can be taken now."],["She lets the comment pass.",["DT19"],"A thought is a sensation of the brain, not a fact and not you. It can be watched as it comes and goes."],["She tells the friend why it matters to her.",["IE10"],"FAST keeps self-respect: fair, no needless apologies, sticking to values, truthful."]]],["She stops fighting it, and the fight comes back when she sees her friend's A.",[["She turns the mind again.",["DT16","DT13"],"Acceptance is a turn of the mind made again at every fork; one turn does not make the next."],["She names the envy.",["ER6"],"Naming an emotion and what it is saying lets you stand apart from it and still own it."],["She lets it rise and fall.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]]]}},{"id":"DT14","title":"what radical acceptance is not; what gets in its way","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why is accepting not approving, and not giving up on change? When would accepting turn you passive?","tree":{"t":"Randy fears that accepting a bad mark means he has given up. What does he do?","o":[["He accepts the mark, and plans the next essay with his tutor's comments beside him.",["DT14","IE24"],3,"Acceptance makes change possible. He needs to do better, and the comments say where."],["He sees that accepting is not approving, and the fear lifts.",["DT14","M5"],7,"The fear came from a confusion, not from him. Finding that cause removes the obstacle."],["He practises accepting a little at a time, each time the mark comes to mind.",["DT14","DT16"],1,"Acceptance is a skill, and he is doing the best he can with it today."],["He refuses to accept it, and stays angry.",["DT14","G4"],6,"The anger makes sense: it feels like loyalty to his own standards. It also keeps the next essay from starting."]],"e":[["He plans the next essay, and feels like he's given up anyway.",[["He checks: accepting is not giving up.",["DT14"],"Accepting is not approving and not giving up on change."],["He keeps the plan.",["DT16"],"Acceptance is a turn of the mind made again at every fork; one turn does not make the next."],["He lets the feeling be.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["He sees accepting isn't approving, and his parent says \"don't just accept it\".",[["He explains: accept the mark, change the next.",["IE5","DT14"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["He validates the parent's worry.",["IE22"],"Validating what makes sense is not agreeing with all of it, and it calms things enough to go on."],["He lets it be.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["He practises accepting, and the mark keeps coming to mind.",[["He accepts again each time.",["DT16","DT14"],"Acceptance is a turn of the mind made again at every fork; one turn does not make the next."],["He does something else for an hour.",["DT9"],"Distraction (wise mind ACCEPTS) gets you through a crisis, used once, with a plan to come back."],["He lets it come and go.",["M2"],"Attention comes back on purpose, now, without judging and without clinging, as often as it drifts."]]]]}},{"id":"DT15","title":"practising radical acceptance","marks":[],"practice":["list :   two things that matter most  ,   two smaller things   that ☉ must accept","r ≔ acceptance :   0  denial or rebellion   …   5  at peace","refine :   each item  ⊨ F ;   no reading ,  no J ;   rewrite until it is a plain fact","pick one from each list ;   practise ;   rate again","writing an item down at all   ⇒   acceptance > 0","keep the pros and cons of accepting where they can be read when it is hard"],"u":"Why can understanding a cause not excuse it? What do you do when the mind shouts No!?","tree":{"t":"Sarah must repeat a year. What does she do?","o":[["She meets her adviser and builds the repeated year: which modules to retake, which to improve, and a study plan for each.",["DT15","IE5","G15"],4,"She cannot change the decision, but she can change what the year does for her. Solving works on what is still open."],["She talks it over with a friend who repeated a year and did well, and the shame loosens.",["DT15","IE16"],6,"Much of the shame is caused by feeling alone in it. A real case of someone who came through changes that."],["She says \"it is as it is\", describes it as fact (\"I will repeat second year\"), acts as if she accepts it, and enrols.",["DT15","DT13"],1,"Accepting statements and acting as if you accept. She is doing the best she can, and enrolling is the turn made real."],["She tells no one, and hides it.",["DT15","G4"],6,"Hiding makes sense: she fears being judged. But hiding keeps the shame fresh and cuts her off from help."]],"e":[["She plans the year, and finds one module she needs isn't offered.",[["She asks her adviser for a substitute.",["IE5","ER12"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She accepts a gap.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."],["She checks the facts about requirements.",["ER7"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."]]],["She talks to a friend who repeated, and the friend says it was awful.",[["She lets that be the friend's year, not hers.",["DT15","IE17"],"Accepting statements, acting as if you accept, and noticing the body all practise acceptance."],["She asks what helped.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She lets the fear pass.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["She enrols, and on the first day she sees her old classmates in the year above.",[["She half-smiles and walks on.",["DT18","DT15"],"Half-smile and willing hands: the body tells the brain, and no one else need see it."],["She says hello.",["ER10","IE7"],"Opposite action works only all the way, and repeated until the emotion changes."],["She lets the shame rise and fall.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]]]}},{"id":"DT16","title":"turning the mind: a choice made again at every fork","marks":[],"practice":["acceptance  r_before ,  r_after ∈ [0, 5] ;   what was hard to accept ;   how the commitment was made ;   the plan to catch the drift"],"u":"Why is choosing acceptance a turn and not an arrival? Why does one turn not make the next?","tree":{"t":"Terry accepted his low mark on Monday, but is fighting it again by Wednesday. What does he do?","o":[["He turns the mind again at this fork, and checks in each night, so the next fork finds him ready.",["DT16"],3,"One choice does not make the next. He needs to keep at it, because more painful events take more turns."],["He sees that turning again is normal, not failure, and the frustration drops.",["DT16","M5"],7,"The relapse has a cause, the pain of the mark. Seeing that beats calling himself weak."],["He makes a turn of a few degrees today, and leaves the rest for tomorrow.",["DT16","DT11"],1,"He is doing the best he can. A small turn is still a turn."],["He stops turning.",["DT16","G4"],6,"Stopping makes sense: turning again feels like going backwards. The cost is that the fight with the mark starts up again."]],"e":[["He turns the mind, and by Friday he's fighting it again.",[["He turns again.",["DT16"],"Acceptance is a turn of the mind made again at every fork; one turn does not make the next."],["He writes down why he chose to accept.",["DT16","DT5"],"Acceptance is a turn of the mind made again at every fork; one turn does not make the next."],["He lets the fight be there.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["He sees turning again as normal, and still feels weak.",[["He validates himself.",["IE24"],"Validating yourself, exactly as you would a friend, is how to recover when someone invalidates you."],["He checks the facts: painful events take more turns.",["DT16","ER7"],"Acceptance is a turn of the mind made again at every fork; one turn does not make the next."],["He lets it pass.",["DT19"],"A thought is a sensation of the brain, not a fact and not you. It can be watched as it comes and goes."]]],["He turns a few degrees, and a new low mark arrives.",[["He turns again, for both.",["DT16","DT13"],"Acceptance is a turn of the mind made again at every fork; one turn does not make the next."],["He asks for help with the next essay.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["He lets it be.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]]]}},{"id":"DT17","title":"willingness against wilfulness","marks":[],"practice":["acceptance and wilfulness ,  each  r_before ,  r_after ∈ [0, 5] ;   one willing act toward G ;   one place ☉ held back from what was needed"],"u":"Why can wilfulness not be beaten by more wilfulness? What usually stands behind fixed wilfulness?","tree":{"t":"Claire refuses to start a resit because she should not have to sit it. What does she do?","o":[["She accepts the wilfulness, then turns toward willingness and books the resit.",["DT17","DT16"],3,"Wilfulness can't be beaten by more wilfulness. She needs to try harder at the turn, and booking makes it concrete."],["She finds the threat behind it, fear of failing again, and the wilfulness loosens.",["DT17","ER7"],6,"There is usually a threat behind fixed wilfulness. Naming the cause lets her answer the fear instead."],["She plays the hand she holds: she sits the resit as it is.",["DT17","DT13"],1,"She is doing the best she can. Willingness is taking part in what is."],["She sits on her hands.",["DT17","G4"],6,"Refusing makes sense: the resit feels unfair. But the deadline keeps coming, and the unfairness does not sit the exam for her."]],"e":[["She books the resit, and wilfulness comes back the night before.",[["She notices it, and turns to willingness again.",["DT17","DT16"],"Willingness takes part in what is; wilfulness can't be beaten by more wilfulness."],["She makes pros and cons of sitting it.",["DT5"],"Pros and cons, written before the urge decides, weigh the long term above the now."],["She half-smiles and opens her hands.",["DT18"],"Half-smile and willing hands: the body tells the brain, and no one else need see it."]]],["She names the fear, and it gets bigger.",[["She copes ahead for failing again.",["ER18"],"Mastery is built one hard-but-possible step at a time, and coping ahead means rehearsing the hard scene first."],["She checks the facts about the resit.",["ER7"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."],["She lets it rise and fall.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["She sits the resit as it is, and it's harder than the first one.",[["She does what she can, one question at a time.",["DT11"],"IMPROVE the moment: one thing at a time, meaning, a short break. Only this moment has to be survived."],["She plays the hand she has.",["DT17"],"Willingness takes part in what is; wilfulness can't be beaten by more wilfulness."],["She accepts the paper.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]]]}},{"id":"DT18","title":"half-smile and willing hands: the body tells the brain","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"How does the body tell the brain? Why need no one else see the half-smile?","tree":{"t":"Jean waits outside an exam room with clenched fists. What does she do?","o":[["She opens her hands, palms up, and uses the last minutes to go over her one-page summary.",["DT18","DT4"],5,"Willing hands outside the exam room, learned where the stress is, and the minutes go to the exam itself."],["She half-smiles where no one can see, and feels the anger soften.",["DT18"],6,"The body tells the brain. The half-smile is a message to her own brain, and no one else need see it."],["She notices that clenched hands mean she is angry at the exam, and lets the anger be there while she waits.",["DT18","M4","ER22"],1,"She is not angry for no reason, and she is doing the best she can in a hard minute."],["She stays clenched.",["DT18","G4"],6,"Staying clenched makes sense: it feels like being ready. The cost is tension she carries into the exam."]],"e":[["She opens her hands and reviews, and the invigilator calls her in early.",[["She keeps her hands open as she walks in.",["DT18"],"Half-smile and willing hands: the body tells the brain, and no one else need see it."],["She breathes out longer than in.",["DT6"],"TIP changes the body to change the emotion fast: cold water, hard exercise, paced breathing, paired relaxation."],["She accepts the change.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["She half-smiles, and the anger stays.",[["She keeps the half-smile a little longer.",["DT18"],"Half-smile and willing hands: the body tells the brain, and no one else need see it."],["She names the anger.",["ER6"],"Naming an emotion and what it is saying lets you stand apart from it and still own it."],["She lets it be.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["She lets the anger be, and it turns to dread.",[["She checks the facts about the exam.",["ER7"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."],["She uses paced breathing.",["DT6"],"TIP changes the body to change the emotion fast: cold water, hard exercise, paced breathing, paired relaxation."],["She rides the dread.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]]]}},{"id":"DT19","title":"mindfulness of current thoughts","marks":[["ξ","a change of response"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why is a thought not a fact, and not you? Why can an event be catastrophic when a thought about it is not?","tree":{"t":"Evita keeps thinking \"everyone here is smarter than me\". What does she do?","o":[["She asks her question in tutorial anyway, and joins a study group to see how others are really doing.",["DT19","IE5","IE16"],5,"The thought is tested where it comes, and the study group gives her facts in place of a guess."],["She sees the thought as a sensation of the brain, firing and passing, and it loses its grip.",["DT19","M1"],6,"A thought is caused, and it is not her. Seeing it that way changes how it feels."],["She lets it flow without checking it or changing it.",["DT19"],1,"This is not checking the facts. She is doing the best she can with a thought that comes back."],["She believes it and stops asking questions.",["DT19","G4"],6,"It makes sense: everyone else seems sure. But treating a thought as a fact costs her the answers she came for."]],"e":[["She asks her question, and someone answers it before the tutor can.",[["She notes the thought \"they're smarter\" and lets it pass.",["DT19"],"A thought is a sensation of the brain, not a fact and not you. It can be watched as it comes and goes."],["She thanks them, and asks a follow-up.",["IE7","IE5"],"GIVE keeps the relationship: gentle, interested in their side, validating, with an easy manner."],["She checks the facts: they had done the reading.",["ER7"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."]]],["She sees the thought as a sensation, and it comes back every seminar.",[["She lets it pass every time.",["DT19","DT16"],"A thought is a sensation of the brain, not a fact and not you. It can be watched as it comes and goes."],["She keeps a record of what she contributes.",["G15"],"One try is one trial. Only a record of several shows where a skill works, so the trial goes on."],["She talks to someone about it.",["IE16","IE5"],"Friendships grow from being near, being alike, talking, and joining in, so one more try widens the chance."]]],["She lets it flow, and it starts to make her stay quiet.",[["She acts opposite: one question per seminar.",["ER10"],"Opposite action works only all the way, and repeated until the emotion changes."],["She checks the facts.",["ER7"],"Checking the facts (what happened, what it means, how likely, how bad) shows whether the emotion fits and how big it should be."],["She lets it flow and speaks anyway.",["DT19","ER10"],"A thought is a sensation of the brain, not a fact and not you. It can be watched as it comes and goes."]]]]}},{"id":"DT20","title":"when the crisis is an addiction","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"When are you addicted? Why can an addiction be any repeated act, and why work on one at a time?","tree":{"t":"Mike games until 4 a.m. every night despite failing modules. What does he do?","o":[["He sees that it meets the test (he cannot stop despite effort and harm), and starts dialectical abstinence: no gaming on school nights, with a plan for a slip.",["DT20","DT21"],4,"An addiction can be any repeated act, so work on one at a time. He did not set out to get hooked, but stopping is his to do."],["He names it as an addiction without judging himself, and the shame drops.",["DT20","M5"],7,"Naming beats blaming. Without the shame, he can look at the causes."],["He accepts that stopping will take more than willpower, and tells a friend.",["DT20","DT23"],1,"He is doing the best he can. Accepting the size of the problem is how a plan begins."],["He keeps gaming.",["DT20","G4"],6,"Gaming makes sense: it is the one place he feels good at something. The cost is the modules, and the gaming takes up more of his life each week."]],"e":[["He starts abstinence, and slips on Wednesday.",[["He recommits at once.",["DT21"],"Aim for never and plan for a slip: recommit at once after a slip."],["He runs a chain on the slip.",["G13"],"A chain finds the link where a skill would have broken it, and that is where the next try aims."],["He tells his friend.",["IE5","DT23"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."]]],["He names it without judging, and his friends say \"it's just gaming\".",[["He keeps his name for it.",["DT20"],"An addiction can be any repeated act that goes on despite effort and harm; naming it is the start."],["He validates himself.",["IE24"],"Validating yourself, exactly as you would a friend, is how to recover when someone invalidates you."],["He tells them what it costs him.",["IE10","IE5"],"FAST keeps self-respect: fair, no needless apologies, sticking to values, truthful."]]],["He accepts it will take more than willpower, and his friend can't help this week.",[["He uses the library to study at night.",["DT23","ER12"],"Willpower is not enough; change what pays, with people who support the change."],["He removes the console from his room.",["DT24"],"Burn the bridges to the old behaviour and build new images that compete with craving."],["He finds another person to tell.",["IE16","IE5"],"Friendships grow from being near, being alike, talking, and joining in, so one more try widens the chance."]]]]}},{"id":"DT21","title":"dialectical abstinence: aim for never, plan for a slip","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Abstinence and harm reduction each fail one way. How does aiming for never while planning for a slip keep the gain of both?","tree":{"t":"Zoe commits to no phone during study blocks. What does she do?","o":[["She commits 100%, out loud, from tomorrow, and has a plan for a slip: phone in another room, back to work at once.",["DT21"],3,"Aim for never, and plan for a slip. Both halves ask her to keep trying."],["She tells herself one slip does not mean all the rest, and the guilt shrinks.",["DT21","IE20"],6,"Guilt is what turns a slip into a slide. A truer thought causes less of it."],["She accepts that slips will come, and lets a slip be a slip without a speech to herself.",["DT21","DT13"],1,"She is doing the best she can. A slip accepted is easier to come back from than a slip fought."],["She gives up after the first slip.",["DT21","G4"],6,"Giving up makes sense: the slip feels like proof. But the slip was one block, and giving up costs every block after it."]],"e":[["She commits, and slips in the first block.",[["She puts the phone back in the other room, at once.",["DT21"],"Aim for never and plan for a slip: recommit at once after a slip."],["She does not judge it.",["M5"],"Not judging, one thing at a time, doing what works: the how skills keep the next step clear."],["She makes the next block shorter.",["ER18"],"Mastery is built one hard-but-possible step at a time, and coping ahead means rehearsing the hard scene first."]]],["The guilt shrinks, and she slips three times in a day.",[["She troubleshoots: where is the phone?",["ER24","DT24"],"When a skill didn't work, check in order and fix the first thing that failed."],["She recommits.",["DT21","DT16"],"Aim for never and plan for a slip: recommit at once after a slip."],["She asks a friend to hold her phone.",["IE5","DT23"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."]]],["She accepts that slips will come, and one slip becomes an hour of scrolling.",[["She recommits now.",["DT21"],"Aim for never and plan for a slip: recommit at once after a slip."],["She lets the guilt pass.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."],["She runs a chain on it.",["G13"],"A chain finds the link where a skill would have broken it, and that is where the next try aims."]]]]}},{"id":"DT22","title":"clear mind: between addict mind and clean mind","marks":[["𝔞","addict mind"],["𝔠","clean mind"],["𝔨","clear mind"]],"practice":["clean mind acts ,  each to be swapped for a clear mind act :\nacts that seem beside the point but led to ⛓ before  ;   «I've learned my lesson»  ;   «I can control it»\n«I'm not addicted»  ;   stopping a medicine that helps  ;   places ,  friends ,  housemates still using\nkeeping the gear  ;   carrying spare money  ;   careless with bills  ;   skipping meetings  ;   isolating\n«I can do this alone»  ;   ignoring what feeds it  ;   «willpower is enough»  ;   «I needn't tell anyone»  ;   «I can't stand this»"],"u":"Why are both addict mind and clean mind dangerous? What does clear mind know that clean mind forgets?","tree":{"t":"Randy has stopped late-night energy drinks and is sure he never will again. What does he do?","o":[["He moves to clear mind: he plans for the night before a deadline, when the urge will come back.",["DT22","DT5"],5,"Clear mind knows it could happen. The plan is learned now, for the night it is needed."],["He notices the small acts that set up a slip (the vending machine route), and feels less at their mercy.",["DT22","DT24"],6,"Each one looks unrelated, but they cause the slip. Seeing them takes away the surprise."],["He accepts that the urge may return, and does not panic when it does.",["DT22","ER22"],1,"He is doing the best he can. An urge is not a relapse."],["He stays in clean mind.",["DT22","G4"],6,"It makes sense: feeling cured is a relief. The cost is that the urge hits him unprepared."]],"e":[["He plans for the night, and it comes sooner than he thought.",[["He uses his plan.",["DT22"],"Clear mind knows the urge can come back and plans for it."],["He rides the urge.",["DT24","ER22"],"Burn the bridges to the old behaviour and build new images that compete with craving."],["He calls a friend.",["IE16","DT23"],"Friendships grow from being near, being alike, talking, and joining in, so one more try widens the chance."]]],["He notices the small acts, and finds he has done three already.",[["He stops at the fourth.",["DT22","DT4"],"Clear mind knows the urge can come back and plans for it."],["He burns that bridge: a different route home.",["DT24"],"Burn the bridges to the old behaviour and build new images that compete with craving."],["He doesn't judge it.",["M5"],"Not judging, one thing at a time, doing what works: the how skills keep the next step clear."]]],["He accepts the urge may return, and it returns very strongly.",[["He rides the wave.",["DT24","ER22"],"Burn the bridges to the old behaviour and build new images that compete with craving."],["He uses cold water.",["DT6"],"TIP changes the body to change the emotion fast: cold water, hard exercise, paced breathing, paired relaxation."],["He makes pros and cons.",["DT5"],"Pros and cons, written before the urge decides, weigh the long term above the now."]]]]}},{"id":"DT23","title":"community reinforcement","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why is willpower not enough? Why does sampling not using work as an experiment?","tree":{"t":"Sarah wants to stop cramming all night with friends who pull all-nighters. What does she do?","o":[["She makes not cramming pay: a morning study group, and a reward each early night.",["DT23","IE27"],4,"Willpower is not enough, so change what pays. Her friends did not choose for her, but her week is hers to arrange."],["She notices how good her clear mornings feel after an early night, and the pull of the all-nighters fades.",["DT23"],6,"Sampling not cramming shows the good it brings, and noticing it changes what she wants."],["She accepts that her friends may tease her, and goes to bed anyway.",["DT23","IE13"],1,"She is doing the best she can. Being teased is a cost she can carry."],["She keeps cramming.",["DT23","G4"],6,"Cramming makes sense: it is time with friends, and it has worked before. The cost is sleep and marks that slip."]],"e":[["She sets up the morning group, and no one comes.",[["She asks one person personally.",["IE5","IE16"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She studies alone in the morning and rewards herself.",["DT23","IE25"],"Willpower is not enough; change what pays, with people who support the change."],["She accepts a slow start.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["She enjoys the mornings, and her friends stop inviting her out.",[["She invites them to something in the day.",["IE16","IE7"],"Friendships grow from being near, being alike, talking, and joining in, so one more try widens the chance."],["She lets the hurt be.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."],["She keeps one late night a week with them.",["M9","DT23"],"The middle path holds both sides whole and bends with the week without breaking."]]],["She accepts the teasing, and it gets meaner.",[["She tells them plainly to stop.",["IE10","IE5"],"FAST keeps self-respect: fair, no needless apologies, sticking to values, truthful."],["She spends less time with them.",["IE18"],"A tie that harms is decided on from wise mind, with safety first and a plan for how it ends."],["She validates herself.",["IE24"],"Validating yourself, exactly as you would a friend, is how to recover when someone invalidates you."]]]]}},{"id":"DT24","title":"burning bridges, building new ones","marks":[],"practice":["r ≔ commitment to  |A_⛓| = 0 ,  0 to 100 ;   then go to 𝔴 and rate it again","list A_⛓  →  check off what is gone ;   list what would make ⛓ impossible  →  check off what is done","the images to call up when a craving comes ;   which were used :  new images ,  moving images ,  new smells ,  𝗈(wave(u_⛓))"],"u":"Why is one bridge left standing enough for a relapse? What does each urge watched and not acted on teach?","tree":{"t":"Terry wants to stop betting on match odds during lectures. What does he do?","o":[["He burns bridges: deletes the app, cancels the card, unfollows the tipsters.",["DT24"],4,"One bridge left standing is enough for a relapse. He did not build the apps, but taking them down is his job."],["He builds new bridges: when the craving comes, he pictures the money staying put for the summer trip, and the craving fades.",["DT24"],5,"New images compete with craving, and they have to be practised in the lecture hall where it comes."],["He rides the wave of each urge, watching it rise and fall.",["DT24","DT19"],1,"Urges do not last forever. He is doing the best he can, and each urge he watches teaches him that."],["He keeps one account just in case.",["DT24","G4"],6,"Keeping it makes sense: it feels like freedom. But that account is the bridge."]],"e":[["He burns bridges, and a friend offers to place a bet for him.",[["He says no clearly.",["IE13","DT24"],"The ladder lets the ask or the no be said again, at the same height or higher, without softening it."],["He tells the friend why.",["IE10","IE5"],"FAST keeps self-respect: fair, no needless apologies, sticking to values, truthful."],["He rides the urge.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["He pictures the summer trip, and the craving is stronger than the picture.",[["He adds another image: the savings total.",["DT24"],"Burn the bridges to the old behaviour and build new images that compete with craving."],["He uses cold water.",["DT6"],"TIP changes the body to change the emotion fast: cold water, hard exercise, paced breathing, paired relaxation."],["He calls someone.",["IE16","DT23"],"Friendships grow from being near, being alike, talking, and joining in, so one more try widens the chance."]]],["He rides each wave, and they come every hour.",[["He keeps riding.",["DT24","DT16"],"Burn the bridges to the old behaviour and build new images that compete with craving."],["He changes the setting: library, not phone.",["DT23","ER12"],"Willpower is not enough; change what pays, with people who support the change."],["He talks to a doctor or a support group.",["IE5","DT23"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."]]]]}},{"id":"DT25","title":"alternate rebellion; adaptive denial; just for now","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why only ask yourself to hold out for now? How does alternate rebellion still serve your goal?","tree":{"t":"Claire wants to skip a compulsory class to rebel against a pointless rule. What does she do?","o":[["She rebels in a way that does less harm: she writes to the course committee about the rule.",["DT25","IE5"],4,"Alternate rebellion still serves her goal. She did not make the rule, but she can work to change it."],["She writes a private parody of the rule on the bus, laughs, and the urge to skip has less force.",["DT25"],6,"Alternate rebellion that does no harm still lets the rebellion out, and that changes the urge."],["She holds out just for today, honouring the urge without acting on it, and goes to class.",["DT25","DT11","M1"],1,"Just for now is smaller than forever. She is doing the best she can, and the urge is noted, not obeyed."],["She skips and fails attendance.",["DT25","G4"],6,"Skipping makes sense: the rule is pointless to her. The cost lands on her own record, not the rule's."]],"e":[["She writes to the committee, and gets no reply.",[["She follows up once.",["IE5","IE13"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She gets classmates to sign.",["IE16","IE12"],"Friendships grow from being near, being alike, talking, and joining in, so one more try widens the chance."],["She accepts the rule this term.",["DT13","DT25"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]],["She writes the parody, and a classmate posts it online.",[["She asks them to take it down.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She accepts it's out.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."],["She tells the teacher first, if needed.",["IE10","IE14"],"FAST keeps self-respect: fair, no needless apologies, sticking to values, truthful."]]],["She holds out for today, and tomorrow the urge is stronger.",[["She holds out for tomorrow too.",["DT25","DT11"],"Alternate rebellion lets the rebellion out without the harm; \"just for now\" asks for less than forever."],["She plans another harmless rebellion.",["DT25"],"Alternate rebellion lets the rebellion out without the harm; \"just for now\" asks for less than forever."],["She makes pros and cons.",["DT5"],"Pros and cons, written before the urge decides, weigh the long term above the now."]]]]}},{"id":"∎","title":"acceptance and change commute","marks":[["α̂","accept the self, leave the world as it is"],["κ̂","leave the self, change the world"],["↪","","r35c.html","35c · More Parts Than Members"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why can accepting yourself and changing the situation be done in either order? Which series stand on each side, and what is the synthesis?","tree":{"t":"Jean failed her first university essay. What does she do?","o":[["She changes first: she books a writing-centre session, then accepts the grade.",["∎","G4","G15"],4,"Acceptance and change can come in either order. Starting with change gets the next essay moving."],["She accepts first, then changes how she plans, and the panic lifts before the planning begins.",["∎","DT13"],6,"Accepting first calms what was causing the panic, and the planning goes better for it."],["She holds both at once: \"this grade is what it is, and the next one can be better\".",["∎","IE19"],1,"The synthesis keeps both sides whole. She is doing the best she can, and doing better."],["She does neither.",["∎","G4"],6,"Doing neither makes sense the week the grade comes back. The cost grows each week it stays that way."]],"e":[["She books the session, and the advice doesn't fit her essay.",[["She asks a second tutor.",["IE5","ER12"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She takes the parts that fit.",["IE24"],"Validating yourself, exactly as you would a friend, is how to recover when someone invalidates you."],["She accepts the grade, and changes first anyway.",["∎"],"Acceptance and change can come in either order, and holding both is the synthesis."]]],["She accepts first, and the planning feels heavy.",[["She plans one step.",["ER18"],"Mastery is built one hard-but-possible step at a time, and coping ahead means rehearsing the hard scene first."],["She changes first next time.",["∎","G4"],"Acceptance and change can come in either order, and holding both is the synthesis."],["She lets the heaviness be.",["ER22"],"An emotion rises and falls like a wave when it is neither blocked nor fed, and it can be ridden."]]],["She holds both, and the next essay is also weak.",[["She holds both again.",["∎","IE19"],"Acceptance and change can come in either order, and holding both is the synthesis."],["She asks for help.",["IE5"],"DEAR MAN: describe the facts, say how it is for you, ask clearly, say what is in it for them, and stay on the ask."],["She lets it be.",["DT13"],"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering; accepting all the way leaves the pain and drops the rest."]]]]}}]}],"ways":["solve the problem","feel better about the problem","tolerate the problem","stay miserable"],"axioms":["people are doing the best they can","people want to improve","they still need to do better, try harder, be more motivated","they may not have caused their problems, but they have to solve them","a new behaviour must be learned in every context it is needed","every action, thought and emotion is caused","finding and changing causes works better than judging and blaming"]};
